      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <p>ILLUSTRATED FASHIONS.</p>
        <p>and in spite of a certain irssTmTarlty 6rereit6lifr, they haa continued fast friends. 'The fresh blood on this occasion comprised a young play-wright whose first successful piece had been running for six months; an Indian Commis- sioner, who had recently received his C. 5.1.; a lady who wrote women's rights novels; and a rising poli- tician—" not exactly our way of thinking, ray dear," it was explained to Lady Clara, "but quite a presentable man, and, I am told, extremely clever." But Lady Clara had had enough of politics for the present, and manifested no inclination to extend her acquaintance in this direction. The first night at dinner she was taken in by the Indian Commission- er' and the lady novelist, not a person of attractive exterior by the way, was almost immediately oppo- site; the other celebrities being not within her view.</p>
        <p>Surely we may nory feel confident that the worst of the season (so far as the weather is concerned) is over. There is a fascination in this capricious month, whose moods vary from smiles to tears with at times disconcerting rapidity, that is undeniable. With the appear- ance of the early spring flowers comes a sympathetic desire to clothe ourselves likewise in fresh and dainty garb, and the last few weeks have :seen great activity displayed in the world of dress. Indeed, if anything, there is a tendency to be rather "previous," and in this universal anxiety to take time by the forelock people are flying to the opposite extreme, and have already placed the order for their summer outfits in the hands of their favourite modistes. It is wiser to delay a little longer, as the modes have only just made their fdibut, and in the course of a few weeks we may see some altogether ousted from favour or modified till they are hardly to be recognized. It is there- fore more satisfactory to wait until things are more settled before determining on summer frocks and hats. The Coronation, and the many social functions which will ensue, are giving a decided impetus to affairs sartorial, and the fact that Queen Alexandra has graciously signified her desire that home manufacturers should be patronized by the ladies of the Court is placing work in many willing hands, and benefitting the country and people alike. The range of fashionable colours this season is very extensive, and includes the most lovely and delicate tones, for strong shades, except</p>
        <p>The Commissioner proved a sufficiently agreeable partner, but Lady Clara enjoyed but little of his converse, for the lady opposite almost immediately tackled him across the table on the subject of the Zenana, on which she held forth with a loud and scathing ferocity which speedily attracted the atten- tion of the rest of the table. The lady of the house, fearful as to what lengths the lady pf the pen might go in her denunciation of the Oriental marriage sys- tem, and anxious that the Commissioner should get some dinner, presently intervened with an a propos little story. "Some of you know Mrs. Gushington," she said. " Once it was propbsed to me that I' should hold a Zenana mission meeting in my draw- room in Clarges Street. didn't quite jump at it, so our charming, but imperfectly informed friend, exe,aimed—• Oh, yes do, dear Mrs. Anstruther, we'll all help—and—what a nice thing it would be, wouldn't it, if we could only get a little Zen ana' in native costume you know, to hold the plate at the the door.' She thought a•Zene.na was a sort of Zulu --Men entendu." " Ben trorato anyway," said Mr. Anstruther when the laughter had subsided—" si non e two." " I assure you it is perfectly true : I was there awl heard it," said a man's voice from Mr. An- strather's end of the table, and Lady Clara almost jumped when she heard that voice, its tones seemed so startlingly familiar. "Thank you, Mr. Lemarchant," said the hostess; "it is well to have a witness when one tells a story in the presence of a sceptical Sadducee like my hus- band." And then Lady Clara caught a gleam of soft lamplight falling on a fair closely-clipt head bowed in meek response to `this gra,cions acknow- ledgement low down on her side of the table, and the perceived it was the same head on which the setting sun had glinted in the children's ward of the hospital. .</p>
        <p>Ii was well that people were too much occupied to note the rosy flush which suddenly- suffused Lady Clira's face at this discovery. When she arrived a few hours before Mrs. Anstruther had remarked on her unwonted paleness. "Our Surrey air will soon give you a colour," she said, and this prediction was by way of being speedily and abundantly kilned. Whether the blush signified satisfaction or vexation Lady Clara herself could perhaps hardly have said. Whiclaever it meant, that momentary betrayal of feeling was all she made, for a very fine reserve took possession of her for the rest of that evening. In the drawing-room Mr. Lemarchant stood near her for some Minutes conversing with others, and once or twice glanced somewhat wistfully in her direction, but she made no sign of recognition. Next day he net her on the staircase, and stood aside for her to pass. He a gain looked wistfully at her, and this time Lady Clara would have spoken to him, but for a sudden accession of shyness which for the moment rendered nugatory the self-possession with which five or six years of society experience should surely have endowed a young lady of her class and training: Mrs. Anstruther rarely introduced her guests. " Let them find each other out," she said, " there's more fun that way." Possibly so, but with shy or diffident people it was a way not unattended by awk- wardness. In the case of these particular guests it appears probable that they would have accomplished their visit without further development of their slight previous aceuaintance, but for the accidental inter- vention of their hostess. ,For Mr. Lem.archellt, in- ferring naturally that his acquaintance was ne::: de- sired, was compelled by ordinary self-respect to take refuge in a dignified indifference; while Lady Clara, now thoroughly distrustful of herself, seemed en- tirely unable to relax her frigidity without risk of running into the other extreme, and, so to speak, giving herself away: for she was by this time fully conscious of her own delight whenever he happened to ho near her, and how her heart had begun to thrill at the mere sound of his voice. -</p>
        <p>" What have you and Mr. Lemarchant been quar- relling about ?" demanded Mrs. Anstruther after several days of this. "You did not speak to each other all luncheon time, for I Watched you. I shall have to separate you like quarrelsome children, only let me hear first which, as they say in the nursery, began it."</p>
        <p>" We haven't quarrelled," said Lady Clara feeb- ly, " at least not lately—we couldn't have, for we don't even know each other; we've never been in- troduced."</p>
        <p>" Don'tknow each other, and haven't quarrelled, at leas!. not lately! Just sit up and explain yourself, please, Clara, if you can." " I can't," Clara said, "it's impossible." " It sounds so, I must admit; but you've got to dolt."</p>
        <p>And thus coerced Lady Clara at last confided in her friend, and told her all that had happened, to- gether with all (or nearly all) her own feelings in the matter. When she had finished her friend said— " Well, you have behaved atrociously—and the whole head and front of his offending is that he is not a Conservative. My dear girl, we don't do things like that nowadays. Didyou never hear that Lord Beacbnatield and Mr. Gladstone once got into the same railway carriage, and conversed amicably all the way to Town ? Politics, my husband says, are a part of ethics, but they must not be allowed to interfere with the amenities of society to that extent. You'll have to be taught better. The first thing will' be to introduce you formally, I suppose. James" —(to the servant who at this moment entered softly to replenish the fire)—" find Mr. Lemarchant and ask him—" _ _ _</p>
        <p>"For mercy's sake—don't !" exclaimed. Lady Clara. clutching at her friend's arm. "Very well then, I won't. But lam glad you have the grace to be ashamed of yourself. It was obviously you who began it,' and it is you who will have to be punished. Your punishment will be to be taken into dinner by the enemy every night for the rest of his stay—and if you don't try your best to make up for your bad conduct, I'll tell him all you've told me—and something more which I see for myself." If you do that," Lady Clara said, "I will never speak to you again so long as I live." Thus was the Lady Clara brought to her senses. She went submissively into dinner with Mr. Lerear- chant, and the rather thick film of ice which had formed over their first acquaintance being once broken they got on swimmingly together, and no doubt were mutually sorry when their very pleasant visit came to an end. . . .</p>
        <p>The efficaci-o frs. Anstrutber's vigorous treat- ment of the case may be inferred from an announce- ment which 'appeared in a journal which has the credit of being well informed on such matters, to the effect that a marriage had been arranged, and would shortly take place, between Mr. Wilfrid Le- marchant, member for south-west Dampshire, and Lady Clara Chaceley, younger daughter of the Earl of Forthampton, which marriage was celebrated in due course.</p>
        <p>I am given to understand that Lady Clara ex- plained her seeming tergiversation by the hypothesis that there is little difference, if in fact any at all, between a moderate Liberal and an advanced Con- servative, a political axiom whichhas received ample demonstration by later combinations on a more ex- tended sale. At all events, whatever differences may have existed in this particular instance were satisfactorily adjusted, and the two parties, as Mrs. Anstruther aptly remarked, coalesced on the import- ant question of Home Rule.</p>
        <p>White Beverals gang's of convicts were at work cur Tuesday 'naming on bogs about a mile from Dsrtmoo Prison, reclaiming land, James Mack, a prisoner, cu down Assistant-Warder Chapman with his—spade. Prircipal Warder Kelly scoured Mack just in time to prevent him stizin the falitn warder', pour con- vict 4 14EO weut V&gt; Chapman's aisistance. MaCh Only came limn Stafford Gaol hat week, and it was his first day on the bogs. Chain., al was in a precaiinus condition art riah. Tuis is trio fitth deaperate attack on warden silt Princetriwn within a (nix, mnthq</p>
