      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>SPRING TOILETTE.</head>
        <p>SPRING TOILETTE.</p>
        <p>when introduced by way of contrast, are lint approved of by the leaders of fashion. Th., pretty toilette illustrated is expressed in sot. -resecla green, trimraed with strappings of blurred citing silk and tiny tucks. Small tucks and pleats are much en evidence on the newest skirts, while strapping is more in vogue than ever, if that is possible. Mauve is a colour which has recently risen greatly in popular estimation, as for many years it was considered only suitable to the middle-aged and pits,yee, but the experience gained during the period of half mourning last year has opened the eyes of many to the fact that in some shades it is very becoming to many people. Green is another tone which requires care in selecting, as it is apt to be trying, and is certainly not to be worn by a sallow complexioned woman. In some shades it has a delightful freshness exceedingly pleasing to look upon. The pretty hat sketched has a bow of black -velvet at the back, the ends falling almost to the shoulders in the manner approved of by many at present. It is, however, a fashion which will not last long, as it is t6O pronounced. and has already become too generally adopted tc • survive much longer. Lace and a bunch ol cherries form the only other trimming of tilt hat, which is of fanciful fine straw. It exempli. 'flee one of the most fashionable Shapes. A pretty and novel trimming employed on tlu plainer varieties of hats, such as Panama ID/ plain straw, takes the form of a rosette made oi loops of pink ribbon arranged so as to simulat( a full-blown rose. This is made still moil</p>
