      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>PRETTY RAT.</head>
        <p>A PRETTY RAT.</p>
        <p>realistic by a yellow centre of stamens. In some instances the huge rosette is merely stitched to the front of the hat, while in others the green stalk, buds and leaves make it look still more like the real flower. A really lovely spring model hat I saw was made of fanciful straw bent into becoming curves over the face in front. It was of a pale pastel blue and the trimming consisted of a wreath of very natural looking sweet peas in pale pink And lilac tones interspersed with leaves and buds. A. little ivory satin was intermingled with the towers, while the hat was raised in front by a bandeau hidden by a bow of black velvet ribbon. It was an exceedingly dainty and be- coming hat, and well merited imitation, though such a step would probably not have found favour in the eyes of the wearer. This charming lace collar, or cape as it might be termed, is a very picturesque piece of dress, £m is hin g as it does in a loosely knotted bow mad long ends. The lace is of a handsome design, and the style is eminently fashionable. An old gown could be made to look quite up-to- date by such an addition, and with the aid 'of some piece lace, a flounce, and some insertion it could very easily be copied. The rage for everything pertaining to the ancient regime him</p>
        <p>DAIXTY LACE AnnAn-41139;NT. brought about the re-introduction of long scarveS of lace or embroidered net, which, in order to be correct, should be Worn just floating on the arms. The present day woman, however, is much too active in her habits and movements to tolerate such an encumbrance, however eleo•ant and fashionable. The scarveS are, therefore, worn round the shoulders instead, caught here and there with ornamental pins, of antique design ,preferably, while the ends are left to fall freely 'down the front of the gown. 'There is less of stiffness in the arrangement of these scarves, as the folds are more loosely and artistically draped than the ordinary fichn. COQUETTE.</p>
