      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>LEADING MEMBERS OF THE BOER PEACE CONFERENCE</head>
        <p>THE LEADING MEMBERS OF THE BOER PEACE CONFERENCE</p>
        <p>AT KLERKSDORP.</p>
        <p>Naturally for the last fortnight or more there has been no topic of conversation except the prospects of peace, and it is sincerely hoped that, by the time these lines are in print, some- thing like a satisfactory settlement may have been arrived at. None of the other abortive attempts to end the War have been taken so seriously either by the Government or the public, but in the present case every facility has been afforded to Messrs. Schalk Burger, Lucas Meyer, Reitz had Krogh to effect a meeting with Mr. Steyn and Generals Botha. De Wet, and Delarey, who represent the men with whom we are ready to treat. And when a cable announced that at last these Boer leaders had foregathered at Klerksdorp, after having chased each other about for nearly three weeks, the excitement all through the country was intense, for it is undeniable that, however determined the nation may be to see the War through to its bitter end if necessary, every- body is heartily sick of it and the misery it has brought in its train. The brave fight which the real Boers' in contrast to the official Boers, have made for their freedom, has unquestionably softened public opinion in their favour—for the Briton has never shown himself an ungenerous foe—and we are ready to concede anything in moderation if the Boer leaders meet us in a proper spirit, as they seem inclined to do. The general disappointment will be very great if the negotiations should fall through in the end. Thanks to the enterprise of Mr. C. T. Terkes and others, the overcrowded Londoner should have a little more elbow room in three or four years' time, for a uniform and comprehensive system of metropolitan railways is practically assured. As everybody knows the old District Railway is about to be electrified under Mr. Yerkes's auspices, and the American pioneer, who has already obtained Parliamentary sanction to his original schemes, is, in a fair way to securing approval of certain pro- posals by which the separate railways at first suggested as distinct undertakings will be linked together so as to form " feeders" to the modern- ised "Underground." These " feeders " will be four in number, and are known</p>
        <p>as the Brompton and Piccadilly Railway,_ the</p>
        <p>Great Novrihern and Strand Rail-</p>
        <p>way, the Charing</p>
        <p>- . Cross, Huston and Hampstead Rail-</p>
        <p>way, and the Baker-street and</p>
        <p>Waterloo Rail-</p>
        <p>way. All four will be worked</p>
        <p>by. electricity, and</p>
        <p>will, in many respects, resemble the popular</p>
        <p>"Tup-p-enny Tube," as the Central London 0</p>
        <p>_ itailwayhas come MR. C. T. FERRER, WHOSE to be familiarly GREAT ELECTRIC RAILWAY called. The trains BCIIRME WILL AFFORD MUCH will probably RELIEF. travel at not less</p>
        <p>than fifteen miles an hour, and a twenty-four hours' service is promised, so that it will be possible for a night-worker in the City to live in the suburbs and reach his home at any hour of the night. The new lines will knit together many of the isolated systems of the metropolis, linking the Great Northern Company with the West End, Euston with Charing Cross and Victoria, and these two stations and Baker Street with Waterloo. It is altogether a gigantic scheme, and Mr. Yerkes estimates that it will cost £16,000,000, although the new founder company has been registered with a capital of only five millions. Seeing that no less than fifteen Bills dealing with the construction of electric railways and tubes for London are before Parliament, it really seems that at last we are within measurable distance of a solution of the overcrowding problem. Certainly the question is now being tackled in deadly earnest, and not before it was time. The capital involved in the new schemes referred to is some- thing like fifty millions sterling. Surely that large sum should purchase a fair measure of relief even if it does not altogether overcome the difficulty of dealing with the great City's enormous traffic.</p>
        <p>Although according to some authorities William • Shakespeare himself is a problematical personage, at least as far as his literary efforts are concerned, his birthday is an established date, and will be celebrated next week at Stratford-on- Avon by Mr. P. IL Benson and his company in the</p>
        <p>THE SFIAKESPEARE FESTIVAL AT</p>
        <p>Memorial Theatre, of which every seat booked for some time for the magnificent presentation of Xing Henry VIII., in which Miss Ellen Terry has promised to appear, on the 23rd, the actual natal day of the great dramatist. These festivities will in no way interfere with the Coronation celebrations, and, as in the year of the Diamond Jubilee, will contain no special features on account of the associations of the year. It will be a festival in itself in commemora- tion of the great master. which . promises to</p>
        <p>eclipse all previous celebrations in honour of Shakespeare. The performances will com- mence on Monday with Twelfth, Night, which will be followed by Vie Merry Wires of Windsor, Romeo and Juliet, Xing Lear, Othello, and Hamlet— a task of which no London company would be capable. However, the powers of Mr. Benson and his artists are !famous throughout the whole of England, and no outside help will in his case require to be called in. Miss Ellen Terry's appear- ance in King Henry VIII., which is quite an event in the history of the Memorial Theatre, is simply a generous action on the part of the famous actress to add to the lustre of the performance, and those who saw her at the Lyceum a decade ago in the rNe of Queen Katherine will be anxious to see her repeat her great success in the play, in which Mr. F. R. Benson will play Cardinal Wolsey. The festival promises to be a most brilliant success in every department.</p>
        <p>After the disgraceful series of scandals which have come from the Sertrian Court since King Alexander became infatuated with and married Draga, lady-in-waiting to Queen Natalie, it is pleasant to read of the genuine love match of Prince Mirko of Montenegro and Natalie, or Lilly Constantinovitch, daughter of Col. Constantino- vitch, the cousin of the late King Milan of Servia,</p>
        <p>AN INTERESTING SERVIAN PRINCE MIRKO AND MDLLE. CONSTANTINOVITCH.</p>
        <p>Their betrothal has just been announced, and, unlike the union of the present reigning royal pairs promises to be a very happy one both for the bride and bridegroom and the Servian people. In view of the childlessness of Queen Draga, and the move- ment for the creation of a great Servian Kingdom under the leadership of a Montenegrin Prince, the marriage is of considerable political importance. It will, moreover, connect the Obrenovitch family for the first time with the , leading reigning houses in Europe, as the Princess will have for sisters-in-law the present ,Queen of Italy and two Russian Grand Duchesses, besides a Princess of Battenburg. Prince Mirk° is the second son of the reigning Prince of Montenegro, and very popular in his own country. He holds a captaincy in the Montenegrin infantry, and is a lieutenant in the Russian army. Re is just twenty-three-- three years older than his beautiful fiancee.</p>
        <p>Flying machines come and go like, the sunshine and the shower on an April day. Every month almost we hear of a new airship which is expected to do wonders, but which somehow or other never seems to realise the hopes of its inventor. Some day, perhaps, the great secret will be discovered and a genius will giVe us a contrivance that will soar upward and onward like the birds we so greatly desire to emulate, but the day still appears far distant. Santos Dumont has achieved partial success in this direction, but his airship in its present form is never likely to become popular</p>
        <p>NEW AIRSHIP.</p>
        <p>rr is SAID THAT THE WAR OFFICE DAS ORDERED ONE OF TILLSIC FLYING MACIIINES. With the travelling public, who prefer a safer and more substantial vehicle, even ,though it be but a mere motor car. The latest airship inventor to claim attention is Dr. Bark)* a Beckenham gentleman, who has devoted a score of years to perfecting the flying machine which he is about to introduce to an expectant public whose interest in aeronautics never flags for an instant, but rather increases if anything. There is certainly something to be said for the Barton airship, if one may 'ridge from the model which has just been on exhibition, though flying machines have an , aggravating way of upsetting the most positive •theories when it comes to practical tests with the completed article. Let us hope, however, that Dr. Barton's long years of hard study have not been wasted,and that their product will prove to be all that is claimed for it It is said that one of these airships, capable of carrying from five to seven men, has been Ordered by the War Office, and there ii no reason to doubt this, as the Continental armies have long been experimenting with flying machines of various types, and the possibility of another nation getting first possession of a practical air Ship is one which Britishers do not care to contemplate. Dr. Barton's airship is a combination of the balloon and aeroplane, and altogether the invention shows wonderful mechanical skill and originality.</p>
        <p>IA LITTLE WONDER.</p>
        <p>Charming Child-story.</p>
        <p>Little May Taylor, whose pretty photograph is repro— duced here, is the delightful child whose story has attracted so much interest in the local press of Liver- pool and Birkenhead, and whose fame has even spread to London. She is only seven years old—a bonny, blithe little maiden, the pet of all.. Who could bear to hurt so sweet a little blossom ? . .</p>
        <p>Yet this little girl has endured wirtt h.e'r-CeTt-h7r.tear- fully described as " agonies " :it is thanks to Dr Williams' pink pills for pale people that she was set free from them and became the healthy, wholesome little beauty that she is.</p>
        <p>The first symptom of her painful tlhoriler was a sort of rash which broke out in the litte one's arms</p>
        <p>ay Taylor—the child wono3cr and leer. This was followed by swellines at the jeintee and then strange blisters, very sore to the touch, which appeared all over the body. " We had a clever doctor in at once," said Mrs Taylor, her mother, who told the story to the reporter in her pretty cottage home at 49. Corniche-road, rart Sunlight, Birkenhead, "and although the doctor preecribeet medicine for her she still suffered. We had to keep her in bed all the time, of course; she suffered great agony with these blisters. She could not bear the pain of turning over in be,4, and used to scream when anybody touched her. At last, when she had been ill about three weeks, it cccured to roe to see if Dr Williams' pink Tills would do her good. I had taken the pills myself before I was married, being troubled with a weak chest, and they did me such immense good that I thought I would try them for my little girl. I rent for a box cfl the pills. and she had rot finiabed the contents when the blisters began to gradually die away, and by the time ehe got through the second box she was in perfect health scam. The pilIA were cut with a penknife and adm:nistered in doses of half a pill." "And how is she now r'</p>
        <p>"Well, I wi,h you could see her tow," replied Mra Taylor, with moth( rly pride, "but she is at ecbool. Her health could tot be bctter."</p>
        <p>A weak impoverished condition of the blood was no doubt the source of this little child's pain. Skin erup- tions and blisters nearly always arise from this cause. People do not sufficiently recognise that impoverished blood is not merely aldisease, but a danger. especially if yoa attempt to cure it by means of opening medicines, which reduce the strength further still and impoverish the blocd more and more. This defective, weak LINA sets up arsenals ; the digestion fsils, food disagrees ; the lungslare weak—to wa'k upstairefcauses breath'ess palpi- -tatien ; purgative medicine,if foolishly tiken,rsduces the strength suddenly, and the result is that a cold is caught the weakened lungs give way, and the patient spits blood. This is the first sign of consumption. Profuse sweating at night, loss of weight,and pains in the ebost follow unless the blood in built no and enriched, death is certain to ensue, Dr. Williams' rink pills show their differsnce as compared with other medicine because they stay in the system : they do not gallop Through the bowels, taking fresh waste with th,m. Hence !her giro such great strength that they enable sufferers to throw of a cold, to shake off the insidious censumption, to get rid of the dregs of influenza, to recover, thanks to natural strength and good, stronallood, from paralysis, Sc. Vitus' dance. rheumatism, sciatica, and all the ale- mentetthat afflict in secret our wives and daughters. Still more valuable are they in the slighter ailments of everyday life. The pills can be obtained from all I chemists, and are genuine when they hear the full name Dr. Williams' pink pills for pa'e people but in case of any difficulty they can always be obtained by post from Dr Williams' medicine company, Dolborn-viaduct, London, for tso acd ninepence, post free : six boxes, thirteen and nine. _</p>
        <p>Presentation to the Rev L W Thomas. The Rev L W Thomas preached his farewell ser- mon at All Saints' Mission on Sunday evening, and at the close of the eervice Mr Sudlory, the Vicar's warden of the mission. presented to him on behalf' of all connected with the mission, a beautiful marble timepiece with the in‘cription " Presented to the Rev L W Thomas, M.A., by the congregation and Sunday School of All Saints' Mission, Warring- ton, April, 1902." and an oak-framed picture which contained groups of the Sunday school teachers and choir, the young men's Bible class, and the in- terior of All saints'. A very handsome lady's companion "was also presented to Mrs Phomas.— In making the presentation, Mr Sudlow said that these gifts were a token of the respect and esteem in which Mr and Mrs Thomas were held by the people of All Saints', He would like to Fay more about Mr Thomas and his work but there was no need to do so DR they had all heard him highly praised very recently by one well qualified to judge. He would only say they 411 wished Mr and Mrs Thomas every happiness in the future, and they hoped their work wou:d be blessed in the place where they Were geiug.—ln thanking the people on behalf of his wife and himself, Mr Thomas said he felt very much touched by these tokens of kind- ness and friendship. It was a great trial to them both to leave All Saint b' just as they were making such close and warm friends. He thanked the choir. and organist, teachers, sideemen and wardens for the kind and hearty way in which they had backed him up in, everything that had been done at the mission. They bad had their struggles—it was no small thing or a place like that to be self-support- ing—but they had stuck to it, and for the last two years they had been able to do without the £lO, grant that•had heed previously macke to the mission from the sale of work Ine begged that they would give Van; Coxon the same hearty support and place Lora lull contideuce in him. Finally he wished them, oneland all; a very earnest good-bye.</p>
