        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRANIS. Pr is easy enough to say bright things; the difficult part is to think of them. "WHAT kind of a hat would you wear with this soot?" asked the flue. " A chimney pot ? " SHE: "Why does a woman take a man's name when she gets married?" He: "Why does she take everything else he has ? " TUPMAN : "I hear that you are building a new house." Snodgrass: " Yes ; I couldn't very well build an old one, you know." A cnaamiNG conversationalist is a person who reminds you of things to say and listens attentively while you say them. MATTIE : I want you to know I don't stand on trifles." Helen (glancing down) : "No, dear, I see you dont." A RECENTLY appointed railway auditor, coming to an item of expenditure for "fish-plates," quericd the same viciously. "'Wouldn't ordinary dinner. plates do just as well ? " he asked. BY PREFERENCE.—Mrs. Brown: " Yes ; she's engaged. And she once told me that she wouldn't marry the best man living!" Brown: "Well, I suppose she'd rather be happy than consistent." A YOUNG man may be said to have reaebed the years of diseretian when he takes down the pictures of actresses from his mantelpiece and puts up a portrait of his rich maiden aunt instead. THAT'S a wise saying: "'Let me make the songs of a nation, and I care not who makes its laws.'" "Yes, one can evade its laws, but one can't got away from the songs." IT is said that few ladies continue to use their piano long after marriage. This announcement is made in order to encourage young men to enter matrimony. NERVOUS Old Lady (for the seventh time): "Oh captain, is there any danger—shall I be drowned?" Exasperated Skipper: "I'm afraid not, ma'am." " a plain, blunt man, Margaret, and can frame no honeyed speeches. Will you marry me ? " "I'm a little on the plain, blunt game myself. No !"</p>
        <p>"You are not looking at my face now at all," said the sitter. "N,," observed the painter; "I'm putting in the expression now." " TIIAT missionary," the head gaoler, reported, "has gone daft." "He will still do," replied the King of Mblipka, "for a simple repast." "I DON'T want mother to marry again," said a boy one day at breakfast. "Why not ? " was asked with some surprise. "because, said he, "I've had one father, and I don't want the trouble of getting to know another." " RECOILED.—The late Adolphe d'Ennery, ths French dramatist, had a ready and pungent wit. A rival once remarked—" This d'Ennery, is a true Jew; that is why he never produces a play without interest." "Ah," replied d'Ennery, "what a good Christian you are ' MlXED.—Hicks: "He says that one rule of his life has been to keep his conscience clear." Jacobs: "Oh, surely you misunderstood." Hicks: "Why, what don think he said?" Jacobs "Probably that the one great rule of his life was to keep clear</p>
        <p>of his consuienoe." • "Buy, Bertha, it was only last month that I paid a dressmaker's bill of £l2, and hero is another this month for £10." "Well, dear Edgar, that shows, you see, that I am beginning to spend less." "MAMMA," anxiously inquired a little miss, "shall I wear my best silk dress in Heaven ? " "No, my child, dresses will not be worn there." "Well, but ma, how will they know thatsl belonged to the best society" HERE is a story of a celebrated actor-manager who said to his housemaid, whilst reproving her for negleotecl work: "What will become of you if you lose your place ? " The housemaid replied: • If the' wust comes to the waist, I shall go on the stige." "WE are terribly cramped for space in our flab." "That so ? ' " Yes ;we even have to use the family skeleton as the hatraelc." • Moyn : "I overheard some callers saying this room is' furnished in execrable taste.' Other Moth "Why, the idea I I never ate more palat- able upholstery in my life I" JAmr WINNER (after the races): "Come, hurry up, old man; aren't you ,going home!" Billy Gottleft : "No, not to-day.' Jack: "What the dickens can you do here until to-morrow!" Billy: "Wait for my horses to finish." RURAL AUNT "And what do you do when you _ _</p>
        <p>are at home?" City Nephew On a holidawy) : "Why I I attend school. rm studying for a, doctor." Rural Aunt: "What's that.? Ain't tba doctor able to do his own studying?" InEwruren.—Merchant "Have you collected •- that bill of J. Smith?" Collector: Have I collected It? I called at-t.lna house and found that seven J. Smiths lived there. Six declared they owed nothing, and the seventh kicked me out of he house 1" Merchant: "That's the one 1 Go</p>
        <p>.</p>
        <p>back and get the money 1"</p>
        <p>A Dallomsoos Pam:mom—Watts : "I wonder if dyeing the hair is really as dangerous as the doctors say ? " N. Peck: You bet it is. Au old uncle of mine tried it once, and in less than there months he was married to a widow with four small children." "How did it happen that MissNingleton refused to marry the young clergyman?". "Why, when he proposed to her, she, being a little deaf, thought be was asking her to subscribe to the organ fund. So she told him she had promised her ,money to some other mission." " WELL,” said a lawyer, as he entered his con- demned client's cell, "good, news at list." "A reprieve 1" eagerly exclaimed the prisoner. "No, sot a reprieve ; but your uncle has died and left you £5OO, and now you can meet your fate- with the satisfying feeling that the noble efforts of your lawyer on your behalf will not go unre• warded."</p>
