        <p>"BENJAMIN !" cried Mrs. Toadies to her hue- band, who was going out of the gate, "bring me two pennyworth of snuff when you came home." "Snuff, Mrs. Toadies, snuff!" he ejaculated, as he paused with his hand on the latch. "No, no, Mrs. Toodles, the times are too hard to admit of such extravagance; you must, tickle your rose with a straw." THE professor was demolishing (as he believed) Darwin and his theories—a task which he fre- quently engaged in—when he triumphantly wound up with the question: "If we are monkeys, where are our tails?" The lecturer, who had been speak- ing his full hour, was startled to hear in reply from . one of the audience: "We have sat on them ea , . long that they are worn off." . "IT is wonderful," said the newly-arrived guest ' at the mountain resort, "how time makes such havoc." "Of what were you thinkit.g, sir?" pleasantly inquired the proprietor. "I was think- ing," said the guest, "what a magnificent building this was in the circular I saw before leaving the ; city six hours ago, and how it has changed since . then." „ BRIDGET (reading laboriously): " Hev you seen , this, Pat? It sea hero that whin a man loses wan av his einses, his other singes get, more develyuped. instans, a blind mon gets more einse av hearin', .- ~ an' touch, an' " Pat: "Shure, an' it's quite thrue ; Oi've not'ced it meself. Wain a mon has ' wan leg shorter than the other, begorra, the other legs longer, isn't it, now ?" Vikhuanr.t.--City Man: "I've decided to take young Smithson into business with me as soon as he and my daughter get back from their wedding tour." is Cashier. "But I thought you had no use for him." City Man: "I didn't at first, but I've changed my mind. I told him he couldn't have my daughter till he had at least £2OO in the bank. He got ale to put it in writing, and then ' . - ' k went out and borrowed the £2OO on the strength of becoming my son•in-law. Such business ability as that mi,,te' he allowed to go to west.," 4.6.1 k. V , , er