      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <p>,</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>There are many titled people belonging to the aristocracy who are engaged in business. Some, like the Countess of Warwick, who used to have a shop in London, in connection with her needlework school in Essex, have gone into trade for purely philanthropic purposes, others have been actuated by a spirit of rivalry, but the great bulk have gone into business for the role purpose of making money. Many of the latter class are anxious to know how they will fare under the more stringent application of the rule that no one engaged in retail trade is to be presented at Court. If this is literally carried out It will exclude several people , well known in Society, though perhaps some may find ' relief under the Companies' Acts. It is not easy to see what difference it makes, but the social position of a woman, or business man, engaged in trade is consider- ably elevated after the concern has been formed tete a Company. The Countees of Warwick, and the Duchess of Suther- land, have both had many narrow escapes in the hunt- ing field. Lady Warwick's recent accident was more severe than was at first supposed, and it has been necessary to postpone the Conference on Rural Indus- tries, which wee to have been held at Warwick Castle on May let, The accident to the Duchess of Sutherland whilst hunting with the North Staffordshire hounds near Mai last Drayton, was less severe, though the Duchess was violently thrown, and bad a very narrow escape frond being trampled upon by her horse. It is singular that Mr Rhodr s never interested him- self in the subject at the emigration of women to Sorth Africa. Be looked so far ahead in scene at hia Imperial ideas, that under favourable circumstances, they are not likely to be attained during this century. Yet Mr. Rhodes neglected the surest sad the simplest method of uniting South Africa under the British Flag, that of finding openings for British born women in the country. Bad he lived, it is possible that Mr Rhodes would have taken ths matter in band, as when the country settles down after the war, there will be many openings In South Africa for teachers, nurses, dress- makers. milliners, laundry women, typists, nursery governesses, and others, with either E01:110 businees capabilities, or who are familiar with life on the farmer homestead. The best way of finding ape-clogs in South Africa, is through cot's own friends or, if assistapce is necessary through one or other Of the responsible Emigration Societiee, or Committees. The late Mr Cecil Rhodes was a confirmed bachelor, and apparently took very little interest in women.— Though be left the great bulk of his fortune for public and Imperial purposes, it does not appear that any part of it is to go to any institution or walk, specially devot- ed to the interests of women. There are same men who loek upon women and children as of altogether miner importance, but Mr Rhodes was certainly not GIVI of these, though it is true tbat he was always more or less preocaupied with his great ecl-emes. He was godfather to the Countess of Warwick's little sen, the Hon May- nard Greville, who, however, does not benefit under Mr Rhodes' will.</p>
        <p>There are many stories, both in action and in real life, of rich people playing tricks upon their heirs and relatives. One instance has recurred in Berlin, where a wealthy man died, and felt instructions that part of his will was to te wade known after his death, and the whole of it read after his funeral. The first part raid " Every member of my family who shall abstain from attending my funeral to receive 300 marks." As a reault.nnly his housekeeper attended the funeral,and, on the codicil being opened, it left the residue of his fortune to be eivided between those who, notwithstanding the loss of the 300 marks, had attended his obsequies. The consequence is that the housekeeper gets everything, unless the relatives succeed in upsetting th e will. A husband's liability in respect of his wife's debts afford, from the legal point of view, many nice points ef law. If the good. supplied are " neceesaries," ths tradesman can recover ;n any case, and who can ray what are, or are not, " necessaries." The Bigh Court however has decided that a draper cannot recover for goods supplied to the wife after he has received notice from the husband that he would pay for nothing sup- plied on credit. The case should serve as a warning to tradesmen not to supply goods in credit to married woman, where they have received notice from the hut- hand that he would not be answerable for the debts of his wife.</p>
        <p>Tucks, gathers, and pleats, which commerce at the Vat and ends at the knees, are in full force, on all the newest eking, and form a leasing change to the quite platn Models. Skirts so fashioned, still fit quite tightly about the hips however, but at the back of the waist two box pleats, or some Icose gathers are correct. At the knees, all tucks gathers or pleats finish, the skirts flowing out full and wide about the feet. All house and waking gowns are ehorter than was the case laat year, but even so, they are well on the ground at the sides and back. Ball gowns this season are 'Daly to touch the floor all round " tails " being diepensed with. The promise of short damcing gowns will cause greatir attention to be paid to evening hosiery. Very beautiful is that of silk inlet with lace, while for those who find this too neatly, there are the silk varieties, upon which trails of embroidered flowers are lightly sprinkled. With regard to evening shoes, the majority glitter with em- broideries, ti.e heels being fashioned in gold, and silver to match. But more exquisite still are those of satin, with lace inlays, and just toucked with jewels, either real or imitation. Fr walking, Cromwell shoes, with ! large buckles, are likely to be well worn. Stripes and spots will be much in evidence during tho coming season. On evening gowes, the former are often delicately expressed by flower garlands, and the spots are produced by weavings, or ore appFgeel. Black spots on white, are particularly stylist) and effective baking, provided they are not too large, when they appear too striking to be in the best of tart?.</p>
        <p>For &amp;dui frccks tuilt of cloth, serge, or alpaca, dark blue v.ill be much employed. We all krow what an excellent stand-by a good dark blue gown is, and how well it lends itself to the brightening " process. At the moment emerald green is being used ler this purpose but whito and gold, or Hack and white and gold; though less modish is perb•ps in better taste. Vivid cerise facings embroidered with black, adorn many a smart dark blue gown, but not all complexions can stand this particular shade. Considerable care reeds to be expended on headgear worn with aunt; costumes, or the whole effect will be dowdy. With the dark Islue,and ' emerald green gown, the het should be dark blue, yith just a suggestion of the green inticduced, and the Fame idea must be carried out, through all the various " brightening " schemes,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>MADGE.</head>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
        <p>'DEATH AND THE CAMERA.</p>
        <p>Of all the many purposes to which the camera is ap- plied, that of providing a picture of features of loved ones, to be preserved after they have be removed by death, is amongst the meat woeful. In this connection an interesting osperiente has been related ty a Hull woman. She was considered to ill that her death was a I mere question of time. In view of that fact her friends persuaded her to have her photograph taken. rdhe did so, hut ROOD after, eha began to try Chm.. Verde's Bile Beans, and in conscquence, she is to day as healthy as ever in her life. Her :lame is Mrs. A. Tong, and she resides in Gordon Avenue Woodcock-street, Bull. Here Is her story :—Eiser since Avenue, remember I have suffered from Liver Complaint. This became chronic, and in the end fdisordered and rendered diseased my whole system. After marriage I became, worse. With the birth of every child my condition grew more eerietv. On one occasion, although my mother was dying, I was au ill that I was not permitted to go and see her. o:ten when I bays been out I have been seized with dizzy fits, and have had to grasp the railings or anything near me to prevent me from falling, I was never free from bilious. nese' end I was so weak that I could hardly walk. I used to be sick at the very sight of food. For days at a stretch I have never brokm my fast, aad when I did it was only a little dry toast or a biscuit that I could eat. I became wasted away until I was almost liken shadow. Some time ago Iwaa an out-patient at the Royal Infirm- ary, but despite the best medical treatment I got uo better." "I was very nervous, and the least thing would upset ' me. Indeed, I could not bear my children to play near me. My long illness wrecked my nerves and so affected my mind that I was afraid at times to be lef talone with the children. I had such pain in my head, that I feared I might do them some mischief. I verily thought that I should have to be removed to the asylum. Oqe doctor saggested that I should go into the hospital to be opet- ated on, for I felt as though I was t,ecoming My sight was obscured continuously by s mist. " Did you go into the Infirmary ?" asked the repor- ter ?—" No, said Mrs. Tong ;"Iws s told that unless I was operated on I should only live three years, but I said I wouid live as long as I could. I felt, boa - ever, that I could not last long, and went and sat for a photograph with my husband, so tbat when I was gone my friends would have some remembrance of me.'</p>
        <p>" Well ! You cerainly don't now look like a woman who has been at death's door," said the reporter. "No," replied Mrs Tong : " perhaps I do not, but for this I have only Bile Beane to thank." " Bow did you come to give Bile Beane a trial ? " queried the representative. " Well 1 I ftent for a box one day just when I felt like dying. The first few doses seemed to have a wonderful effect open me, so I Cott- tioucd to take them. You may believe me or not, but a few boxes cured me of en y defeotive eight And made me so well again that I went cut and washed clothes in order to get money to buy more Bile Beans ! As I took the medicine my ailments vanithed and I got better and better until lam once again enjoYi,ig rood hesltb. I have never since been without Chas. Forde'a Bile 3eans in the house, and T never intend." This is bet one case out of hundreds wirch prove the merit of Bile Beaus. They never fail to cure all dis- orders of the liver and kidneys, dizzint es, arDeMja, indi- gestion, constipation, Wood impurities, eruptions, weak- ness, and all female ailments. Of all chemists: is 311 or 2.8 9i per box'. Beware of imitations, which are all wortbless.</p>
        <p>Stourheadethe residence of Si? Henry Hoare, at Stour- en. Dorsetshiree was destroyed by fire on Wednesday. Four men are in trouble in London charged with fleecing wealthy young foreigners at cards. The police raided a luxuriously furnished suite of rooms in the West End on the information of a young Austrian, who Lost money at banker.</p>
