      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>LA= micting r‘tzszyip.] LADY CLARA'S CANVASS,</head>
        <p>LA= micting r‘tzszyip.] LADY CLARA'S CANVASS,</p>
        <p>FREDERIC ADYE (Author of" The Queen of the Moor," 0.)</p>
        <p>PLET ll.—CHArrsa 111.</p>
        <p>The election was over. It went off without further disturbance than a few scrimmages among the less responsible portion of the Danchester populace. The voting area being large and the polling places nu- merous, the votes had all to be carried to the Shire Hall of Danchester to be counted, and the result would not be made known before noon of the follow- ing day. Lady Clara drove in to hear it, with blue rosettes at her ponies' heads, and a basket of dainties packed up by the house-keeper for the little victim of her canvassing, and his comrades in the children's ward. Her spirits had risen within the last twenty- four hours; and she was fairly sanguine again, if not quite so sure of success as she had been at the commencement of her canvass. Surely Dampshire could not be so ungrateful for her father residence in the county, as to refuse tO return his ownnephew? A fresh bright morning too may have had something to do with her renewed confidence. It is difficult to be hopeful in muggy weather, such as is so pre- valent in Dampshire. To-day the weather was su- perb. The roads also were in good order, and the spirited ponies full of go ; so Lady Clara enjoyed her drive, and her face was so bright and rosy with pleasant anticipation that she was wondrous fair to Bee.</p>
        <p>She drove first to the hospital, not without a latent hope that she might meet the kind-hearted stranger there again. Shq had rather enjoyed her little pass- age of arms with him, though she had been somewhat worsted in it. She thirsted for revenge; and lying awake the early :part of the preceding night, when the brain is nnic4vet, had bethought herself of sever- ,o6 arguments which she was quite sure he would fink it difficult to confute ; though that he was clever she must admit ; and somehow she felt a little pride and satisfaction in his cleverness; yet this she could not have explained. However, the stranger was not at the hospital, as, when she came to think of it, how could he have been? Would he not be fully occupied in supporting his friend during the trying ordeal of suspense? How could she have expected it ?—Yet she felt a little chill of disappointment notwithstanding. Lady Clara spent nearly an hour in the children's ward, petting and amusing the inmates, and from the hospital drove to the market-square. The clock over the Shire Hall was now close upon twelve, and the square was already packed with people awaiting the result of the poll, which was to be declared at noon. Mindful of her former experience Lady Clara drew up on the edge of the crowd, with her ponies' heads towards a by street, through which she could quickly gain the open country on the first sign of hostility. She had not long to wait. The echo of the last stroke of twelve had hardly died away when a movement was made by the crowd towards the steps of the hall. A lull of a few seconds, during which Lady Clara's heart went pit-a-pat, and then the out- bursting of a loud roar, amid which could be dia. tinguished loud shouts of " Lemarchant." It Was all over then, and her side was beaten. Lady Clara bit her lip hard with vexation and gathered up her raps. Before, however, the groom had time to spring away from the ponies' heads there was another rush of the crowd to the further side of the square, and following it with her eyes Lady Clara beheld a tall man with tread shoulders and fair uncovered head standing on the topmost step of the stairs, waiting calmly till the wild cheers and whoopings should have ceased to address the people.. It was her stranger friend. " Who is that ?" she asked of a man standing by. "Him, Miss ?--Why, Muster Lee-Marchant tobe sure. Hooray fur Lee-Marchant !" How inexpressibly stupid of her not to have guessed who he was when she talked with him about the Election! As though stayed by an irresistible attraction, she waited to hear him speak, which he did as soon as the crowd was still. Only a few short words clearly and steadily delivered, without visible elation, thanking them for having returned him, a assuring them of his full intention to represent their best and highest interests in the Parliament to which they were sending him; reserving at the same time the right to use his own judgment in so doing, and judgment which, he begged leave to remind them, he had endeavoured carefully to form and train with a view of fitting himself for the responsible position to which their suffrages had that day called him. A fine manly little speech, as Lady Clara was forced to admit, free from conventional clap-trap, exultation over a vanquished opponent, ad captandum argument, or truckling to the mob, Without wait- ing to hear Temple Goring, she drove away, not angry, yet with. a strange depression at heart, in striking contrast to her morning's mood, for which the defeat of her cousin did not wholly account. What a pity such a man should be a Liberal! (she would not- call 'him Radical now) and her cheeks burned hot all the way home as she reproached her- self for the oontumely with which she had loaded the unknown, but now victorious, candidate. There was one consolation, she would never see him again • yet somehow even this reflection failed to bring all the comfort it should have brought.</p>
        <p>Cusp= IV. For some days after the election Lady Clara was depressed and dull. The re-action from so much excitement and arduous endeavour was inevitable; but she was at the same time conscious of a mental irritation not entirely attributable to her late ex- penditure of vital energy in the Conservative cause. She still smarted, not merely with the sense of party defeat, but from the castigation administered to her- self by the masterful stranger with whom she had con- tended during her canvass. She was angry with, yet, woman-like, attracted by, tbeman who had beaten her; and more angry with herself for taking the least interest in one who, if only for his politics, was unworthy of her regard. It was a dull time of year too. The old earl, her father, was afflicted with a sharp attack of gout, so there could be no shooting- parties for the present. Lady Clara was not allowed to hunt without escort, and bicycles for ladies were not yet, so she could not take it out of herself in either of these ways. B emeinbering the taunt of the irate washer-woman of Danehester, she made friends with the curate and got him to show her (so far as he was able) how to benefit without demoralising the poor of that dirty town. It was rather nasty work, but Aio stuck to it womanfully, finding her greatest relief in visiting the little victim of her canvass, who was progressing favourably in the hospital. Baby bones soon unite, and in a very few weeks she was able to ccuvey him to his own home; while thereafter both he and the children's ward of the Danchester Infirmary did not fail to derive consider- able benefit from his involuntary immolation beneath Juggernaut's car as represented by Lady Caara's carriage.</p>
        <p>" Clara must have a change," the Countess re- marked to her lord, after. observing with maternal solicitude her daughter's unusual depression of spirits. "She is not looking at all well. Her lord grunted, but whether in dissent or ap- proval was not evident. Under pressure, however, he admitted that the girl was not "within a stone of her proper form. What's the matter?" he demanded. " Liver or love ?' '</p>
        <p>"Disappointment, I imagine, over the election : she worked very hard, and has overdone herself."</p>
        <p>" Confound those Radicals, said his lordship, "there is no end to the mischief they do. I don't know how the dickens I shall do without her; but pack her off if you think it will do her any good." There was merit in this assent, ungracious as it seemed; for in those agonizing attacks of gout to which Lord Forthampton was subject (a tendency in- herited rather from a port-imbibing line of ancestry than due to any indiscretion of his own) his younger daughter was the only ministrant he could at times bear near him. Hers it was to read the papers to him, write his letters (paraphrasing as she went on the terse strength of the original dictation into such re- fined and courteous expressions as one might expect in the correspondence of a peer of the Realm) and hear the brunt of the storm when anything went wrong upon the estate. • Rent-day was just at hand, and being coincident with the gout a hard time awaited Lady Clara, but for this happy thought of her mother's that she required change of air. It was a complete change for her from her Mid. land home, where dense woods and.large sheets el stagnant water in the park rendered the atmosphere in late autumn heavy andhurnid, to bright and breezy Surrey; and the house-party which she joined in • that pleasant county was a gay and lively one. Mrs. Anstruther, whose invitation had come in se Opportunely, was one of those quite modern matrons who fulfil a useful function in society by bringing people together who might otherwise have corn. passed their course through life without ever coming within speaking distance of each other. Without being exactly a Mrs. Lyon Hunter it was her de- light to get hold of anyone who gave promise of tal- ent and ability and introduce them to her own par- ticular friends. Parties were stupid, she averred, where everybody know everybody, and she liked to get fresh blood and watch how her new guests hit it elf with the old. Lady Clara was one of the old: sibiaallakarunct cif Mrs. UstrAtherlslaideEmiids.</p>
      </div>
