      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>LICENSED VICTUALLERS' ASSOCIATION. Coronation Banquet at the Lion Hotel,</head>
        <p>LICENSED VICTUALLERS' ASSOCIATION. Coronation Banquet at the Lion Hotel,</p>
        <p>A company of over one hundred in [lumbar sat down at a grand coronation banquet wnicn was held at the Lion Hotel, Warrinptou, on Thursday last week,in aid of:the benevolent fund of the War- rington and District Licensed \retaliation Associa- tiOn. Sir Gilbert Greenall, . Bart presided, and he was supported by Mr Robert Pierpoint. M.P., Colonel W 11 SI siker, M.P., Councilior J Evan.. (president of the Warrington and District Licensed ushers' Association), Mr J Reid Walker, Mr Henry Greenish, Mr it I Wynne Jones, Mr W B Roberts, Mr James Mouro, Alderman Francomb, Alderman Bolton, Councillor Isaac Turner (Liver- pool), Mr A Browne, Mr J Fulton Smith, Mr Rep- herd, Mr Howson, Mr J Clarke, Councillor S.hilleock ;vice president of the Association), Councillor Rogers, Councillor Baird, Councillor Malkin, sir P H Lake (bon treasurer), Mr T Walsh (s9cretary), Mr J Allison and Mr Perdue (trustees), Councillor Sampson, Messrs Thomas Massey, Si! F Turner, Peers Brimelow and r Partridge (com- mittee). Excellent arrangements had been made for the banquet by the fohowing special committee consisting of the President,Vice-President, the hon. treasurer, secretary, Messrs IL Shacklady, it Ander- ton. P J Whittle, and J Allison, The function was neld in the ball room of the Lion Hotel and the tables were artistically set out. Covers were removed shortly before six o'clock. An excellent rapist was served in Mrs Gilbert's best style, and a long toast list was 'afterwards sub- mitted, Mr R Shacklady acted as toastmaster.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>Tit&amp; LOYAL TOASTS.</head>
        <p>Tit&amp; LOYAL TOASTS.</p>
        <p>The toast of "The King" WeiB proposed by gir GILBERT GREENALL. it was over, sixty years he said, since there had been a coronation in this country. They had reason to be thankful. tbs.. the past reign bad produced such a time of prospeiity and development. However much they regretted the loss of their late beloved Quest' Victoria trley looked with confidence to a time of equal prosperity and he hoped shortly of peace under the wise rule of King Edward VII. kApplause.) The toast was drunk with musical honours, the Orpheus Quartette Party singing"" God save the King." "Queen Alexandra, Prince and Princess of Wales and Hest of the Hoy al Family " was next proposed by the CHAIRMAN. This toast was also drunk with musical honours, "God bless the Prince of Wales" being sung, Mr Whitehouse singing the solo part.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>APOLOGIE3 FOR, NON-ATTENDANCE</head>
        <p>APOLOGIE3 FOR, NON-ATTENDANCE</p>
        <p>Sir Gilbert Greensll announced that he had re- ceived apologies for non-attendance from Sir Peter Walker, who stated that urgent private business would prevent him from attending, from Mr Arthur Croatleld who telegr4hecl that he was most dis- appointed and mortified that a sprained ankle put travelling out of the question. Writing from the Hotel Beau-Site, Cannes, to Sir Gilbert Greenell, Mr A H Crosfield further said he particularly wanted to come because it was the second time the licensed victuallers had boon kind enough to ask him and because he (Sir Gilbert) was in the ehair. Letters of apology for non-attendance had also been received from Mr J C Parr (mayor), Mr Alderman Rudman (mayor of Salford), Mr John White, Mr Travers Pickmere, Colonel J C Ridgway, Alderman Siddall (Ashton-under-Lyne), Mr Joseph Robinson (St Helens), Mr T J Down, Mr John T Whitley and Mr William Owen.</p>
        <p>ARMY AND NAVY AND AUXILIARY FORCES.</p>
        <p>Air A BROWNE proposed this toast. He admitted that our army was a small one when compare i with foreign armies, but with regard to the navy that was second to none, In referring to the Vol- unteer forces, he paid a tribute to their services during the South African war. Not only had the Volunteers of England responded nobly to the call, but Volunteers had come to our aisistanrle from Canada, New Zealand, Australia and other colonies It was a matter for great thankfulness that we had such loyal citizans in all parts of the globe. Whilst we were able to speak with pride of the British Empire, We must not forget that all the great em- pires of the past had bad their rise and fail and it behoved us to take lessons from the past and avoid the causes which led to their downfall. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr It I WYriNE JONES. in responding, said he regretted that Sir Peter Walker was unable to be present to yespond :to the toast. In a book on Egyptology, he found that some 4,000 years ago there lived a personage whose duty it was to write the obituary notices of all the great men in Egypt. The writer generally finished up his obituary uotiee with ' the words "And their cakes shall be swee;, and their ales shall be strong." (Laughter,) That proved that the Egyptians were in the habit of drinking ales. (Hear, hear.) Then Shakespeare, talked of cakes Min ale, and added that "gingtr shall be hot in the mouth." Re (Mr Jones) might say that ginger would be very hot in the mouth of any Government that. tried to tamper with the liberty of the British workingman and the British public generally from drinking what they pleased. (Hear, hear.) He recently heard a speaker recite the following lines t--</p>
        <p>" Cold water is the beet of drinks. • Teetotal poets slog. But who am I that I shou'd take The beet of everything. Let Princes revel at the pump. Let Peers enjoy their tea. But spirits. wines, or even beers Are good enough for me "ii) THE "HOUSES OF PARLIAMENT."</p>
        <p>SPRECHES BY MR. PIERPOINV, M.P., AND COL. WALKER, M.P. -</p>
        <p>Mr W B ROBERTS, in proposing the toast of the "Houses of Parliament," said that when Parliament increased taxation, the licensed viciaaller always did his duty in supporting the action of the Gover- nment. Whilst supporting the Government in all national requirements they could not but look to the members of Parliament to protect the licensed victuallers' trade from the numerous enemies who were ever ready to imperil its very existence. Referring to the new licensing bill he said that though tome clauses were of a stringent character its aim was towards the eleva- tion of the trade by the suppression of the greatest of all evils—drinking clubs—and the extermination of that social pest, the habitual drunkard. (Hear, hear) The education bill was a welcome measure and one that should be received with the greatest spirit of liberality possible. Its name spelled success or failure in the competitive race of life. He hoped the schools would be supported by im- perial taxation and administered by one central authority. Its object was of a national character and must not in any way be restricted by local pre- judices. He was sure that the trade in the future, as in the past would loyally accept the legislation of Parliament so long as it was governed with equity and justice. (Applause.) Mr ROBERT PIERPOINT, M.P., in resnonding to the toast, said be did not intend to refer to the Government's licensing bill mdrii than to say that he had a general approval of it. There might be details which would have to be dealt with by the Grand Committee before it became en Act.. In speaking of the House of Lords, Mr Pierpoint saidi he had often been to the House of Lords listening to the debates and his opinion was that it was a house composed of exceedingly able men who conducted their business in a businesslike and exceedingly ab:e manner. There was nothing in the fact of a maa being born a peer to prevent him from being very sensible and good Man. The work of the House of Lords was to review the work of the House of Commons. The country owed the House . of Lords a debt of gratitude for refusing to give its consent to the Home Rule Bill llear,hear). When they considered the present attitude of Lord Rose- berg on that question, even Lord Rosebery might congratulate himself on the House of Lords having- thrown out the Home Rule Bill. Mr Pierpoint re ferred to the useful work of members of the House Of Lords on various Committees, Ile (Mr Pierpoint) remembered sitting for 29 days on a committee which inquired into the subject of Irish railwa ye. The committee was composed of four peers.and four members of the House of Commons. There was an impreesion abroad that members who sat on Par- liamentary Committees were paid for doing so, but that was a great mistake. They did not receive as much as would Pay an omnibus fare to West- minster, though he believed it was Possible for a committee man to get a glass of i water for nothing. (Laughter). Ile had been a member of the House of Commons for 10 years and he considered it a high honour to represent such an important and admirable borough. The ncernbers of the House of Commons might be divid- ed into three classes. There were some ministers I who talked a great deal and sometimes forgot that 1 there were other men who liked to talk. Then there . were men who talked whenever they could, though ' they had not much worth listening to, and those</p>
        <p>who talked whenever they had something to Say which was worta listening to. Then, in the third place, there were men Who did not talk at all. The ' last-named slass were not to be despised because they tad not the gift of the gab. (Hear, hear.) Personally, he never addressed me House unless he had something to say. Mr Pierpoiot proceeded to refer to the conduct of some of the &amp;fish members who Cheered at the defeat and capture of Lord Methuen, and pointed out that although they were disgusted at such conduct they must not for. I get that throughout the war the Irish regiments had fought nobly. (Applause) The war might be nearing its end but however far it was from the close,he trusted that the courage and determination of the British people would be such as to bring it to an end that would make for On well-being of the empire and prevent the recurrence of ouch a state of affair&amp; Colonel- W a Masten, M.P., also responded. They were there. he said, to make a pleasure of business or to put it the other way, a business of pleasure. Be wished to refer briefly to the Government's Loceasing Bill. It might appear to many of them t be a Bill calculated to do very little harm to the licensed victualler in per- forming his arduous duty. I; had been introduced with very good intentions and it had been approached with courage and breadth of in ad. It the amendments which had been submitted to Mr Ritchie by the .trade were accepted, he believed the Bill agouti meet wtil general approval. In its present form ic was too severe on liceassd vim uallers, and he hoped much would ho done in committee to make it less severe.- Ho was ot opinion that a cammittee of law would nave been the hest to take charge of the Bill, but it had been referred to a trade committee. I a Csnamittea of law they could fled more men of unbiassed mind and that was a distinctly important point in many of tae q lestions that wodlia come before the committee. Toe tnil øas full .if pains ad penaltiee to the licensed vic- tuallers. Thera was no reward whatever to the mall who did his duty. it waa somewhat unfair that taere should be nothing but the use of tha bircnrod and no prizes or scholarships to the man who did his duty. (Hear. heir.) It else bill came into law as it was now, nom puss and penal:ies so stringent and arduous, his was afraid the.) would 10k upon the life ot is ilea iaed ectualler as too hard to undertake. Tnere were only two crumtss of comfort which nail b ass throwo to the triads and they were vsry insignificant ones. The first was that the disqualification of ma4istrates to hear licensing questions because they held railway shares was caccelled and they could now sit on the bench. The other was the alteration of the date of tis licens- ing sessions to the spring of each year ' Tha bill itself was divided into three parts (1) habitual drunkarda,(2) licensing law, (3) registration of clubs. Clause four of the first part of the bill relating to the habitual drunk- ard was one which the trade accepted id principle. The habitual druukard was the greatest pea to their business. There was one serious matter he wished to call attention to. The . onus of proof was put upon she publican Be was to be con- sidered guilty until he provedahino,elf innocent. In Eng- lish law; no person was guilty until proved guilty. Tne publican was to be the on exidption in the law of toe land. It was a s risus matter, but he had not the slightest doubt that a just and equitable committee would rectify this matter. Part 2 dealt entirely with the licensing acc The trade approached Mr Ritchie with the view of expressing their opinions before the Bill was drafted. Mr Ritchleohowever declined to accede to their request: The resuls was that some clauses in the 8.11 were far too severe. Ii• com- pelled publicans ta make structural alterations to meet the whims of any magistrate, and there was no relief from Unnecessary oppression. Dealing with the third part of the Bill, that relating to clubs, Colon. I Walker said workingmen's clubs were good institutions, but the bogus clubs were bad and a nuisance to the t ade. Be was one of those who thought and hoped that the ROOMS of Commons would is time recognise the excellent manner in which the licensed victuallers of this country administered 'a very difficult and arduous trade. He was hoping that those who fre• (silently and in the most wanton manner vilified a respectable body of men striving to do their best,would learn in their better judgment how unfairly they, had acted and in the future show a more Christlike sprit of forbearance, dictated to them by what, he believed, was the gen. rat opinion of the vast masses in our King's domicile:ls. (Applause). 6, WARRING roN AND DISTRICT LICENSED VICITIALLER'S ASSOCIA HON."</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN, In proposing the toast, sail the Asso— ciation was composed of men whe ware most desirous of seeing the trade well and lawfully conducted, and who were anxious to maintain a hip standard in all things connected with it. (Hear, bear.) They also materially assisted members who throueh old age or fin social loss required help. and did much useful and gbod work to those who were connected with it. One of the most important features of the new lieensiog bill wss that dealing with the habitual drunkard, by bringing him more effectively under the power of the law, and stopp ng the nuisance he created wherever be appeared. No one would welcocne that reform more than the publican. The habitual drundard was a nuisance and they would all he pleased to see the last pf him. There were a fe w things con- nected with the bill that required some alteration and modification. One ina,ter relvod to the serving of people who were supposed to be hateito.l drundards. Toe drunkard WA9 ibl to a fine of 20 i for the first offence and 40i for the second, whilst, the publican was liable to a fine of £lO for the first offence and £2O tor the second. That appeared be out of all fairness and ought t ) be ' altered before it became law. With respect to clubs, it was well-known that there were many bogus clubs which were nothing more or less than drinking . dens of the worst description anti were responsiol) for much rA the drunkenness which was put down to the publican. Fortunately there were no such cubs in Warrington and 68 hoped a law would be passed to prevent any being opened. (Hear. bear). • Councillor Joins EvaNs, in reply, thanked Sir Gilbert arsenal( for the manner in which he had proposed the toast and also those who had so heartily received it. He, was very p'eased to hear Sir 'Gilbert refer to the objects of the association. With the exc-pticn of one unfortunate circumst ince which occurred on the funeral of the late Queen, they had not had one police case for seven years. Colonel Walker spoke of people villifying the trade and its mem- bers. If the teaching of teetotalers was correct he (Councillor Evans) was in a very bad way. .Ha was not only the oldest licensed victualler, but his people had been In the trade for six generations. He believed he was the fourth Evans to occupy the premises he con- ducted, therefore his was a very bad caw,. (Laughter). But if God gave him health he would endeavour to be a respectable citizen. (Hear, hear.) COUNTY BOROUGH MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>This toast was proposed in felicitous terms by Mr J Bold Walker. In referring to the qualifications of a mag- istrate he said that he ought to he just and unbiassed but not bigoted ; firm tut not obstinate ; tomperate,but n 4 a bigoted teatoTaler. Mi HEPEIERD responded and thought they might °or- gratulate themselves upon having a very fair bench in Warrington.</p>
      </div>
