      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>SUBSCRIPTIONS To 'TUE BENEVOLENT FUND.</head>
        <p>SUBSCRIPTIONS To 'TUE BENEVOLENT FUND.</p>
        <p>COUEICHIOr JOIIN EVANS read a list of guests who had subHeribed to the benevo:ent fund. The amount promised wag 90 2.. (Aprkl,mse). THE NATIONAL DEFENCE LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Mr JAMES MONRO. in proposing— this toast said for many years there had been a ceaseless attack by fana- tical members of the community against the trade and all those connected with it, white the Legislature,which ever Government was in power for the time being to please their own lobeided supporters Or smooth diffreul- ! ties with their opponents, had also harassed them by all kinds of Utopian, but irritating and troublesome laws and regulations till the conduct cf their bes'nse3 bad really become a science and the somo 500 Acts of Parliament bearing on it were as difficult to unravel , as any nightmare. (Hear, hear, and 1 eughter,) It had always been, and always ought to be, a maxim of English law that no man should have his property irate!. feted with, or taken from him either for private, public or national purposes, without his receiving aa equiva- lent in compensation. (Hear, hear) Notwithstanding arsenic scares or any other scares, and the keenest opposition of teetotalers, the bard working hard handed lads o' Lanca,hire would have their drop o' beer and say of it" with all thy faults I love thee still." (Applause.) Councillor ISAAC TURNER responded. Rarinp the evening the Orpheus Quartette Party,con- istating of Messrs Davenport, Whitehouzio, Aukland and Scattergood delighted too company with several pieces. Solos were also well sung by Mc Whitehouse and Mr L Ankland. A flat° ad piccolo duet was contributed by Messrs Brimelow and Roy. The accompaniments were played by Mr Frank Booth. Mr Muirhead of Barrow, gave two clever imi'ations of a trombone and a baasoon and was deservedly applauded. The proceedings which were of a very successful character, concluded shortly after ten o'clock.</p>
      </div>
