      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>PENKETH AND THE COHONATI ON.</head>
        <p>PENKETH AND THE COHONATI ON.</p>
        <p>Annual Parish Meeting.</p>
        <p>The annual parish meeting for the township of Penketh was labia in the Wesleyan Schools, on Mon- day evening, under r; he presitienc y of Dr Murray Thine were also present ;—Messrs Withinshaw, it Garnett, B W Clarke, Snoddy, J Millington (mem- bers ot the Parish Council), Mc J Dodos Mr John Jackson, Mr J ruttier, Bobsoa. Mr daintily, Mr Dan-bin and Mr Foster (clerk). Prior to the pariah meeting, the l'arieti Conned met in private. The following officers were appointed :—Caairman, Dr Murray; vice cuaireasu. Mr J i Withiusha w ; overseers, Messrsi W Clarke and J B Aitken; and, burley men, Messrs James Millington and Joan laden.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>YEAR'S WOES!</head>
        <p>THE YEAR'S WOES!</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN gave a resume of the work of the Parish Council during the past year. During the twelve mouths,he said, the Parish Council haci met eleven times. Toe questions that , had been dealt with had been footpaths, lighting and the roads. Au additional lamp had been put up at Halton'is Bridge and the Sankey Parish Council had tileJ pin, one on weir portion of the road. Halten's - Bridge nad beau a so oject at °tiny meetings, EMI ',titters tied been sent to the Couaty Council, with the result that, they now understood the improve- ment would be done in some little tune. As sug- gested at the last annual meeting, the fact that there was no magistr.te for the county (Warring- ton Divison) rest ling anywhere 011 this aide of War- rington was brought before the County Bench. A deputation saw me magistrates' clerk, and some conespoudeucetooic place,but sa far there had been no result. The footpaths iscl in some instances 'been ciudered, and the question of a stile at the encint the footpath coining out by the station tad been put before Captain Hughes's ag-nt. In the early part of the year Nleslra Gar- nett and inns' cabinet works were des- cloyed by fire, and 'an attempt was made by the Council to induce the firm to rebuild the works. a memorial was also sent :)y the tradespeople of ; the videge Unfortunately the firm weir° unable' t see their way to rebuild. The nightsoil contract of lir Pownall was discussed by the Council, and they unanimously recommended the District Coun- cil to increase the amount paid The cemetery award was found to exclude those residing in Penks di from the right of interment, and after due ith,ttiry and c insideration the Council requested the Ban of Agriculture to amend it in accordance wi;;;; ;he original intention of the Commons Com- missioners. such amendment bad now been made and the cost of the alteration (under £6) had, been borne by the .Council. The London and North- Western Railway had been approached wi; h a view to the) improvement of thefwaiting room accommo- dation awl the oil lamps. Up to the present time the company could not see that any improvement was repined, The fountain had been repaired several timev,;the • cost being defrayed out of the jubilee fund (1897),and the Council hoped that they had now repaired it in such a way that it would be lasting. Frost end by were responsible for the fountain getting irequeutly out of ordmr.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>-THE SANHEL -CHAPZL BURI&amp;L GROUND.</head>
        <p>-THE SANHEL -CHAPZL BURI&amp;L GROUND.</p>
        <p>Mr Hon-so.; thought the burial ground ought to be thrown open to Penketb and Great Sankey. If the vicar had any rights rilitive to the burial around, he did not think the vicar would lose anything if be would forego those rights and allow a dissenting minister to enter the ground. Toe Cue.ruaratY said that by the award, as he read it, it was ii,Chwen of England burial armed. Mr SNODDY asked whether it was not a waste of time to discuss the matter. The burial ground and chapel connected with it belonged to the vicar and if those people who subscribed C 3 it did not kaow what they were doing so much the worse for them. The CHAIRMAN said the burial grouud belonge I to Sankey Chapel and itnketts had hardly any right to suggest anything. I must be done privately, if at all. Mr Seiontir a •ked if the Vicar could alter the matter ? Mr leinggeierox said the Vic ir had no power Co pick andgoimose and allow whoing be thought St to use the burial ground. The subject then drooped. COMPLAINTS AB MIT 'IIIS LONDON AND NORTH-WESTERN</p>
        <p>'Dodds had said. Some of them had been waiting for ! the leaders in toe village Co take the lead in the matter and state what they wanted. He thought it was an improper t ling to take any money out of the rates for celebratisg the event. (Hear, hear.) fie hoped Mr Dodds would press toe matter forward, and get some- thing done that night.</p>
        <p>Mr DODDS a4keitor the names of tbe members of the Parish Cou,ctl.</p>
        <p>The CLERIC edd the Council was composed of Dr Murray (cUatroaan), Mr Wicuinshavr Mr Parker, Mr It Garnett, Mr Clarke, eir Saoddy and Mr Millington. It WAS ultimately agreed that a comrantee cousiszing of the Parish Omacil, with Mr Didds, Mr Grane and Mr Hodgkin/ion. make arrangemeai tor the celetrations at Penketh.</p>
        <p>Suggestions were invited for celebrating the eve. !di bIsTODDY thou4ot the chil men ought.to be gathered together awl games aad races pzovided for them as wet' as a tea. _ _ _</p>
        <p>_ 111 r Wrrarssaaw suggested tut the Penketh Band should be asked to give their services on that occasion. The band got • god deal or support from the neigh- bourhood. _</p>
        <p>A member of the band said the residents of the vdlage bad no claim on the ba id. Toe band belonged to the Penketh Tannery Company. The CHAIRMAN said be always supported the band,a ad others ep ,ke to the same effect. Mr SHODDY thought it would be very strange if the bans went out of the villsge on the day it was most required. It was possiole that the baud might be able to return f r the evening. Mr DODDS said be was sure the members of the band did not wish to put themselves against the township. At the pri,sent moment the band was in debt and the members thought if they could get an engag meat at a high p -tee, it aught clear the band of debt. Ho (Mr Dad as) would netlike them to suffer through the band stack. in the village. The directors of the company would meeting ma week, and no don't the directors would defray any loss occasioned by the band through , stopping in he village. Mr Durraisni thought the band ought to be paid out of the celebration funds. _ _</p>
        <p>It was ultmastily decided to invite the band to give their services on Coronation Day. Kr DODDS chought rue Coma:does ought to provide at least one good meal for the children and the aged poor. (Rear, hear.) OR the euvestien of Vr Jogai JacKsoz,r, it was deci- del Co leAve the c'etaits in tho hands ot the Corninit inee:An clue u led with an acknowiedginont of the Chairman'.3 servicai.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>RAILWAY STATION AT FIDLER'S FERRY.</head>
        <p>RAILWAY STATION AT FIDLER'S FERRY.</p>
        <p>Mr Sxonnv ,called attention t o the halting of the road leading to the London and North-Western Railway Station at Fidler' Ferry. He contended that the oil lamps which were used were of very little use. Those who had used the road during the winter months would know how unsatefactory the lighting of the road was. fie had known the lamps to be out f)r weeks together.. When they compared the lighting of the road leading to the Cheshire Lines Station at Great Sankey ' they would know what a tri•mendous contrast there was. When the Parish Council drew the Landon and North-Western Railway Company's attention to the lighting of the road, it was thought that the company would take advantage] of the fact that the gas main had been extended to Standish'a Corner, and would use the gas for lighting the station sad its approaches. But the L)ndon and North-Western 'Railway Company did not do things properly. The Parish Council also • drew attention to ' the want of passenger accommodation on the down line. There was but a shelter hut, which was a very dirty Place. and was used by !ale company's workmen when they had their meals. Too fire was only lighted when re- quired for cooking purposes and it was a place altogether ensuited for female accommodation, not to say male. He would ask the Clerk to read the reply of the Com- pany to the Councii's complaints. The CUM( read a letter from Mr Dent, ol Lime-Street station. Liverpool, wino wrote that the-Loudon and North-Western R.ilway Company had put lamps on the approaches to the station at Fidler's Ferry, and these, In -bis opinion, ,met the requiraments. He was not aware that the Hell; was defective. The waiting-room on the down side of the line was similar to that provided at other stations where the conditions and traffict were the (ARV% The accommodation appeared to be suffieient. Mr BNODDY said the traffic, both passenger and goods. Was twice as Much at Fidler's Ferry as at Great Sankey and yet the Cheshire Lines had twenty gas lamps at the station and its approaches. He would suggest that the parish meeting forward a letter to the London and North-Western Railway Company pointing out the necessity of inspection on their part and asking them to take up the matter. He moved a resolution to that effect. Mr RoßloN seoanded, ant the resolution was unanimously carried.</p>
        <p>THE SANITARY RATE.</p>
        <p>Mr Jamzs FISECER suggest( d that property owners should be compelled to pay the sanitary rate instead of the tenants.</p>
        <p>The enemata:l Raid that was a cpestion of arrange- ment between property owners and tenants. Toe Parish Council bad no power to act in the tristt‘l'. Mr Frsues thought it was ridiculous that tenants should be canal to pay a rate for BO mothine from which they received no benefit. It was very unfair. The CHAIRMAN : I quite agree with you. Mr FISHER : The owners of cottages pay the sanitary rate. Mr MILLINGTON said Mr Fisher must get the law alt6red. Toe CHAIRMAN Sail there were many people who did not take advanta 20 of the dust contract, but who had to pay towards the expeu93. The subjcet then drupp, d. THE JUBILEE FUND.</p>
        <p>Mr SNOOD-Y asked whether. when- the Jubilee fund was exhauir eci in repairing the fountain the future expense in connection with it would come on the parish? The CHADIXILN : Ye.!. Tho jubilee fund is getting exhausted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>&amp; COUNTY MAGISTRITE WANTED.</head>
        <p>&amp; COUNTY MAGISTRITE WANTED.</p>
        <p>Mr SHODDY thought it an opportune time CO introduce the sutject of the need of a county magistrate in tile village. ;Mr Honsos Psked whether the Parish Council had applied to the Chancellor of the Duchy of Lancaster or to the member of Parliament for the Newton Division (CoLnel Pilltinglion) with reference to the appointineut of a county magistrate from Penkoth. The Crtaircasy said the matter was brought before the County Bench at Warrington. Mr SNODDY thought it might be advisable ti interview or write timi member for the Newton Division asking him to use his influence in the matter. Mr MILLINGTON hoped that the chairman (Dr Murray) wculd be made Chairman of the Rural District Council and then Penketh would have a representative on the County Bench. (Hear, hear.) Mr Idoesort moved that they.trish Council take further steps in tha matter. Mr FItHEEt seconded and the motion was agreed to. THE CORONATION. PROPOSED CELEBRATIONS AT PENEETH.</p>
        <p>Mr DOBSON. in referrieg to the subject of the Corona- tion c..:lebrations, thougrit a grant might be obtained through the Rural District Council for the purpose. Mr DODDS thought the members of the Parish Council ought to have some proposition ready to put before the meeting for celebrating the Corona- ti. They were all sincerely loyal and he hoped all would unite—Churchmen and Dissenters, Radinals and To;ie I—in celebrating the Coronation of the King. Pew of those present could remember a pre- vious coronation, and he hoped they would bairn some- thing in Penketh to commemorate the event. 113 was in favour of a ettong committee being appointed and la them have a eelebratioa of their own, indepHident f the Rural Disttiot Council ior any other authority. (Hear, hear.) Mr ENODDY raid he wculd like to erupliasiv what II</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>– THREE KINGDOMS FOR TWO LEGS.</head>
        <p>- THREE KINGDOMS FOR TWO LEGS.</p>
        <p>A dying King of E 'gland once begged his physician to tell him plainly what he thought of his ca e. " Well, truly," said the p aysician, " I wonld not have your Mai sty's two lee for tour Majesty's throe kingdoms " " But I would gladly give my tnree kingdoms for y tfro lee," replied the King. The exchano cool I not be eff .cted ; but even if i had been possible, the phy- sician, as we have seen, was averse to the deal. Con- sideration of this anecdote-rawhich, unlike a good many of its kind, happens to be true—brings home the fact that the rich m.n'e hold upon sound health is little, if indeed at all, superior to that of the poor man, the beat remedial agent known being practically within the reach of all.</p>
        <p>_ The prover`, that it is better to be born lucky than rich his been explained by showing that whereas the born rich som.tim-s beeoms poor, the born lu icy always beceme rich—and contrive to remain so. Tb. explanaloo woold be more satisfacrery if riches ured happiness; bu, they don't. They go far towards attain- ing it, but by no means assure it. fere is a true story of nerd Noir turned into good fortune by simple means. When Mrs Emma Besot:, ot Bayston Rill, near Shrew- bury, married, her prospects of happiness were as bright as those of most people who enter into that state. But the promised happiness was marred by if-health. Says Mrs Bason : " Shortiy, after my marriage I began to droop and fade. I bad an ever-present feeling of languor and fatigue. After eating, however light the meal, I was oppressed by a weight at my chett, with severe pain between the shoulders. Then my appetite failed almost entirely. When I asked my doctor what it was caused me to be to weak, be evaded rnv question and inquired if any Of my family were consumptive.. Nothing seemel to bene- fit me ; and my friends, I have since learned, thought I was not long for this world. While in this condition my sister brought me two bottles of Nether Seigel'a Cura- tive Syrup. She had great difficulty in inducing ma to try it, but at last I consented to do so. and from that day my recovery began. Mine was a slow convales- cence. and I took several bottles of Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup before my health was clmpletely re- stored ; but I was encouraged to cootinue with it, find- g myeel arrow better daily." Few rurna of Fortune's wheel bring a happier change than that der:Wood in the foregoing declaration made before a Commissioner of Oaths on January 11th, 1902.</p>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>Dasitcn.—ln °rumen:ion with the Penk3l.h Cricket Club a wry successful dancs has been hi-ld in St Mlry's sun- day choolroLfl. Grist Baulrey, on Friday evening 13.3 t. Sixty °lupins were present, and daneiniT was induiged in fr )m eight till two The M.C'3. were 11,ssis '1 E Shoenridee and A Cutteril/. ani the mosic was' supplied by Nt-eers Kendrick and Etay. It.froAnneate were supp led by Mrs Deady.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Personal.</p>
        <p>Capt&amp;n 1U Medlei,-,;firhrist Volunteer Matti- lion the Princ6 of We' V (South Lan- cashire Regiment), ha. resigned Ms commission. Social Gathering of Liberal Workers. Al !erman an 1 Mrs Henry itoberts entertained the Liberal workers in Whitcross Ward to tea on Tuesday evening, in recognition of their long ass- ciation with he Ward and the Ward Committee. About seventy persons responded to the invitation and an excellent tea waS served in the schoolroom) Thewlis-street. The catering was in the capable hands of Councillor James Beans, The subsequen, proceedings were presided over by Alderman R doerts, who was accompanied on the platform by :Councillor- James Evans and Councillor Walter Brough. Some Interesting speeches were delivered and several musical items given. Mr Barton. jun„ contributed two violin solos, and songs were sung in Mr Nathan Cook, Mr Cook, juur., and Mr Peter Smith. The accompaniments were played by xir T Barton and Mr T Brough. S311:10 amusing recita- tions were given by Mr Willcocks.-11. cordial vote of thanks was tendered to Alderman and Nits Roberts for their hospitality, on the proposition of Councillor Brough, seconded by Mr T Dale, and sup- ported by Councillor Kvans.—&amp; very pleasant gathering concluded with the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
        <p>A rigor ht cipzl whila cromin,9; tha D4nobo, sovooteeo per!on3, including seven' children, Pain drowned.</p>
        <p>A gentleman woo tendered a bad shilling at a Ken- sington inn, and was taken to the police scation by tee landlord on a charge of trying to pass base coin, on Wednesday recovered £l2O darn.aree for slander. Mr Justice Grantham on Wednesday passed sentene, of death at this Old Bailey on George Woolf, soldier, sor the murder of his sweetheart on Tottenham Marshes, The prisoner, who protested his innocence, gave evidence on his own behalf. A remarkable motor-car ride has just been completed with success by Mr Wfli Vanderbik,,:jun,who (as the 'Globe'') travelled from Monte Carlo to Paris in seven- teen hours. As the distance is nearly 650 miles, Mr Vanderbilt's average rate of speed was some ihirty-eight miles an hour. The route included some hill-climbing in the Esttrel Mountains and a certain amont.t of bad roads near Paris. The nor used was of a similar type to that which holds the world's record.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>NASAL CATARRH CURED SY T HE DROUET TREATMENT.</head>
        <p>NASAL CATARRH CURED SY T HE DROUET TREATMENT.</p>
        <p>The last "Supplement " to the " Journal for the Deaf and Analytical 11-iyiew of Diseases of the 1310,e and Throat," points out the con,tant!y increasing success attained by the Drouet Method of Treatment for Catarrh of the Nose and Throat. This is undoubtedly due to th 3 thoreuahuess with wt-Pch this SpecialiLed Treatment is adapted to the needs of each individual case. Au ilky- tration of a Cure in a complicated CASS is described by ,Mr J flowey. The nasal ifFmptoms were accumulations of mucus in the nose, bad smell to the breath, frequent collee:ions of phlegm at the back of the throat, dry nets and stuffiness of the nose deafness and distressing noises in the head. Alihougb this condition had persisted for 12 years, the cure was complete and is described as tollows : Framwellgate Moor, Durham, Feb. lath, 1902.</p>
        <p>Dear Sir,—You will think I hare forgotten you. I was waiting to see if the disease troubled me again, but ir has not ye. lam Wad to say lam a good deal better. Thanki to your treatmn. I will not require any more medicine.—l remain, yours sincerely, J. HONEY. Those intere4tel in this subject will find full particu- lars cf the other cured co s in the " Supplement.' to the " Journal for the Deaf." A free copy of this placation . can be ()brained by addressing the Secretary, Drouet ' Institute, Regent's Park-road, London. N.W. Each " Journal " is accompanied by a Patient's Report Form, which enables applicants to receive full IpArticuNre for applying the treatment at home. Any ad vice by correspondence is absolutely free.</p>
      </div>
