      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>STEADISM AND TREASON.</head>
        <p>STEADISM AND TREASON.</p>
        <p>[BY MB. H. W HITHAM]</p>
        <p>Just recently I have been poring over a number of journals written some fifty years ago. Written in England and for English readers, they were reprin- ted in the United States, so as to keep the suns and daughters of Uncle Saul posted in the peculiar way some of the people ar, that day had of showing their love for their native heath.</p>
        <p>Under the heading of " Conservative Peace" a long article is devoted to the work of sketching how a Caalitioa Government could bb formed, in which Mr DL,raeli was to be dragged at the tail of Mr Cob leo, and Lord S:anley to be smothered under the broad brut of Mr Bright. Oar old friend" Punch has a word to say to the dtead'a of his day, and to we it seems appro- priate to say it at this time:—</p>
        <p>Bt there ! if I've a thing to do. my maxim always is to do it. . .</p>
        <p>If I've a job to go through, I makes my mind up to go through it. :Tie all the same, I don't:care what—washing, or ironing or scrubbing, And if so be as we [have got tosive them Roo- shape there a drubbing I never leaves my work half done—a stocking or a gownd half mended; What has to be agin begun is twice the tune afore 'tis ended :</p>
        <p>And what I find with needlework, is found, Um certain sure, with nations Sa don't be ted away too quirk and quidd!e with negotiations. _</p>
        <p>The litera,ure of fifty years ago will fully repay any Britisher just now. Then, as now,and pos.tble with more reason at that time, there were men holding high positions who thought it wise and well to bring all the nation'edirty linen out into the market place to be washed. In other respects times have changed very much. eow, week atter week, I take up my Sunday paper, and find a Lendoner, one who to-day is fond of talking ab iut his brother Boer, treating his American readers to column after column of praise and glorification of •he B and vituperation of the worst kinds of the British Government. Our dear friend, Mr Stead, and hi. " stop the war friends forget 9ne thing, they for- get that some of the people not only read what is , written and said to-day, but tee' sometimes re- , member west was written and told yesterday, and are gui led in their actions accordingly. Mr Edward T Cook formerly editor of the "Daily ' News," in his book "The Rights and Wrongs of the Transvaal War" throws an amusing light on the facility wan which the celebrated Stead changes sides. Five years ago, it seems, the author of "if Christ canes to Chicago," called Mr Kruger 'The Sultan at Peoria," and added in his usual fiewery flowing style, "Paul Kruger, like the Sultan, is master of the situation, for he believes in himself and in the trusted minority who do not h-sitate to shoot. Kruger, like Abdul Ramie, has a population differing in race, in religion, in language, under is feet. He has dh.arcued them as thoroughly as the Turk digsrms the Armenian villagers He denies them the elementary rights of free citizens in a free country" He laughs at their petitions, and shrugs his shoulders at, their peetest." Did Steal believe this when he wrote it five years ago? Did he know that, as a powerful and influential writer he was at that time engaged in forming a powder opinion the outcome of which he is, at home and abroad moaning about to-day? lie, in fact, has sown the wind, the nation is, to-lay, and will for years reap the whirlwind. See the change he makes in his brochure 'bearing the characteristic title "Hell:Let Loose." He now mildly informs eie coun- trymen that they are "trampling under foot the elementary rules of civilized war, and d iing deeds of rapine and of outrage which recall the Huns of Attila and the Bashi Bazooka of Abdul the Damned" We must all bitterly regret tee war, but we must also deplore the fact that there always seems to be a secret history about these matters. It would be interesting to know a little of the private history of the Pall Mall Gazette" of sixteen years ago when Mr Cook, Mr Millen. (now Lord Milner) and Mr btead were running it, then we should perhaps understand Mr Stead's present attitude. Now, I have no hesitalon in saying that, in these days of electric newspaper work the " stop the war" agitators in Eng'and have done more to prolong the war in South Adrian than either the blundering government, or the "contented souls with:lth° flannelled fools at the wicket or the muddied •,oafs at the goal." Tee same has bean the case in the United States. The certainty is that the Philippine war would have been over long ego had it not have been the misfortune of America to haves fair sample of the same traitorous nature to deal with. Speaking of traitors implies treason is around somewhere.</p>
        <p>We have in the States two gentlemen who hare no objecrion to sesing the magic letters, " attached to their names. I refer to Mr Joseph' Devlin, end Mr William Redino•nd two of Irsland's representatives. Addressing an audience in New York, and reported March 23-d, Mr Redmond said : "If our policy in the British Parliament can do nothing else, we can exp)ga our grief, we can harrass the British Empire, we can stand upon the floor of Parliament and pra‘: and call God's ble-sing on the Boars every time the English are defeated. If we cannot in the English Parliam-ut win home ' rule, I promise you we will destroy the Parlia- ment of England. If we cannot govern ourselves, we can take good care not to allow them :o govern themselves."</p>
        <p>If this is not treason, and perjured treasorn words have lost their meaning. I have just read in the " Manchester Guardian ' of March 3rd, the speeches of three gentlemen who hold a deservedly high place in the esteem of their proud counir v- men, and whom I shoilld be B)rry to class with Mr Stead. I refer to Mr F Harrison, Mr Bryce and Mr Birrell, and yet one wonders how they manage to reconcile the varied features of current history. Thee gentlemen all deal with the two most burning questions of the day. and read with an eye turned towards Mr Asquith's address to his consti,-,uents, most lead one to think the Liberal Peril will never agree as to a policy towards Ireland, or as to the justice or injustice of the Baer war. How can Ireland ever become reconciled when her sons, at home and abroad, never tire of tellinst you their sale ! aim is complete severance from the British Empire? Is it not true as Mr Birrell said, " The man was not in politics whom he could fancy standing at the table of the [base of Commons, on the very spot where Mr Gladstone twice stood introducing the Lioms Rile Bill tor Ireland I supported Mr Gladstone's policy with my vote, but as Mr Asquith puts it, 'proportions and persp-ctives have shifted,' and with the experienCe I have had of the Irish in Greater Ireland they would:no longer have even my tolerant support." _ . .</p>
        <p>I had a little argument recently with a rather wild Irishman. I had just read of the shooting of a p:Jor woman in her cibin. She had committed the crime of buying hay from a farmer who waq boyco; ted for having mud his rent. My wild Irish</p>
        <p>frren,3 owns two ho'u-e-ei, one of which 'he_re.nts out</p>
        <p>for 25s a week I BimpPly put the queNtion as to the justice of boycotting his tenant for the crime of paying rtnt. The poor ignorant fellows know noth- ing of the rent question. Its ethical and social featuree they are strangers to. but my frieni ran right against a practical side of it, and hurt him-</p>
        <p>self. '- Touching the Boer question, Kr Bryce said " the Liberal party desires negotiations for pesos." I take it the British Government a'so carries about with it a very fervent desire of the same sort. All parties desire peace, but it is surely wrong to say in the same breath that the British Government is engaged in destroying two Republics. Suppose the original Barna settlers in New Amsterdam had succeeded in carrying out the same policy what would have been th," consequence and where would the America of to-day be ? .Remember Punch's words :—" What has to be again begun ia twice the time afore 'tis ended."</p>
        <p>H. WHITHAM.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>WARRINGTON. Fire at Cockedge Mts.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON. Fire at Cockedge Mts.</p>
        <p>Many poople were attracted to Cnkhedge Mills shortly before noon on Monday by the soundinz of "buzzer "which denotes an outbreak of fire. For- tunately the fire was not of a serious character. it having been caned by a hard substance getting into to,e machine in the blowing departm.lit whica produced a spark, and it ignited some cotton. Those who saw the outbreak were equal to the emergency, and the flames were quickly extin- guished by the firm's fire appliances Toe Warring- ton Fire Brigade bad been sent for, hut before they started out they were informed that their services would not be required. The fire was discovered by Herbert Gilbert one of the overloekers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Primitive Methodist Chapel, Legh- Street.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Primitive Methodist Chapel, Legh- Street.</p>
        <p>ILast Stvaday special servioes were held at the Legh- street Primitive Methodist Chapel in aid of the Choir ' Fund. Large congregations assembled at each service. : and very effective sermons were delivered morning and evening by a student from the P.M. College. ' Alexandra Prix, Manchester. Ica the afternoon I lan exceilent musical service was given by 1 the choir, Mr T Steel presiding. Toe music I was of a high-class character, and IOU:lout exception both sOlOii and chorusee were ably rendered. A notable feature in all services of this character at Legh-street is the part played by tie choir, under the leaciership of Mr A Spruce. All the /anthems and choruses were brought to a high state of pe. tecti-n, upon the attain- ment of which the choir and their conductor are to be congratulated. Stainer's anthem " God so loved the World" (Oraeifixion) was renderel as a quartette, and the effect was very fiue. The choir are for- tunate in. having a very able organist, Mr F Podmore. M.1.11.M., who is rapidly rising to a posi- tion .of high rank atnoug the muucians of Warrington . A detailed programme is given testew :—Solo "My hope is in the everlasting,' Mr A Griffiths; chorus "On Fettle., whose almighty power," Choir; solo "Two cities, ' Maclaine Caristiem ; solo " Ora pro Nobis," Mies Ida Hewitt; anthem " Rook of Ages," choir; duet "Love divine," Madame Christian and Mr A Griffitbs ; solo "The ctiidren's home," Mies Ida Hewitt; quartette "God so loved the world," Allrese4 Bridge sod Woodland. Weirs Corrin and Spruce; solo "Lead, kindly Light." fdadamea,ri tun; chorus "Kyrie Kiesoo," choir. At the eveareg service solos were given by Miss Alice, Bridge also Mr A Grifßaui, and toe onorus," The Heavens are telling," w .ii sung ey the choir, the trio being taken be Miss Alice Bridge, Mr J Clrriu and Mr J Bissell. The eff ate put forward by Mr Spruce and his colleagues proved highly suocetsful, and the collections were very satisfactoey. . Bold-street Wesieygn Church.</p>
        <p>At this place of worship on Sunday the preacher in tins morning was :he Rev W C Neviile and in the evening the Rev d L Milne, pastor of St dehrt's Pr_sby teriaa Cnarcb. In the afternoon a musical service and organ recital were held. There was a large congregation. The Werriegtou Male Voice Choir gave an effective render- ing of "Tao Crusade,," and Mr Harry Berrey's fiae tenor voice was heard ti advantage in" fhen shall the righteous thine "(" Elijah "). Seats had been reserved to, P.S.A. Aud Bible cies. members. Ir Frynk Hickman presided at toe organ and gave a recital with his customary ability. Councillor Walter Brough occupied cnsir. A special c Alection was made in aid ot the n,-w organ fund. lift Bough. in the course of his speach,said that an important part of all public worship was the service et praioe. The great Apostle, in writing to the early Christians, told . ChM " to Si6lit, making melody in their heats to tbe lord." Music touched and stirred the emotions last could be awakened in their ful- -1 nes •in no other way. Whatley, r they twid a great religi- ous movement they bad argreat new outburst of song. They used to say that Luther's hymns did more than Luther's sermons, and when the great evangelical revival of the ei nteenrm century came along, there was sueh religious singing as the world had never heard be- fore. Music was ene of the handmaids of religion, and the church that eschewed rondo, or failed to make a right Use of it was sure to be a cold, situated member of the Body of Cnrist Therefore it followed that the murk: of th . sanctuary should for the most parr be suet) that all could join b—id all should join in to the best of their pi were. True spectacle presented in some churches, where the congregation 84 and listened tj a performance ten lered by the choir (composed at least oceasionally of a. numb:r of men and women who had only a profes- sional interest in toeir work) was by no m cans edifying. Worsnio should never to done by proxy,and though the heart might accompany with its silent rnieody some musical rendarioe sweetly rendered by mare skilled voices, he conteaired that no religious service should be held in which the chief mudcal items were not within the reach of all. (Applause.) _</p>
        <p>Buckley-street P.S. A. A special open meeting of this society ,wai hell on Sunday, and was Ltreaq, attnaded. Mr Thomai M,lrton presided. The speaker was Mr J Corbett. The "E jiaft Glee Pry gave" The long day closes,"" In a cell or cattail deep," and "home, sweet home." Mr Corbett gave a solo, accimpanied by Mr R Scirisbrick no his one-stringed fiddle. A special collection was taken in aid of the circa, fund.</p>
        <p>- - - - Wycliffe P.S.A Society. The meeting of this society on Sunday afternoon wee presided over 'by Mr A Downes. la the course of a brief address. the Chairman made a few pointed observations on the tc.xt "13-sr vo one another's burdens, and s. fulfil the law of Corist." Mr Ei Faris% of Lltvton, de- livered an address in which he commented on the con- ditions under which poor people lived in this country. A man lacked tnep true spirit of Christianitg aba could wk past the ninralsluah of our big towns and be quite indifferent to the welfare of those who were less fortu- nate than himself. The world required men of strong Christian character who were prepared to maks sacrifices in the service of tneir fellow-men.—Mr B Farrar also addreined the meeting.—San organ solo was very effec- tively given by Mr W l Payton, Mus B ac.—Tne service was also taken part in by Mr George Smiley and Mt W Beswick, jun.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>_ --Is- GREAT SANKEY PARISH COUNCIL ---- The Local Government Board and Sankey Brook.</head>
        <p>_ --Is- GREAT SANKEY PARISH COUNCIL ---- The Local Government Board and Sankey Brook.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of Gre4t Sankey Pariib Council was held oa Tt.lsday evening in the Board School, the ehir b ing titken pro resi by Mr George Thompson. Teere were as prPsent :—M-s6r4 T Lialtoa,H Singleton, fidanaw. and J Smith (clerk).</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF CIIAIISAII.N. Mr Thomp?on auuou .cea that cue first busiaeSs was tiv electim of a chairman for the ensuing year. Mr Halton, in proposing the re-election ot Mr Thomp— Son, said it bad been his inter.ton when he came into the room ro propose the election of Mr Fulton Smith as chsiraysn,but he had been informed that that gentleman had !eft the district and was now living in Watrington- In these circumstsnces be proposed th 3 re-appointment of Mr l'lnalpson,who had discharged his duties wonder- fully weti. (Hear, hear.) He b)lieved in these honours being distributed :rum year to year, but in the circum- stances they cluld not do better than re-elect Me Toompson. (Hear, hear.) Mr Singleton seconded, and sail he would have supported the election of Mr Smith if he had been gm- posed. The resolution W 49 carried unanimously. The Chairmsn. in reply, said be regretted that Mr Smith had left the neighbournood, They could ill-afford to I )se 1119 n of his stamp. as he was one who had taken a lively interest in the affairs of the parish. He was not now a rsifent, but, pernaps lie riaglot qualify and Con- tinuo his useful work. (Bear. hear). He (the Chair- man) thsnked the meeting for the honour which had beers conferred upon him and promised to do all he could to further the intare3ls of the hEople of Great Sankey. VICE-CHAIRdIaN. It was decided t pleto me the election of vice-chair- man to the next meeting. OVERSEERS.</p>
        <p>Mi,Sqrs Halton. Wrier., and tue Chairman were ap— pmuted th o7erßeeit.</p>
        <p>WINWICK ASYLUM EWAGE Q7ESTION. SANKEY BROOK ONCE MORE. SERIOUS COMPLAINTS.</p>
        <p>The CI 'rk read .a letter be haa written to the Local G wernrueht Bard on the subject of the pollution of Sankey Brook. It was a somewhat lengthy but, inter- esting docurnmt, and stated that their representative to the Rural District C eancil had reported that the Asylums Committee of the County Council of Lancaster intended to cut a sower from the new Aajlurn at Win- wick to Longford Bridge.where the sewage would enter the Warrington and Sankey B:ook, which formed part tfin tiound.ary of the Courey Borough'of Warringtm% His (the Clerk's) Council uncerstood that the Warring- t n Rural District Council had moved that permission be granted for the construction of the sewer,and,in view of toe certain conscquenes of danger to public health, felt greatly alarm-d. The brook for many years ' bad been a fearful nuisance, and the subject of much correspondence and discussion. The , borough of St. Eirlene appeared to have been turning. i sewage into the brook, but were, they were informed. erecting plant for treating their sewage. The Warring- I ton Town were a,so prepared to treat their sew- age, S3CEIS part of which was at present discharged into , the brook. It was of the utmost importance that the brook should not be used as an open sewer for the Wit:- wick Asylum. The Clerk stated that he had received a letter from the Local G wernmeat Board to the effect that a copy of the Councii's letter had been forwarded to the Lancashire Asylums Board" for their observations thereoa." BETTER LIGHT REQUIRED AT SANKEY BRIDGES STATION.</p>
        <p>The Clerk state t thar 119 had written to Mr Andrews,</p>
        <p>Lime-street Station. Liverpool, with reterence to the lighting of Sankey Briars railway st ation. The letter stated thst at the annual meeting for the parish of Great Sankey, attention was called to the lighting of the station premises at Sankey Bridges. It was con- sidered that tho oil lamps at present in use might with • advantage b 3 replaced by gas lamps. It WaR &amp;ISO sug- gested that a lamp be erected on the roadway approacla to the station alongside the canal. Mr Smith added that s:lr Andrews bad acknowledged tbe letter and had said that it should be forwarded to the proper quarter. The Chairman : There has been a representative of the company down to the station, and I have no doubt the matter will be attended to. CADBURY'S Cocoa Is a pure relined beverage, nutritiou stimulating, a-d digestible. The" lAncet " says it" repro- sante the standard of highest purity." Entirely free from admixtures, such as kola, malt, hops, alkali, etc. Insist upon baring OADBIJKY'S, as other Cocoas are sometimes übstituted tor eitza profit. In Packets and Tins only. (4.) TRUSS.—New washable waterproof is the most suitable for . tropical climates ; is light, clean, Cool, comfortable, and durable; can be worn whilst bathing. WHIT &amp; WRIGHT Surgical Ins' rnment Makers. 93, Renshaw-street, opposite top of Bold-street, Liverpool„—Adat.</p>
