        <p>JOHN WALKER &amp; CO. COMMERCIAL AND G-ENERAL PRINTERS, EXAMINER PRINTING WORKS. BEWSEY STREET WARRINGTON. TELEPHONE NO. 0489. _PAPER, RILJED TO ANY _I.A.TTERN</p>
        <p>JOHN WALKER &amp; CO. ALL KINDS OF PRINTING FOR CONCERTS TEA PARTIES SOIREES SERMONS BALLS MEE TINOS Sec., ,kc</p>
        <p>THE CHANCELLOR Of The Budget Proposals. the Exciixonuu is a greatly daring man to venture a duty on imported corn at a moment, when,on his own confession,there is reason to hope that the war is near its end. The tax, of course, is the feature of the Budget, and round it most of the discussion will gather. With the earlier part of the speech, vindicating his estimate and justify" ing his action _ in not cutting it down because gt the peace project, few people will be inclined tg quarrel. It is highly probable, however, that the CHANCELLOR, in apportioning the amount to be raised by taxation and by loan, was influenced at the eleventh hour by the conference at Pretoria. Where ignorance is bliss it is folly to be wise, and the Cabinet alone hold the secret upon whom the CHANCELLOR had predatory designs in presenting his entire original demand for ready cash. Still, Sir MICHAEL HICKS-BEACH did not wholly ignore the silver lining to the cloud. Indeed, he used it most effectively to we rk on the sympathies of his audience and of the nation at large. Look at it as you like, was practically what he said, and you will be willing to respond to my appeal' Those who are against any movement that could possibly be construed as a sign of weakness were bound to applaud this formidable provision for keeping our army in the field. Thos3 who object to it as intimidatory or unnecessary on that score are asked to assign it to bringing our soldiers home and restoring peace and prosperity to aa embittered country. They are told that if thinge go on as is hoped a large part of the money will be used to show how we love our enemies, and are willing and ready to adopt a magnanimous atti- tude to a brave foe. The alternative propositiona are calculated to sooth the susceptibilities of both classes of ratepayers. They pay their money and take their choice of view as to its disposition. The question which, however, must excite seri- ous debate is the proposal to raise two and a half</p>
        <p>million of the comparatively small extra taxati4 by a duty on imported corn. The CliAlicELL(</p>
        <p>put the argument as deftly as he could, and he was perfectly right in saying that no baker would have to put an extra farthing on a loaf of bread because the flour might cost 'lira the fraction of a farthing more per loaf. But the price of bread changes with variations in the marka,and itstands to reason that when a slight rise or fall occurs the baker will take the new element into his calcula: tion. Unless good fortune again follows the action i of the CHANCELLOR, and the; addition is balanced by an increase of harvests abroad which would tend to cheapen bread, the.burden will ultimateli fall on the consumer in an enhanced price of bread at certain periods, and any way he will feel the 1 difference. The aim of Free Trade is to make the cost of the staple means of life as cheap as possible for all classes,because we are a great manufacturing nation, and live by our manufactures for markets all over the world, and it is our interest to meet competition by making the cost of production e-4 low as it can be. Therefore, we endeavour to clir tribute the taxes without favour. The effect of the present proposal would seem to be to heighte the price of food by a process of taxation whiel' shall relieve the English corn grower of his share of the:burden. Sir MicaA.mr. HICKS-BEACH'S use of Mr. BUXTON'S defence of Mr. GLADSTONE Wils clever, but it Was not sound argument. It 1001, be quite true that a small tux which exists is appreciably felt, but a new tax is taken notice 12f and brought into the calculations. The real objee- tion to tho fresh departure is, however, its insidi‘ rumness. Future Chancellors of the Exchequer may not have the same determination as IMICIIAEL HICKS-BEACH, and may be tempted ta give more financial help to the landlords, for the are the people who will benefit from any extensi°ll of the scheme,</p>
        <p>MiNaia</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <p>Tun people of this country The Peace Negotiations. must be careful lest they loss their good name for equability of temperament. On Thursday those who were most sanguine of an immediate settle- ment of the conflict with the Boers were greatly depressed in the belief that a serious hitch has occurred in the negotiations. There is really no cause either for elation or despondency. They</p>
        <p>who know the Dutch character are aware that tinder the most falourablo circumstances that curious body of men who have gone to Pretoria to meet Lord KITCHENER would be slow in their mental movements. Those who study the situa- tion in South Africa are also fully aware that the problem which offers itself for solution is an exceedingly difficult one. Remembering these facts the frequent meetings of the C ibinet and the stream of despatches from South Africa which involve conferences of Ministers with the KING, should not produce any sensation. The "Pall Mall Gazette" stated authoritatively on Thursday night that actual terms had not yet been submitte d by the enemy. That is quite consistent with the activity of our G warm:L:l3ot during the past few clays, and with Mr. BALFOUR'S guarded announce- ments in the House of Commons. The draft of a possible settlement will be made piecemeal by a series of cautious inquiries by —C. Schalkß URGER, STEYN, and their friends. It has been asserted in one London newspaper that a hitch has occurred on the amnesty question. That, of course, is the most embarrassing matter of Eall. Let us take first the Boer view. What- ever sacrifices the commandants and others now at Pretoria may be disposed to make in recognition of their defeat, they are certain to be most uuwilling to give away those allies in Cape Colony who have helped them substantially. They may ba told that their own people will be treated with generosity, and that money will be found for the 'restoration and restocking of farms in the two States for the combatants on both sides, but</p>
        <p>_ that we must draw the lino of magnanimity against the rebels from our own Colony. The Boers would naturally be averse from a compact which would brand them as selfish and indifferent to men of their own race who had no selfish end to gain. Let us look on the other hand at the case of the Cape Loyalists, who find so strong an advo- cate in Lord DIILNER. It is easy to understand their indignation at a proposal that those who, enjoying the Liberal measure of self-government granted in Cape Colony, turned against us, should suffer no privation for their rebellion. A few of the Cape Loyalists have benefitted</p>
        <p>financially from the war. S)MO of the traders have reaped small fortunes from the business that it has brought in its train. Bat most of thorn have been badly hit by the dis- turbance. They have been put to all sorts of in- conveniences by martial law,which, of course,could make no distinction of persons. Many of them have forsaken home and business to fight for the Empire, and some are mourning relatives. They would be more than human if they did not bit- terly resent the idea that people who contributed largely to the misery and mischief without:any grievance should be freely pardoned, and allowed to come back as if nothing had occurred, The loyalists at the Cape are not in a position to look at the matter so dispassionately as we do here, or to credit that a large-hearted action in this glair would bring its owa reward. If the public at home ponder on the Opposing argarnsnts they will realieo wily it is that the G)verntnent is unable to report ptogress and that the Cabinet finds it necessary to meet almost every day. It may be that tacs will bring about a happy compromise, and the offices of Lard KITCHENER, who is greatly respected by the Boers, should be very helpful in this direction. Lord MILNER has never been coo- ciliatory, but if any scheme can be announced with his concurrence as well as that of Lord KITCHENER it will at any rate satisfy our irritated and suscep- tible fellow countrymen. For the present there is</p>
        <p>need of patience.</p>
        <p>WITH the help of the lambed. baser Tories and of those political hybrids who still</p>
        <p>.call themselves Liberal.Unioniste, and whose sole function is to play the jackal to their bi;ger and more honest allies, Mr BALFOUR is slowly but surely proceeding on his foolish and mischievous task of destroying the character oF the House of</p>
        <p>Commons. Step by step the subordination of the souse to the Cabinet proceeds; step by step is it being reduced to a mere machine for registering the decision of the Government of the day. Full and free discussion even of the Eitimates and</p>
        <p>Supply is being filched away, and Mr BALFOUR would like to take "every shred of independence' from the House of Commons just as his uncle would from the Boers. The recent changes will, as ROBERT REID said, "sat an almost final seal on the humiliation of private members," and similar complaints as to the effect of Mr BALFOUR'S proposals are made by Tories like Mr. Citirrmi. When the work is completed the country will cry " Ichabod," but it will not bestir itself to prevent the mischief being done. Another generation will find that the boasted power of self- government through the,representatives of the People is only a matter of ancient history,and that the great inquest of the nation" is a solemn farce. Oh for the spirit of the men of 1621, whose resolu- tion should ba writ large over the Speaker's chair to-day, and the acceptance of which should form the first and supreme test of the fitness. of a man for parliament! "That the liberties, franchises, privileges, and jurisdictions of Parliament are the ancient and undoubted birthright and inheritance of the sub- jects of England; and that the arduous and urgent affairs concerning the KING, State, and defence of the Realm, and of the Church of England, and the Snaking and maintenance of laws, and redress of grievances, which daily happen within the Realm, are proper subjects and matter of Council and de- bate in Parliament. And that in the handling and proceeding of these businepses every member of the House bath, and of right ought to have, freedom of speech to propound, treat, reason, and bring to conclusion the same."</p>
        <p>The tide of popular indignation is still rising against the Education Bill. The closer it is studied, the clearer do its sinister features become revealed, and the country will saon learn that it ls really an impudent and iniquitous scheme for sacrificing the educational interests of the children of the people to the greed and domination of a Oct. The adverse criticisms are by no means con• fined to Nonconformists who see the danger that t. hreatens the principle of religious equality. It is quite as strong from Educationists, who realise hac the progress made since 1870 —substantial, if slow progress—towards English elementary edu- cation something like that of America and the "ontinent, will be checked by this meas•ire. 'Such experts as Sir Joshua Fitch and Mr E.</p>
        <p>Brodie, who have had long practical experience of what the School Boards have done, emphatically protest against its proposals for destroying or weakening their work; and it is an open secret that the bast Minister of Education that ever ruled at Whitehall, Mr Acland, is ranged on the side of its active opponents. It is not long sinca the Duke of Devonshire appealed to the country for a breez3 to enable him to start his educational measure of reform on its voyage. There is, we re- joice to know, every prospect of such a gale and storm of public indignation that the pirate craft he has launched will have a good chance of being wrecked before it has gone far. If when it founders it should take with it not only the crew from the Education Board, but too self-appointed pilot, Mr Balfour, and the whole Government, the cause of public education will gain, and the country as a whole will not lose thereby.</p>
        <p>The arrangements which are being made to carry out the King's truly royal intention of feast- ing half-a-million of the London poor to mark his Coronation bear in some of the minutest details the mark of his Majesty's personal thought and care. Nothing important has been settled without those who are now responsible for the event taking the King's opinion—not as a mere matter of form, but because his Mijesty has clearly thought the thing out in all its difficulties, and has in it more than the interest of one who con- ceives a large idea and is willing to leave its execution entirely to others. On his wish it has been decided that the dinners should be held simultaneously throughout London on the Saturday following the Coronation; and it is also a settled point that the meal is to be served in public buildings. The wisdom of both these decisions is recognised on reflection, and they help to remove any lingering fear lest the organisation possibly should not be equal to the emergency,and a catastrophe take place comparable to that at Moscow when the Czar was crowned. It is the King's wish also that too strict an investigation should not take place into the character of the people entertained, so long as the openly bad and dissolute are excluded. There is to be no raking up of small delinquencies dozens of years old, and no distinction drawn between the poor who are and those who are not in receipt of relief. Lastly, it appears that the King's generous gift has been increased by a considerable sum for the provision of the cups to be retained by the guests as mementoes of the event.</p>
        <p>, One does not need to be a great inventor, or oven a scientist, to see the force of the logic in the remark which Mr Edison is alleged to have made to M. Santos-Dumont in New York on Sunday. "When the problem of aerial navigation is solved," the electrician is said to have observed, "there will be no gas balloon connected with the flying machine"; and he added that M. Santos-Dumont must therefore work systematically to reduce the size of his balloon until inspection of his flying- machine with a microscope would not reveal the balloon bag. That this view is shared ny other great inventors than Mr. Edison is shown by the offer which Sir Hiram Maxim has just made to present £50,000 to the inventor of a flying-machine which will require no assistance from a balloon. And those who have seen M. Santos-Damont's machine must certainly agree that he has a long way to go before he can achieve the triumph which he has promised the world shall witness in ten years' time—viz., the navigation of the air space between Europe and America in a time from port to port which shall pill. the fastest German liners out of court. But it appears to be a ques- tion whether this consensus of opinion against the balloon as an assistant to aerial navigation does not reduce the old problem once again to the position it occupied for hundreds of years before 'M. Santos-Durnont appeared to startle the world. What the Brazilian aeronaut has not found, and what has still, therefore, to be sought,is the means whereby the flying-machine can be lifted and propelled by its own energy, and not taken up by a lighter fluid. Until such a means is fo Ind the pr..)blem can be said to have advanceA but little, and Sir Hiram Maxim's money and the popularity of the German liLers are safe.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOMENT. --- A Provincial Letter.</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT. --- A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>There have been two au-absorbing subjects in which every part of the country is equally interes- ted during the week—the Budget and the Peace negotiations. Evidence accumulates that the move- ment in favour of peue shows a serious attempt on the part of the Broer leaders to come to an understanding with Lord Kitchener, and the know- ledge of the tact will of itself bring comfort to the hearts of heads of !arailies in all parts of the pro- vinces, ho a way that has not happened before for the past throe year 4. Some time may yet elapse before anything definite is known of the course and character of the Beg rtiations. But, all the same, there is real consolation in knowing that toe Boers have been displaNing a more " coming on " disposi- ::ion—or it should perhaps be said a more coming in disposition—than could have been expected from the Boers in Europe. If their demands had been absolutely impossible the negotiations would have been abruptly terminated, and it is something to the good that men who stoutly declared a few weeks ago that they would not negotiate with Lord Milner have been doing so.</p>
        <p>With regard to the Budget proposals. some of the most contentious come home to this provinces especially and very directly. It is very well for the " Standard" t 3 say that the new import, in the shape of a registration duty on corn and grain is only about an eighth of a penny on the quartern loaf—it is a trifle more than that—and that no one in his senses can suppose that this will affect the price of bread. As a matter of fact, and of experi- ence, it has been shown that the bakers are men who are very much in their senses, and it was announced on Tuesday that London bakers had decided to raise the prico of bread by a penny. What the London bakers did on Tuesday provincial bakers may have done by the time these lines appear. Now, as in urban households the art of bread-baking is practically extinct, the consumers will have to bear a charge that will be a serious matter in many a poor man's family. It is per- fectly true that a rise of one-eighth of a penny accepting such estimate for the nonce as correct, I ought not to cause an increase of a penny, or even of a halfpenny, in price; but the bakers have now I been given an excuse which the public can under- stand, and although competition may bring- the price down again, every consumer who has to pay more far his bread will know that he has to thank the Budget for the distasteful experience. Into some of the other points it is unnecessary to enter hers,</p>
        <p>Parish Councils may do much in their several spheres to render the Coronation celebrations memorable in the annals of the country. Tneir power to spend public money, however, has been somewhat limited, and it will have come to the members of most of them as- a pleasant surprise that Mr Walter Long has decided to include such Councils in the Order about to be issued sanctioning the meeting of the expenses of local celebrations out of the rate 4. Thus Perish Councils all over the country will be able to give the day of :,unday school children a treat, hold sports, or light beacons and bonfires, or to do anything within reason to celebrate the crowning of King E iward and his (been. This is a decided advantage, though it is hoped and expected that private liberality will in all places supplement the rates.</p>
        <p>Alien immigration is. said to be unusually brisk this ycar. Ti sis a subject which has been much discussed in the English newspapers of late, lind as a result of the C )mmission which has been formed —or possibly it is only a coincidence—there appears to have been a stir in the direction which may be considered the least desirable Anyhow, the average British workman will not feel particularly pleased to hear that more than eighteen thousand foreigners arrived on our shores from the Clatinent last month, as compared with fewer t,nan fifteen thousand in March, 1901 ; while in the three months the arrivals numbered about thirty-five thousand, as against nearly tnirty thousand last year. Two—thirds of these totals are men, women, and cdildred who COtliEf here en route for America and elsewhere. A large number of to other lands,do those not stated to be jourreying as a matter of fact, leave the country, but the re- siduum is added to tbe already:excestive number of destitute foreigners in uur midst.</p>
        <p>. -</p>
        <p>By a very narrow majority in the Council,Cardiff has decided that there are to be no Sunday trams in its streets. Cardiff is a great and growing and enterprising town, but the ideas of members of its Council are sometimes as parochial and unprogres- sive as they well can be. The convenience of the Sunday trams is provided at the public expense and sanctioned by the law of the laud, and practically, as matters stand, the decision just arrived at means making a present of all the Sunday trams to the local 'nue company. As this com- pany have a monopoly they have al- ready increased their rates, and no one can verb well blame them for doing so. The qaestion Is one that has been raised in ether cities and towns, but the public have hitherto insisted upon their right in the matter,and, in the city of Bristol, for instance, an arrangement has been arrived at to run only in the afternoon and early evening of Sundays. It has yet -to be seen wnether Cardiff will adhere to the decision now arrived at,by seven- ten as against sixteen votes, but not long since an effort was made to seek for legal power to make spitting in the streets a penal offence, and as far as one can recollect, proved abortive. Everybody feels now that something should be done to sup- press,as far as possdole,the:disgusting and dangerous practice,from a health point of view,but everybody would not think of going to,the,length proposed in the Cardiff Council,</p>
        <p>Reference in the above to a matter affecting the public health reminds one of the decision of l%lessrs Elder, Dampster,aud C0.,0f Liverpool to import into this country ten thousand mongooses—why not " mongesse" or" mesgeesi. ? "—which is a response to the call voiced by many medical officers ot health in the provinces that an exterminating cru6ad3 should be waged against rats with a view to prevent the introdution of bubonic plague, and to arrest the dissemination of the morbific germs should the disease untortun- ately find a lodgment in the country. Lord Cromer's report, on the progress of Egypt,issued on Saturday, contains a passage in which it is pointed out that the plague is proving vexatiously tenacious in the valley of the Nile,to whico the mongoose is indigen- ous, In this country the climate and other influ- ences are not altogether favourable to the propaga- tion of the disease, though the experience of Glas- gow eighteen months ago did nos quite bear out such conclusion. the enforcement, of pre- cautionary measures is to be encouraged, and the great thing is to reduce the number of rats, sviich are the living agents of dissemination. The mon- goose is the natural enemy of the rat,and where the " Herpesces Griseas" thrifei the rat has little chance of life. When imported into Jamaica the mongoose speedily cleared the cane plantations of rats, but it likewise played great havoc with the poultry, and attacked and killed wholesale other harmless animals that are necessary to maintain the balance of nature. The question i., therefore, with some people, whether the introduction of the mongoose into this country may conceivably prove a disease worse than the evil it i. meant to cure.</p>
      </div>
