      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <p>E EXA MIN ER FRI. ~‘ APRIL 18, 1902.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>NOTES BY IHE NAY AT EARLESTOWN,</head>
        <p>NOTES BY IHE NAY AT EARLESTOWN,</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>The police are already fairly at work in their efforts to fulfil the promise they have made to pre- sent the District Council with a horse am oulauce. They have received a substantial amount in dona- tions and they expect to increase this by the pro- jected football match which is to take place on the 20th inst, between the police and tradesmen. It must not be supposed that they are depending upon the money they will take for admission to the fi-ld, for it is pretty certain a good many people will not trouble to see the match. To provide against this they have had a large number of tickets printed, which they are disposing of to all and sundry, so that it is immaterial to the police whether the ticket purchasers turn up or not. No doubt the effort is a good one, and the intentions of the police are praiseworthy. but as to the need for a horse ambulance is a question around which there is much debate. Probably when we get our own hospital the advantages of a horse ambulance .will be more pronoun ced.</p>
        <p>The County Coroner end' the jury empanelled on Saturday had a very delicate task set them; and one which needed the most careful handling, when they had to inquire into the circumstances sur- rounding the death of a scholar of the Earlestown Wesleyan school. 'Coroner Brighouse is notA tie his urbanity and geniality on occasion, as also for his unbending severity when the exigencies of the case necessitate severity. The parents of botn boys concerned could not complain of his attitude on Saturday, and the verdict of the jury was the only one which could be arrived at under the circum- stances. Teachers of schools have before them in this case a strong object 1e3500, and they should lose no opportunity of impressing upon the scholars under their charge the danger which exists in pro- miscuous kicking,and of treating with severity any case which comes under their notice, as having occurred during school hours or within the confines of the school premises</p>
        <p>The District Council are very dilatory in regard to the suggested Coronation celebrations 'there was a meeting of representatives of Friendly Societies the other night, to discuss what action they were prepared to take, but in consequence of their want of knowledge as to the intentions of the District Council, their deliberations were practically of no avail. It was understood however, that the Societies were prepared to carry out a programme somewhat on the lines of that which was enacted at the jubilee, but they are anxious to have some indication of the intentions of the Council, as also are the general public.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>SAD DEATH OF A SCHOOL BOY</head>
        <p>THE SAD DEATH OF A SCHOOL BOY</p>
        <p>Al EARLESTOWN•</p>
        <p>A Fatal Kick.</p>
        <p>rt S Brighouse, Coroner, held an inquest at the Town Eirlestown, on Saturday, on tbe body of the boy William Ashton Cave, seven years c.f age, who died on the previous Thursday from injuries inflicted by another boy named John Cutbber; Atkinson, of Bridge-street, Earlestown. The fathers of both boys wore present in the room during the inquiry. Margaret Cave said she was the wife of William Cave, a wagon maker, living at 68 Bing-street, Earlestown. The dedeased was her son and was seven years old last birthday. He came home trom school at dinner time on Tuesday. but made no complaint. He returded re school after dinner, and about half past four in the afternoon she found him in lEing,-street leaning against some railings, crying, and oa questioning him he said that fj John Atkinson had kicked him in the s'admach, and th4t it ached. Dr Gauge was called in about 630, and he attended him until his death at 11 o'clock on Thuisday morning. The deceased boy told him that hr had been throwing at Baskerville's boy, and John Atkinson kicked him for it. John Cuthbert Atkinson, a sharp-looking lad, eight years of age, oaid he attended the Wesleyan School in Crose-lane, and Willie Cave went to the same school. On Tuesday he kicked Willie Cave for throwing stones at Joe Baskerville. He kicked him behind and he cried. The Coroner said he felt very sorry for Mr Cave and he had no doubt the jury were sorry for him, but they must recogniza that Lila.) was a worldly knowledge,that boys wee boys. It was an unfortunate ccdurnecr. Dr Gauge said he was called in to see the deceased boy on Tuesday evening and found him suffering from a large swelling in the right groin, what was called a blood swelling. It was such as would be produced by a kick and it would not requiro much violeace to set it up especially in the region where the boy was kicked. The deceased was under his care to the time of his death. On Wednesday night there were symptoms of internal toe morrhage and he found him in a state of collapse. A very small amount of violence on that particular part of the body would set up what was found. The immediate cause of death was collapse and internal heemorthage arising from the injury receive!. The Coroner addressing the jury thought they would agree with him that there could not be any wilful inten- tionwon the part of the lad Atkinson. They must look at the matter not only from a legal standpoint, but's, com- monsense standpoint. He would not have any intention to cause the death of one of his school mates.iff he had kicked him on any other part of the body, no isjury would have resulted. Children of,tender years were not supposed to have any criminal responsibility unless it could be proved that the boy Was of a bad and wicked ' nature. The jury after a short deliberation, returned a verdict that de/eased had mot his death by misadventure, and there was no intent to do grievous bodily harm. They sympathised with both families, as they considered it was a very sad case. Mr Atkinson said he was much obliged to the 'jury for their sympathy. TT.3 occurrence had,been very dig- tressing to both families.</p>
        <p>ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH -CLASS MINIM tiL WATHRS.—Advt PEOPLE WILL TALK about the good value they get in ALF. TAYLOR'S GOOD BOO.—Advt,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>THE REV W H. STABLES.</p>
        <p>The Rev W stables, was publicly collated on Sunday to the old Parish Church of Over by the Bishop of Chester. The Archdeacon of Chester, afterwards took the induction service. The Bishop preached from the text " What mean ye by this servos ?" and Canon Maitland Wood (the Bishop's examining chaplain), and the aural Dean (the Rev W Binney) read the lessoprs. Mr Stable 3 has been senior curate of Runcorn Parish Church for eight year_,s with special charge of Weston. BRUNSWICK WRSLRYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The interim foreign missionary meeting was held at Brunswick School on Sunday afternoon, under the presidency of Mr William Unsworth. There was a very god attendance of members. rhe Rev li Morrison gave a very interesting address of missionary work in Ceylon Mr Edward liniveton, one of the secretaries, reported that the total sum received from boxes and collections during the half-year, including the proceeds of the present meeting, amounted to £4O le 51d, a very slight decrease on the amount raised during the corres- ponding half of the previous year. RUNCORN AND WIDNES TEACHERS' ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday evening a general meering of the above association was held at the Victoria-road School, Runcorn under the presidency of Miss Clayden. There was a representative attendance. Mies Barry of St Edward's Schools, Mr Walton of the Higher txrade School, Widnes and Miss Baker of the National Infant School were rerreived as new members.—Mr Moulton reported that on behalf of the Benevolent Orphan association the sum, of £5O had been banded in by Miss Clay len at the conference at Bristol from the local association. A report was also read from the delegates who attended the recent conference held at Bristol. A lantern lecture was afterwards given, entitled "Round Bristol." delivered by Ntr T Showan. A splendid prozramrne of music was also submittei. PING•YONG TOURNAMENT AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On nrSci-ilWe;;ning, with the object of benefit- ing Miss Hankinson's Girls' Club, a ping-pong tournament was held in the Primitive Methodist Schoolroom, Oreenway-road. There was a good arteudance, and the contests excited much interest. The winner.; of the events were :—Ladies' corn- petition: 1, Miss Annie Warner ; 2, Miss Winnie Littler. This was a most exciting event. After scoring 19 points all, the best of five points was played, Miss Warner winning the odd point, and securing first place by 22 to 21 points. Gentlemen's event: 1, Rev C P Bashfortn; 2, Mr C Cunningham. This was also a very even game,and well contested. Mrs McLennan kindly distributed Vie prizes, and Miss B Johnson acted as scorer, with the Rev W tocholes as referee. SONS OF TXMEMILANCE, KUNOMN. On Saturday evening the usual meeting was held in the Greenway-road School, under the presidency of Mr Thomas Clarke. After the routine business had been disposed of Mr Thomas J Holt referred to the position occupied by the S ins of Temperance Order, and showed by comparing the mortality re- turns and te sick rata of ordinary friendly societies, that the temperance organization bold an improved position. , Mr George Powell, one of the auditors, gave his quarterly retort. He stated that tne cadets now numb-red 113. There had bsen paid in sick benefits during the quarter £4 9s 61. Itt the adult order the, e were 316 members, an increase of three on the quarter; 34 sick members had received £.52 48 41. Tne reports were unanimously adopted, cia the motion of Mr Thomas lieesoco, ssrcoaded by Mr Thomas Dutton. The following officers and members took part in the meeting :—The Chairman and Messrs Thomas R Whyte, W 13isbrown, John Cooke, James Johnson and Thomas J Holt, Two young ladies were almitted to membership of the order.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>INTERESTING PRESENT/. TION.</head>
        <p>INTERESTING PRESENT/. TION.</p>
        <p>An interestin,z p-resent ation took place on Thurs- day evening at the Mariners' Mis.:..ion room irwell- lane, when most of the officials connected with Holy Trinity schools met together to present to Mr H Turner a dinner service on the occasion of his marriage. The company included the Rev C R Killick (vicar), Rev N J Poole (curate),Mr J Woods, (superintendent of the boys' school), Mis Fisher (superintendent of the girls' school). Mr A Hulse (secretary), and nearly all the teaching staff. The presentation was preceded by a tea,the preparations for which were kindly undertaken by Miss Fisher. The Vicar, who presided at the subsequent gather- ing, made the presentation at the invitation of Mr Woods, and wished Mr and Mrs Turner a long and happy married life. Mr Turner feelingly acknow- ledged the kindness of heart which had prompted the gift, and said he should Nalue it very highly. He was born and reared in Holy Trinity parish,and he hoped to end his days in it, The Rev NJ Poole also joined in the gencral expression of good wishes ' and expressed the hope that t',ey had gained another worker in the parish in the person of Mrs Turner. The remainder of the evening was spent in harmony, some pleasing vocal items being con- tributed by the Rev N J Poole, Mr H Turner, and Misses A Turner, E Turner and F Anne' t.</p>
        <p>HALTON WESLEYAN METHODIST TEMPERANCE</p>
        <p>t;GCIETY.</p>
        <p>An hour of temperance song, recitation, and speech was given in the schoolroom on Thursday night by Mr henry Taylor, Church of England Temperance Society's Police Court Missionary at Warrington Mr J B Clarke, who was announced to take the chair, was absent :through illness, and his place was taken by Mr James Looker The Chairman said he had read a gees t deal in the local press about their visitor's work, and was glad to meet him and give him a hearty welcome In a speech full of humorous anec- dotes and racy Yorkshire wit. Mr Taylor denounced drink as being bad morally, physically, socially and financially 'and advocated out and out teetotalism. He assured his hearers that if they wished to be respected by otters they must respect themselves, and to respect themselves they must leave drink alone. Abstainers he urged to work to the best of their abi!ity in their own way and sphere, and declared that in spite of the boasted wealth of the drink trade, temperance would win for God and Truth were on its side. The following programme was rendered in an unusually clever manner: Songs "Working for me," " Keep your houses clean,"" Leaving it off by degrees." Recita- tions "A death in the bowie,' "The tarmer's fright," " How Jane Conquest rang the bell." The audience was very large and freely showed its ap- preciation of the entertainer's efforts. Mr Tom Goutien accompanied A collection was taken on behalf of the llalton Society. On the motion of Mr S Barrow seconded by Mr 0 Baguley, a very hearty vote of thanks was given to Mr Taylor, who pro- mised to come again next session to give a lantern address on his Police Court and Prison Gate Mission work</p>
        <p>SPRING MILLINRRY. _ Dresses, Sill( e, tsiouga_</p>
        <p>Jac.?..e7s-,—lcdan7lW,-63;;;;7' Lace Collars, Tie's, &amp;c. New Cboice and inexpenelve.—W. T. WILLIAMS, Sankey- street, Warrington.—Advt. " LNA-FRANUIS" CYCLNS.—Britain s acknowledged best. Sole Agent, GARLAND, Bridge.st, Warrington. CoRONA.TION MEDALS.—WM. BESWICR, 21, Butter- market-street. Samples to ba seen.—Advt.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S CREAM C It!A CNE</p>
        <p>CRISP</p>
        <p>AND CREAMY</p>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the above Council was held on Wednesday morning at the Town Hall. Mr R H Poe- nett occupied the chair and there were also present Weirs D Bisbrown, M Sweetman, J H &amp;Likeld, T Dan, J J Shaw, E Imison, N Lightburn, 8 Walton, E Gatley, G H Kirkham, J Wilson, F Wiswall, E Mareiall (secretary), F A Lake (law clerk), J Wilding (surveyor). MR. SAMUEL TAYLOR, .T.P , APPOINTED CHAIRMAN FOR</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>FOURTH TIME.</head>
        <p>THE FOURTH TIME.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said he had pleasure in proposing that Mr Samuel Taylor take the chair of the Council for the ensuing twelve months. He was sure it would ba a matter of regret to all that he was not able to be with them owing to his ill health. He had seen Mr Taylor yesterday and found him suffering from a severe cold which rrqaired nursing. He spa+ quite sure that if they elected Mr Taylor chairman for the next twelve months they would have a mars in the chair whom the town would in every way join in delighting to honour, and who, in the trying circumstances of the neat twelve months would show nein a pattern of a loyal and patriotic citivn. Mr Ws wall in secondintr remarked that it was the feeling of every one that MC Ylvlor should be chairman for the next twelve months. He had always done credit to himself and the town when filling the pasiton in previous years. The proposition was unanimously carried. Mr Lightburn said that claiming the questionable privilege of being senior member, he, on behalf of Mr Taylor, was quite sure heliwould be pleased with the additional proof of their appreciation If there was one thing more than another which strengthened the hands of a public man it was the knowledge that he had the good will, the support and the respect of his fellow townsmen. Nothing he could say in the form of praising Mr Taylor could prove his spechil fitness for the post became by the previous speakers it had already been done. Ho would, ho was certain, have the unanimous support of the meeting in proposing a vote of thanks to their late chairman for his services during the past yet r. He was a very modest gentleman and anything said in his favour would make him blush and be did not want to put him in that posi- tion. (Laughter.) He a'so had pleasure in proposing that Mr Posnett be vice-cliairman fot the next twelve months.</p>
        <p>Mr Doan a.conded. Dia Council bld always appointed as Vice-chairman its previous Chairman. It was a custom that had always worked well and they could ro'. do better than follow the usual course. The resoluticn was carried.</p>
        <p>The Chairman thanked them for tbeir kind remarks, and for the appointment. With regard to th future he would do RH he could for the town,not as a dutv but out ot love and good will. No'thing would be wr.nting on his part to support Mr Taylor in the chair.</p>
        <p>THE SENtR MEMBER FOR MERSEY wARD.</p>
        <p>Mr W,swall extended a hearty welcome to the new Councillor Mr Shaw. H., hoped he would have pleasure and health in his duties and every success and honour pertaining to them. He thnught Mr Shaw would agree with him that the senior mamba. for Mersey Ward should be Mr Jones and that he should be elected for the three years. If in twelve mo Iths time Mr Shaw' candidature was contested he ward pledge nimself to work to! him. He moved that Mr Jones be the senior ar,mber.</p>
        <p>Mr Shaw said he wool I like to second Mr WkwaTs proposition. He WAS prepared to move that Mr Jones should *le electod sminr member and take the seat for three years. He was strongly of opinion,that it wag the proper course to adopt and was ploascd to second tha revolution.</p>
        <p>The proposition wig carrieri. THE HEALTH COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>• On the proposition of M.,ssre Wilgon and Walton it was resolved that the Health Committee consist of the whole Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>FINANCE COHMITTEE</head>
        <p>THE FINANCE COHMITTEE</p>
        <p>Ou the motion ot th Cnairman ti followjng gentle- men were elected mernhers of the Finance Committee : Messrs Hewitt, Jones, Kirkham, Liahthurn and Walton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>WATER COMMITTEE,</head>
        <p>THE WATER COMMITTEE,</p>
        <p>. --- Oa the merlon of Messrs ImHon aad Walton, the fol- lowinz ,entlemen were elected t) form the Water Cernmittee :—M:tars Pritchatd, Suaw, amn, Gatley, Taylor, Wilson and Wiswall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>THE GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Mr Damn prvosed that the Parliamenory aryl Glneral Purposes C unmit-,ee consist of Messrs Lirlithurn,lmis.)n, Bishrown, Jones, Pritchard, Wilsln and Wiswalf.</p>
        <p>Mr Sifield seconded. and thn motinn was passed. THE LIBEA.RY COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>It was resolved crt che motion of Mr Bisbr tarn that Messrs Cunnicgham, Gr, Haukinson, Hatton, Imiso Kirkham, Momton, A R Nirman, Posnett, Ri!ey, greet- man and the Rev Canon Wood form the L.brary CJITI• mittee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>MARKET AND CEMETERY COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>THE MARKET AND CEMETERY COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>01 the propoorirm of Mr Wiswail it wai re•olve.ithrit thl Marken and Gmletery Committee consist of iNtsi•fi Walton, Galley, Bisbrown, Snaw, Eiewitt, Sa!ke!cl and Sweetmar.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>AR &amp; LARGE OR SMALL COMMITTEES TIM REST</head>
        <p>AR &amp; LARGE OR SMALL COMMITTEES TIM REST</p>
        <p>Mr Wiswall proposed that Lae Technical Listructim Committee consist cf the whole Council with Sir J T Brunner,M.P.,Rev J Coarnters,Messrs W J Cunningham, J MlPrIto.W liankinson,J Holt,J R.rpfild,F W Hatton, J Lictler,W M,vtoo,A R Norman. F J N mLan,S Pope, R Rawlinsoo, T Rile 7, Sayle, and J Turnbull. Re said that helhad always believed that a email committee was more suitable than the preent large one but seeing that if the New Education Act became law tLe committee would be entirely altered if not actually done away with and another appointed, he thought it wiser to rc-elect the old committee. The act stipulated that Urban Councils under a population of 20..000 wcrl not to have powers to deal with education. 'The County Cou,mil would be the predominant authority and would appoint a com- mittee to deal with the question of education in the town. Seeing that the town was second to none in the country with respect to education, elementary, secondary and technical, ha thought they ought, wi:U other authorities, to petition Parliament.</p>
        <p>tilr Imison am sorry !to interpose, but I rise to a point of order. I submit that any review of Parlia- mentary proposals is not pertinent to the matter v. hich is before the Council, which is the comuosVion of the Tchnicl Instruction Committee. and I venture to ask the Cha'rman's ruling on this point. The Chairman I take it that Mr Wiswall ia arg ainr, in favour of the standing of this Comm tt.l and I should be sorry to interrupt him. Mr Imison : I should Ilk, you then t in one step farther and take the advice of the LAW Clerk on the point. I think lam entitled to pat it that way, because it is leading into the discussion of questions that I want toßrevent..</p>
        <p>- The Chairman : Mr Lake tells m. that he cannot give a decision on the question at al!. It soeins to me that nvone can a rzue as he likes on the question of the ommittee in making the proppsition. Mr Wiscrall's argument is an argument in favour of letting it remain as it is so that it?may die in peace I take it. (Liugeter.) I rule that Mr Wiswall proceed. .</p>
        <p>Mr Wiswall sa'd that when he was interruWed he was saying that they should petition Parliament to alter that part of the Edivion Act that srioulated Chat Urban Districts under 20,000 could not In the educational authorirr an I that a district council with a population of 15,000 should be albwed to take (-N'r whatever powers Par:iament might grant to any education com- mittee. He made those remorlts with a view to strengthening the proposal for the c ommittee to remain as it was so that the Council and the outsiders together could fairly consider the quest;on. If it were dealt win bv a small committee it would have to come before the Council. He moved,tbereforc,that the committee stand as it was. Mr Dean seconded. Mr Bisbrown moved that Mr J F Moulton be added to the Technical Instruction Committee. Being a school- master it would be a decided augmentation to the com- mittee. The Northwich Technical Instruction Com- mittee had two teachers on it. The prejudice against having teachers on the committee was a toolish one. Mr Dean seconded. The Secretary, at this point, read a letter from the Runcorn and Widnes Teachers' Association in which they deprecated the action of the Council in objecting to the presence of teachers on tie co.nmittee, at the last committee meeting. Toe letter also pointed out that it was the custom jo the majority of towns to per- mit teachers to sit on the committee and that consider- able benefit would be derived from having on the committee persons whose lives were devoted to educational pursuits.</p>
        <p>Mr Imison. rose to-support Mr Wiswall's proposition. lie was not in favour of the idea of the Cot.iicil petition- ing Parliament, and when Mr Wiswall argued in this way he was giving the best possible reason why such a petition should bo discredited. If the prayer cf the teachers were granted they would have persons acting on the Committee as masters and servants, which was an anoms!ous position which, he was sure, the Council w(re not prepared to bring into operstion. At present he considered it premature to discuss the question raised by Mr Wiswall. ' On the amendment being put six voted for, and six against, the Chairman giving the casting vote against the amendment.</p>
        <p>Mr Wilson then proposed aq en amendment that the Technical Instruction Committee be composed of the following gentlemen : Sir J Brunner, Rev Canon Wood, Messrs Posnett, Handley, Taylorjrnison,Wiswan, Lightburn, Shaw, Kirkham, Wilson, Cunningbam. ilankinson, Hatton. F J Norman, Pope, Turnbull and Pritb. He said they could not tell whether the education UR would became law this year or for the noxt five yeast. Ttey had to look at the Instruction Committee from the present point of view and from present day affairs. The work of the Council was done better by small committees. Seven members could conduct a council committee meeting of the utmost importance, and yet it took 38 to 40 members to conduct the business of the Technical Instruction Committee Last year they had the utmcst difficulty in carrying out the business of the committee. They had so few rnern- hers at che meetings that it was with difficulty they got along with the business. He would like to see an active</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>committee formed that would look after the best inter- ests of the town and make it a success. The Chairman seconded the amendment. Those men as outsiders and members of the Council who wore re- maining on the committee and were not attending any of the meetings were acting in the worst interests of the town. Seven or eight of the men on the committee shoul retire. The committee was in danger of being swamped with drones. After further discussion theameniment was put when only the proposer and seconder voted for it. The original proposition being put was csrri(d. REPRESENTATIVES ON THE BET:INNER GUILD HALL COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Sr J T Brunner,M.P., Messrs Kirkham and Sweetman were elected to represent the Counca on the Brunner Guild Hall Management Committce.</p>
        <p>This conclud,A- the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>ST HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST HELENS.</p>
        <p>CHILD DROWNED NEAR ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Mr Coroner Brighiu,e held an lay lest ou thursday afternoon, at the Sor Inn, Billinge, near St. fielens„ relative to tla3 dean of a child nanald William 1 homas Waterworth, two years old, 13,Wigan-road, Wurstanley. Oa Wednesday afternoon, alt-'r the deceased and his twin sister had been playing, his bodv was found in the Island Dam. The dam is about 200 tards from de- ceasea'a haus ~ A verdict of " Accidental death " was returned. THE Sr. HELENS " HIGH Rip" GANG —HEAVY SENTENCES ON VIOLENT CHARACTERS.</p>
        <p>Three aye:l-known characters were brought before the St. 113 lens magistrates yesterday, each on charges of drunkenness,assault,and freTienting. Toe accused we,e Edward Leahey,of Canal Bails; Peter Clarke,of 17,Canal Bank;and Frank Riley,of Back College-street.—Constable Littler said that at halt-past eleven the previous nigh', he was in Warrington-road, and he met two nraa runtiv. They were both bleeding, aud had been severely •ssaix- ted. One roan had had hiA necktie and a gold pin, value stolen. Liter sent the mon back to find their assailacte, and when tbey got to the Raven Bride tlie three prisoners set on to them. Littler follawed close up behind, and when the prisoners saw him they ran away. He caught Leahey. He found a small niece at the tie outside the urinal.—George Davies,of Ellbess-lane, said he was at the Palace on Monday, arid afterwards had a walk round the town with a man who said he was a pawnbroker. When they go: to the Raven Bridge they were followed into tta• urtuel. Leaney grasped the other man's watch guird and pulled his watch out, wheieapon Dv e cau,mt him by the throat and made him leave it. Two ether men then dropoed on to him and got him down in the urinal. They klcked him and jumped on him mist cruelly. One of them said, " He is Welsh. Lt's throw him into the canal."—Police- ccnstable Smith said that Clarke and Riley admitted being with Leahey.—Srgeant Sisson said that Davies was spirting blood for two houri after he came to the Town Hall. Toe prisoners were pert of a " High Rip'' gang.—Lethey was sentenced to ton in hard labour for the different offerinea, Clarke ta six months, and Riley t ) three.—John Dillon, a young man, ada) ot Canal Bank East, was gent to gaol for a month for assaulting Police-canstable Crockett earlier on Monday evening.</p>
        <p>MARRIAGE OF MR LIONEL PILKINGTON.</p>
        <p>The marriage of Mr Lionel l'iljrington, son Of Colonel Filkington, M l', of ltainford Elsel, St EL.lens,Lsocas'aire, to Nl'Se Evelyn Beckett, only daughter of Mr and Mrs T Beckett, was seletnnized on Wednesday at S 5 Peter's Church, Eaton-square, London. Among tne invited guests were Lord and Lady Stratrocona, Sir John and Lsdy Farley, the Hon Elward and Mrs Blake, sir Divid Gamble, Mr and Mrs Gilbert Parker, Madame Alban!, General Farrington Cluny and Mrs Macphersen, Sir Fitzroy and Lady Mac'ean, Captain and Mrs D T Browne, Coif-ono! Dudley North, Colonel and Mrs Richard Pilkingtnn, Msj or and Mrs Amyats Hal,. Captain and Mrs Ernest Pilkingtoo, and Mrs Alexand ‘r G 'schen. The br;de, who was given away by her father, wore a dress of white crepe de chine, embroidered in silver, and made with a full C mutt Cain, tulle veil aid wreath •of natural orange blossom and wh,to heather. There were six bridesmaids—Miss Bank:os (cousin of thn bride), the 1‘11,533 Pidriegton (tw o sisters of the bridegroom), :Iliss Robertson, Miss Naomi Browne, and Miss Veda Mat- phersou. They wore spriag coloured costume. trimmed • with pale blue, and lace hats, trimmed with black feathers. E Lch carried a silver basket tilled anti pink ros,s and white heather. Mr Charles Pilkington sup- ported his brother, and the wedding reception was held at the Ha.;s-Crescent Rotel.</p>
        <p>The servico was fully chorA, and the chancel was handsomely decorated with Kiutia palms, banki-d with arum lilies and daffod 11 and foliage &amp;ants. The cere- loony was impressively conducted b tho Rev ENii,se Wilson, M.A., Lshsted by the Rv John S.lorrs. lA., I vicar of Sr. Peter's Church. Miss Beckett carried a bridal bouquet of orange blossoms intermixed. win white heather, tied with white satin screamers. Eirly in the afternoon Mr and Nlrs Lionel Pilkington left for their honeymoon tour. the bride's going-away orn being of fawn crepe do chene tritnm3d with Irish [are, sad black picture hat. The pr-sears numbered over 250. Mr Lionel Pilkingtoo, is a director and manager of the !large colliery firm of M-rs R Evans anl C firydoc c, and in connection with his inarria vs the offi3iais and staff have presented Mr Piikington with a p Lir of silver candlesticks, nree rose bowls, a kilvEr tea sereine, and silver coffee service. Mr and Mrs Pilkington will reside at the Woodlands, Newton- le-Willowe.</p>
        <p>\</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>LATE MR. WILLAIW. MASSY.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. WILLAIW. MASSY.</p>
        <p>At St. Paul's Chapi lon Sanday evening the Rev. Alfred la ells, superintendent mini-ter of the Runcorn circuit, delivered an impressive and learned discourse on " Uokuown work and worker." At tho close of Ids sermon the rev gentleman gave an interesting address on the late William Massey. lie said it was not his pur- pose to attempt to do anything that would appear to glorify the late William Massey. To him it would have been utterly repugnant. He was well known te most of them n and there was no need bat to say very little about him. First of all he was a Methodist and he was a Wsslayan Methodiit. He had received from Mr John Simpson, who for so many years had benso intimately conneatA wits the Runcorn circuit, on the very morning that Mr Massey died, a letter in which beisaid that Mr Missey was a Methodist of the fourth generation. The letter further stated that he had been educated in their day scho-ls and Sunday sehools. He looked upon Methodism as his own home and he was never a wanderer. There he abode and in dun time became associated with the offi- cial life of old Brunswick in more departments than one. In each he served his generation according to the will of , God. Perhaps in nothing Was his love of Methodism I shown more remarkably than his intense love for the class meeting—which ought to he loved more to-day than it was. Ho would never forget the fi,s‘ time be met Mr Massey. It was at the class meeting when he came to ;speak and he (the speaker) turned round and fixed his eyes upon him. He was struck with the very , original way in which he sooke which led him to have I some conversation with him. He than attributed to the class meeting,as he had done ever since, his pres-nt firm believe in Christianity. In business life, too, he was no wanderer. He entered the Office of Messrs Simpson, Davies and Comnany, when a boy and he rose step by step to the responsible pesi- Lion he held at the time of his death. He was greatly valued by the heads of the firm. One who was quite competent to speak had summed up in a few words what one might take as a fair estimate of the man. "He was ihorouehly upright, honest, punctual. Always did what he took in hand with all his might. Always busy never was, ed a rhomeat, willing to help others even to the humble office boy." His great public work was doubtless that which he did in connection with No ithellowship. No one kaew the time and the energy he spent in trying to improve the conditions of the local branches of this great organiz tion. Every office it was possible fir him to hold in connection with the local branches, and even as a dis- trict official,everyone of these he held for about 20 years Be was one of the most trusted officials and one of the. society's trustees. In a letter which came from one of the head officials it was stated that through the whole of that time he discharged the duties without a fault and never fell out once with any members of the ludee. His work in that direction could not be over estimated. He would like to say to the young men that Witliarn Massey was a reader and he was especially a result of those books which touched closely upon the great social ' side of life and the welfare of the deople. The continu- ous studs of these problems brought him into the great- est difficulty of his life, which was when he was brought suddenly face to tare with the blackest diffiml- tat and the:enigMa of existence,the horrible travesty of judgment andj ustice that was manifested in the social circumstances of those around them to-day. He told him that he was then in danger of drifting into agnosticism if not into actual infidelity. It was i only for a time but t was the darkest hour of his lite. He fought the battle out alone and eventually the cloud broke and the old faith came back. The last time the speaker saw him downstairs he said that in a short time ha might be better and be back at his work at the .office or he might he " going home." It was hard he said in one sense for him to go but it was all right. " I have got over that old question. God is juet.and I know in whom I have believed." Perhaps one of the most marked features of his life was his utter un- Reins/mesa. He lived for others. His home life was sweet and was .too sacred to be mentioned. His sun went down while it was yet day, and he displayed a greater heroiem than that which marked anything done lon the battlefield. The last time the Speaker sat by the bedside he said in a touching manner, thinking of a story previ-usly related to aina " You see I have got hold Of My Father's hand." Ile lived the life of the righteous and bodied the death of the righteons. It was a loss to the church, to his society and to the town. The choir rendered the anthem " What are these arrayed in white robes,'</p>
        <p>\ -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>For the fourth time in Mr Samuel Taylor's public career he has been elected chairman of the Runcorn Urban Council. Being coronation year he has thus had conferred upon him an honour which does not come within the purvue of many a lifetime. It is no sickly sycophaucy,but rather a just appreciation of the services he has rendered the town, to assert that a better*airman could not have been found, in view of the approaching festivities within or outside the walls of the Council (.camber. One thing is certain, that as he has adorned tee Ball of Deliberations in the past by his presence at the head of the Council he will embellish it still more in the future. Of his public work which induced tbe city fathers to select him as the man whom the town delighted to honour it is unnecessary to speak. For many years he has nobly borne the burden and heat of the day striving to tusks this town of soap, salt water and chemicals a little more habitable for those who have toe happy fortune to nestlis under its wing. In every public movement which has been for the betterment of the individual and the uplifting of society, Mr Taylor has worthily played his on. Nor has his work been exclusively con- fined to the showy but less substantial veneer of platform oratory. In the routine drudgery of Com- mittee work which no one sees, and which few estimate at its real value, Mr faylor has always unobtrusively done his share. Hi work in connec- tion with local affairs other than the Council is too well known to be reiterated, It is sufficient to say that the different organizations over which he presides, and with which he is 'son_ fleeted, have brought him in touch with almost every typo of the Runcorn Redtail —the minister of toe gospel, the magistrate, the philanthropist, the educationalist, the moralist, the athlete, tho business man and even the man in the street. Secessarily a man who lives in harmony with such powerful yet divergent social forces must be broad minded without heresy. Ctherwise he will be a proper target for th s wrath of the " uucoo guid." At the same time his sympathies must be:extended to all sorts;aad conditions of men- And as it is doubtful whether any man can lay so just a claim to such is:liversity of gifts as Mr Taylor it is theretore most fitting that in the auspiallous year before us—a year which should link, at least morcentartly,—high ano low, rich end poor, strong and weak, togetner,—he should ba at the head of affairs. The inhabitants will unaaimou4y notify the selection ani the hoot) may be expressed that Air Taylor's year of office may be a signal record of achievements in the history of tne town.</p>
        <p>According to custom the retiring chairman Mr Posniiit was elected vice-chairman of the Council, a unanimous vote et thanks being accorded him for his year's service. Mr Posnett, everyone cmi- siders,fis a forrunata man. For the last twelve months he has tilled the seat of egnoar with a dig- nity and a business like precision which is commend- able in FA young a man. He has won his spurs early and were he to resign all his public offices to-morrow he would have already achieved posi- tions in civic life whico are usually filled by men the wrong side of middle age. Darin _ his year of dice an event of more than passing importance made him the cynosure of eyes near and fir. And at present he is unquestionably one of the most plpular men of the town It is to be noted with satisfaction that the financial and soil administration have in no way retro- graded during his presidency. In short he ODA risen suddenly in popular favour by ability, geniality and thoroughness.</p>
        <p>Should a teacher sit on a Technical Education. Committee ? Tula was one of the questions Arnie). the Couccil tried to decide at their annual meeting. Last year they tried to settle the question and failed; this year thev tried and faileu ; next year* they witl have another try and will probabiy But like Bruce's spider they will per tans keep on trying, and after they have made themselves supremely ridiculous by reason of their mental con tortions they will succeed in the course ot the next six or seventyears in answerinz the question. Truly a Town Council is a wonderful thing after all!</p>
        <p>At present the Technical Instruction Committee consists of a conelomeration of individuals many of whom know as much abeut educational require- ments as Topsy knew about the origin of species. It is a matter of notoriety that there ;ire men on the I committee who, tltough thorough experts at their particular calling, possess abiolutely mo•ability to qualify them to sit on aboard to discuss educational Pursuits. Now one would think teat Le introd ace two or three gentlemen whose lives are devo ed to the instruction of the rising youth ; who take the broadest possible views of education; who are not acquainted merely with the theory of learning but with the practical routine of the work; men whose cobs desire is the advancement of knowledge, so that the child shall be thoroughly equipped for lifa's race, would he the very men qualified to have a foremost seat on an educational c.ommittee. Yet tha Council are so bound or, in red tape that for fear of creating the dreadful bogey of a precedent it blinds itself, like the ostrich, to the goo I which would re derived from the acquisition on the committee of two or three exper. s Thus, under the existing conditions, forty members who sound the whole garment of trades to legislate for a profession abou- whica they know a little more than the teacher knows about theirlrespective trades in the aggregate.:Oh ! What a lack of dignity would be there were the teacher and the commit!ee man to sit side by side I! The picture of master and ser- vant hobnobbing together in friendly discourse at the same table conjures up a scene at which Dame Grundy veils her eyes and lifts her hands in pious horror. There ! No one wool!' think we Runcornians were half so jealous of our petty social dignities as we really are. Tee „trutn is that there is quite as mach starch and ruin ss and etiquette about the " broadmindsd ! middle class RS there is in the " bloated" (?) aris- ! tocracy. Caste, you see, exists in places other than ! India though it is only at special times that its existence becomes apparent. " Man, proud man, dressed in little brief authority, most ignorant of what he's most assured, like an angry ape, plays sucaffantastic tricks before high heaven as makes the angels weep."</p>
        <p>Vicar Blogg has not yet heard the last of his utifettunate remarks on Sunday sports A well- known comic paper presents its readers with a series of pictures illustrative of the games which would probably be played were the Continental Sunday actually in vogue. The first picture gives a eapial representation of a game of Lawn Tennis , played under the shadow 01 Frodsham Church. At the nets stand the Vicar and his Curate in their lawn robes, the latter gentleman evidently having the best of the set, judging by the anxious look of the priest in charge. Picture number two. suggests that the choristers should play musical chairs. A group of clioir boys in their robes are scrambling round some half dozen chairs in the chancel while the organist peeping round from his seat is evidently enjoying the fun. Tne third car- toon pictures two of the sidesmen in the vestry armed with the regulation offertory p!ate intently engaged on a table with the interesting game of .‘ shove Hapenny." The artist in the fourth sketch has not forgotten the humble bellringer and pew opener. In the bottom storey of the belfrey stately old dames and dignified old gentlemen are wit- nessed wheeling round in the per,4nnial game of Kiss in Ring." Even the Parish Clerk is not for- gotten. The fifth sketch represents him seated at hi 4 cle,lc indulging in the highly criminal card game of "patience." The climax to the series is found in thejlast picture where the sextons are indulging in a game of hide and seek with the grave stones as hid- ing places. Evidently they are enjoying the fun and think the game a splendid one to while away a tedious Sunday afternoon. Of Course it was never the vicar's intention that his words Ehould be taken ; EC) literally and one must feel sorry for the pain which the ungodly have caused him. Still it for- cibly idostrates to what lengths Sunday sports I might be carried were they to become popular in England. And the artist has given the vicar a fine moral beneath his thinly-veiled sarcasm.</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL. a nt, the Bbreosnt gtho tr form, On Saturday as well as Monday Runcorn played two sterling games against Swinton Rangers. On Monday theyshowed and the combination was really wonderful. Every- one worked so well that it would be invidious W particularise. Hougton has got back t..) his old form and played a sterling game. Flannagan played wooderfogy well and has a bright promise h9fore him. The Rangers were completely out classed on Monday, in every department and the score was a fitting conclusion to the game. MOTOR BICYCLES.—The "Bayliss Thomas Eyed- slor "Sole Agent.,l GARLAND Bridge-at, Warrington. N ASIn MTH LIN% TONIC t 5 THR KING} Or TONICS.-63, 86li1tEY-STERET .Warrington.—(Advt</p>
      </div>
