      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>LANCASHIRE CENSUS STATISTICS.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE CENSUS STATISTICS.</p>
        <p>On Saturday official st—ati—stici of the census of 1901 for the County of Lancaster, were issued. The following are a few extracts : Tte ancient or geographical county of Lancaster, which is a county palatine, contain., according to revised returns furnished by Bis NI jesty's Ordnance Survey Department, an area of 1,203,385 statute acres Ice p, pulation numrera 4,406,409 persons. In 1801 the popuhtion was 673.466. It has, therefore, increased during the century neartv sevenfold. The highest inter- censal rate of increase (27.1 per cent.) occurred in th decennial period terminating in 1821. A marked rise took place in the period 1871-81, hot with this exception the rate has declined ever since 1821. Tim lowest rate of increase (12 2 ror cent) ozcurred in the intercensal period just cancluded. The administrative county, together with 15 county boroughs. c intains an ores of 1,196,753 atatote and a population of 4,387,043 againsr. 3,907,370 in 1891, an increase of 12.3 per cent. The num t-er of inhabited houses is 882,753, against 760,495 in 1891, an increase of 161 per cent. 01. uninhabilea houses the number is 66,878, against 53.711 in 1891, an increase of 13.9 per nt., and of houses building 7,535 against 5.1E4 in 1891, an increase of 46-3 per cent. Of the 66,878 unin- bah'. o:: houses. 32,939 are "in occupation," that is utiliz'd for business or other purposes, but without in- mates on census nights. The females enumerated in the administrative county and county borout,. be exceeel he males by 171 833 ;there are 1,082 females to every 1,000 males. The number of chillvia of the school ag,e (thret: and under 14 year.) is 1.019,043, of whom 505,431 are boys and 513,612 girls. ' The number of children aged five and tinder 14 years is 823,914, of whom 408,462 aro boys and 415,452 girlq. 01 the males. 1.285,381 are unmarried, 745,883 are married, an t 75,841 are w,dowed. Of the females, 1,341 861 ac tv.m,rried, 763,136 are married, and ' 174,441 are widowed. Pauper inmates of workhouse est•blishmenz a in the.reghtration county numbered 30,084 u-ra,ms of all•agee, of whom 16,930 were males and 13,154 females. Prim:arra in local and conariet prisons in the vaistra,tion nounro numbered 2,623 a7,ainst 2,353 in 1891. Of these 1,740 were males and 833 females. Tin r,:norni of popdation in this neigh- bourhood, with comparisons, are as tollows :- 1901. 1891. Warrington Wigan Widnes Garston Sr. f3elens Liverpool Manchester 543.872 ... 506,363 Salford 220,957 ... 198.139</p>
        <p>../6•••,.. 6 Ot the 23,603 foreigners in the county, 17,514 are mates sod 11,089 femalbs Nitives of Russia hays in- creaccl from 4.914 to 10,506 since the census of 1891, whila persons of German birth have decreased within the same period from 5,015 t 4.314. Natives of France have decreased from 1,112 to 305 ; natives of the Unitt d Statas cf America from 3.367 to 2,300, aod of other parts of the Amsrictri Cmitinent, :Ist being British pos- sessions, frcm 1,252 to 244 ; white native. of Austria have increased from 736 to 1,483, of Italy from 891 to 1,456, anti of the Dinubian cipaiities from 356 tn 1,003. Liverpool contains 3,124 Russians and 1,453 Germans, and Manchester 6,363 Russians, 1,319 Ger- mans, and 965 Austrians. The number of persons returned as " blind " iS 3,363 and of these 91 also suffer from rime otter infirmitv• The former figure compares with 2 819 in 1891. There has therefore been an increase of 544 in the number of rerions returned as " blind." This is an dric-eve from 0.07 p r cent, of the population in 1891 to 0 08 per cent of that in 1901. Similarly, deaf and dumb pereons, including 137 who are said C. be (lamb only, number 2,084, se against 1.830 in 1831. These figures show an increase of 234,the pet cent ag,,e to popudi• ion in 1891 an i in 1901 remaining stationary. Of these 2,064 nersons,33 who are returned as deaf and dumb, and 29 as dumb only, suffer also from some other infirmity. Lunatics now numb, r 9,741, including 31 afflicted with some ()idler infirmity; and the imbecile and feeble- minded 6,337, including 69 otherwise Filleted. The total of.tnes• classes is 18,078, against 11,822 in 1891.</p>
