      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>MR.' A. H, CROSFIELD AND THE VALUE OF VOLUNI EER TRAINING.</head>
        <p>MR.' A. H, CROSFIELD AND THE VALUE OF VOLUNI EER TRAINING.</p>
        <p>Writing to Sir Gilbert Greener!, from the Hotel Beau- Site, Cana.es, on April 8:n, Mr Crosti.-Id explains that his aosence from the Licensed Victuallers Banquet, is rendered unavoidable by is severely sprained ankle. In the course of lois letter ha discusses national affairs and remarks as blows :—But cifferesces of opinion on the eause of this War SOO% pre vont US seeiiig eye to eye as tegarda the responsibilitioa which we have incurred by it, and its extension ot our Empire, an 1 the necessity for recognizing aud facing tuese respousibilitiea May I close thee° preliminary remarks with the assumption that in prepariog t ) face these responsibilities, the great majority preferred voluntary to compulsory miiitai y service, as being better suited to English character, and calculated to give more dash and a better fighting force in the field. It follows then, does it not, that it is the bounders duty of those who cake this view to help to make our voludcary syetem as effective as possible? And here comes the point which I venture to bring under your netice. Since the new regulations for atten- dance of Volunteers in camp we.e passed there has been a disposition on he part of the employers in some parts of the couetry to cold shouldior tnose of their workmen who as Volunteers attend camp. Surely this is not only very unfair bua very shortsigeted. As a military friend of mine pithily put it to me he other day, it empleyers take this course they may find themselves forced to shoulder a mtnket in tee ranks theinieivas, and sooner than thay think. I have alluded to this once or twin,: in 'Warrington, an: just before leaving England I brought it upb.fore the trade association of employers, with which I am conuemed, and they had promised to give it a favourable ooneideration between then and our next meeting, and they discussed questions such as the ages between which men should be tree to at, end • the week (or. as in the case of the 35 volunteer baltalions,of which Warrington is one, the fortnight) in camp and the payment of wages during ruck military service. I won't trespass on your time by going into such questions In derail. I only venture to to submit the matter broadly for the consideration and I trust, approval, in principle, of yourself, of our mem- ber, whom I had hoped to meet on Thurs lay—and of other large employers of lah or who I know will be preserr• Their powerful influence would count for a great deal. In British industries whore foreign compstition is of small account perinissioe to workmen to attend camp meant increased coat of manufacture, and that in- creased cost would in the long run fall upon the con- Bonier. So that, even on selfish grounds, one fails to see what vaild obj-ction can be raised. In other hiduAries wh,•re loreign competition does largely ewer into the question, Capitalists may object to handic.p themselves by an addition to the wages bill.To that the I answer is,cf courseethat the gain in beartb,physique and discipline through attendance in oamp is Fa% a gain to the map alone but through their ncreased ,fficiency as workmen, to employers as well, and if there is a certain increaan in cost of manufacture that i 3 after all hut a tax air the safety of the Kingdom, and a much liehter tax than we should hive to pay if the regular army had to be doubled. I have ventured to write yon at this leughth because the question is so important and becoming se urgent, and because I believe yen will acres with me that all possible support and encouragement should be given to our Volunteers, and that in no other way can we give more practical proof of loyalty and patriotism—genuine patriotiam— in the Coronation year. One would like to aas Warring- ton well to the fore in bringing this about. Please give my kind remembrances to Mr Pierpoint, and believe me, dear Sir Gilbert, with best wishes for a successful banqu-t and a pleasant evening, and with many regrets that I am prevented attending it.</p>
        <p>SALE OF WORK:AT SILVER-STREET</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>Opening by The Mayor.</p>
        <p>03 Friday afternoon a sale of work in aid of the trusts of liewney-road Church and Silver•street School was opened in Silver-street Senor)l by the Mayor (Mr J Charlton Parr). It appears that the baziar hetet in Parr Hall in April of last year, while being very successful did not raise quite sufil dent IIIOA:18 for the thorough re— novation of Bewses -road Church, and thi re is still a considerable debt on Silver-itreet School. It is hoped that by this sale c f work an additional £lOO will be raised. There was a good attendance at the opening ceremony, Mr E J Guest presiding. It had been announced that the Mayoress would open the sale Lott unfortunately she was unable to be present. In her aosence the duty was performed by the Mayor who was , accompanied by Miss Parr. After devi tional exercises, Ttri CHAIRMAN delivered a short speech. tie said he was afraid they would not entirely accomplish the object they had in view but he trusted they would approach that amount. They would like to raise £2OO by the sale but whether they would do Boor net be did not know. The school was in dent to the extent of I something like £5OO. There was a permanent ,debt; oh £470 and a further debt of £3O bad been incurred for alterations. They hoped to clear .off the £BO at any rate.</p>
        <p>. . Tbe Mayon. wtio was cordially received, apologised for the absence of the Mayorese. Whatever, ho added, might be said of bazaars in general be thought they had all come to regard them as a social necessity for the rime being until people opened their hearts and their bands spontaneously to support the various schools and charities within their sphere of action—and these de- mands were, as they knew, growing from year to year and he thought there was eV, ry probability of their not growing Im—bazaars seemed to be the readiest and most valuable means of realizing" the needful "from time to time. Baz ears had also the ad7sin tags of interesting arrl employinc, se many people who might otherwise be unable Or unwilling to assist in any other way. In this way a bazaar vras,it he might so express it, a sort of ce--ipsrative instil tr ion in which all might j and feel that oath was contribut- ing his or her goota, by doing more or less for the good of the cause which it was intended to promote. anere was ample evidence before them of the work and the labour which had been bestowed upon the preparations for that sale, and it only remained for them to show their appreciation by purchasing and patronizing largely and heartily. and so ensuring the signal success ot the sale. In tno name of the litascress ho now begged to declare the sale open. (Applause), Alderman SHAW Gmass proposed, a vets of thanks to the Mayor and Miss Parr for their presence. He said be dii not think they saw as mnch as they might do of those friends of th-irs' who worshipped in other places, and the opportunity afforded that day of seeing the Mayor amongst them was to his mind a considerable advantage. They had reason to be proud that the Mayor had felt it his dutr and also he (the speaker) be• lieved a pleasureto come amongst them.Ele sincerely re- gretted the absence of the Mayoress who had graced 1 many such gatherings an that on previous occasions,but be was delighted to know she had a capital substitute in her daughter whom they were glad to Se following in the footsteps of her mother. They at Baur sey-mad and Silver-street did their best to triotnote the education of tie young and to do their share in tho moral, social and spiritual work ef the t.iwn, and they needed the little sympathy and help which those gatherings would give co them.</p>
        <p>Mr POVEY seconded and the motion vtaa heartily apreed.to.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR having responded, tho buEiaese of the day commenced.</p>
        <p>The stalls were heavily ladon with a variety of wares, the following ladies being the stall holders :—Refresh- ment stall : Mrs Hocken. Mrs William Parkinson and Miss Elie Bailey. Work stalls : (1) Mes.dames Win- atanley, Upson and Wsdsworth ; (2), Misses Booth. Sim Hand and Healey ; (3), Misses Ward, Yarwood and Fletcher. Fancy stall : Misses Britch, Lewis and Jones. Praise is due to the Rev C El Hocken and Mr J Upan. who had discharged the secretatial dte.ies in connection with the sale.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>I CRICKET BATS WICKETS " Slogger* 63 AM (plain) 38 Ash Ferruled 3 9 4 :DKoly:k.. leorie" 7,6 Ash Solid Tops 5/. Ash Revolving 6/9 . elan "Suggcess" 12/6 i Iron Shod 96. set extra l,iiril Ran.ll (pat.) 15,., 7,7/6 , tl,l, Lancs. NV4t2.4 G BALLS 111;i, Bounderio, 21/- Chsllengo 271. AM Soggcess 3/6. ---.....-. g -..--7 r.." Klynker 49, •-•wm. BATTING GLOVES Ns itch 56. 5 , 36,46, 5 6. 66,76, 8,8 LEG GUARDS WICKET-KEEPING 2,r6d:57676,1Nre'r psa?; 0 GLOVES Senn-Skeleton, 3,695,' :et -_, 36,LG, 5 6, 6i6, 8 6,10 6 76,89, 10 6, per par. .v 1 CRICKET SHIRTS 8881e"n• 8 81)8r 11. P3ir• ' 16, 79, 36. 4G. i .11 S, CRICKET BAGS CRICKET BOOTS Brown Leather, 6 6, 7/6, 10/6. 44, 611, 76, 10,6, 12,6. White Buck, 96 126. /5/6, 22/6, 27/6. FRANK sid:.m S 0 . (JE . i,,.14,....z• U . If` :4,,..,,- i.°t RDST ' Cifl FREE , . LIVER POOL</p>
        <p>NORTHERN LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. SALFORD.</p>
        <p>The return match between Warr ngton and Salford was played at Warrington on Monday eveaing before 3,000 spectators. The visitors had a strong team though D Smith was replaced by Brown at full back, whilst the locals were represented by the same team which accounted for Wigan There was a hearty reception in recognition of Saturday'. victory over Batley. Salford commenced a game of seventy minutes duration, and the opening items were a series of brilliant exchange kicks, the fie:ding of both sets of backs being very fine. Bventually the Salford for- wards displayed capital foot work and Hallam was called upon and the subsequent play saw Burgess and Allen open out the game and Brown's charge was endangered. .Idickenson had a shot at goal and Brown saved. Play fluc- tuated considerably after this but it was not until Hocken- hall and Harris combined effectively that Salford bad to fall back once more. The Warringtonlans looked dangerous repeatedly, but failed at the crucial moment and following a clever mearance by Rhapps, Williams and Price exchanged with the result that the ex-Wakefield man scored at the owner Warrington retaliated and a strong effort by Fish saw the home saipper pulled up a few yards from the line. Burgess next tried to force his way over, where a serviceable psi. would have been almost sure to have yielded a tangible score. Following this came another capital effort by the Salford left wing but Fish made no mistake in tackling Price The game continued to be carried on at a fast rate, and after Harris and Forster had been checked when getting dangerous, the visitors got the better of some exchaage kicking, and play once more ruled at centre, Davies made a fine run and Lomas made an open— ing t for Williams, brit the tackling was sure, and at he interval Salford led by a try te nil. Resuming, the War ingtoalans were carfy aggressive, and Morri.on making a mark Fish placed a goal from a diffieult angle. With only a point behind War- rington cent nued to force the game, and after Allen had passed to Burgess that player had a rare chance to give his side tbe lead, but p eterred to stick to the ball and another glorious opening was missed Williams changed the venue, and piny was locate lat the nayilion end From some loops work Davies semred possession and dodging through the Warrington backs, scored in brillaant style, for Lomas to add the goal. Even play was next witnessed at centre until near the close, whets Dickenson intercepted a pass and raced in n. fine try. Fish added the extra points. Final score Salford one goal two tries (eight points) ; Warrington two goals one try (seven points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>RUNCORN SWINTON.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SWINTON.</p>
        <p>Played at Runcorn on Saturday twfore a very poor gate under favourable condition'. The visitors won the tops and for some time were kept defending. (Thorley clearing when a score seemed imminent, stud a fine round of passing, little- ted by Langley, resuhed in Flmulgau losing the ball with only one man to pasa. Houghton was called upon. aud boat his man, transferring play to the visitors' quarters A fine sprint by Wallvrork enabled -vs intoo to attack finely, but no opening could be found. Williams, with a nest run, averted &amp;diger, the Runcorn loners getting into their stride and giving the visitora a warm time. Flannigan 'ost 5 good opportunity. and a moment late, Swinton had the line at their mercy. Farmer kiaked a penalty goal. Half-time— Runcorn 1 goal, Swinton nil Even play was the order on resuming, and then Runcorn pressed, but the defence of Swinton wae very fine. The visitors Find a turn at pressing without result. and near the do Riohardson dropped a flue goal for kunown who Won by 4 points to nil:</p>
        <p>RUNG:loss v. fiIIoUGEITIN The above mar oh was played on the Runcorn ground on Monday before a very poor ernwi The visitors were several men short while Runcorn played a representative team. The home contingent won the toss and at once eonteeted matters in the visitors' quarters. some very neat bouts of passing were witnesse but a, deems, was very effeotive and for some time Itueeorn could not find an opening after several near shaves Broughton were penalized Farmer kick- ing a iv:int. Aided by'lgood corn-illation the Linnets again I attacked fiercely but they were repulsed in fine style. is I grand run by Widd aeon transferring play to the Runcorn end. I The visiting backs bad hardly got into their stride when Richardson, obtaining possession kicked, Fielding's return being charged down by Walker. The ball just rolled Over the line where Walker dropped on it in the nick of time. Farmer failed at goal Resuming, give and take play followed Runcorn having slightly the best of matters. Sterl- ing work by Fielding averts I danger more than ones and nothing but the finest defensive work could have averted a I score. A. fine round ef passing in which all the home backs handled the bail resulted in Ftannagan missing the pass with only one man to evade. ieveral fine openings were made by the Runcorn b‘cks but they 'ailed to make most of their opportunities. Relief was eventually brought by Wirlideson who with ati 6 sprint placed Itunenrn on the de- feneive. The attaek was easily repulsed and tracks were R.gain made for their opponents' line. Broughton for a season now got the upper band and scrummaging on the line gave the hemesters an uncomfortable time of it Offensive operations were. however, quickly resumed, and the interval arrived with Runcorn leading by one goal one try. to nil.— Oa the restart even play followed neither side gaining any material advantage. The monotony was relieved b) a neat round initiated by Langley which was broken up in the nick of time by Fielding. Houghton was called upon and had a futile drop at goal. Runctrn still maintained the pressure and were not to be denied. After several near shaves Jolley secured and handed neatly to Richardson. A bit o: tricky passing between Warder and Butterworth then saw Richardson gather the ball and slip between the posts, Farmer converting. The vlsitors played with more spirit after this reverse but could find no opening. Walker got over but the ball was brought back and on several ocoasions a score was:averted by thelmerest ithance.:s everal fine openings were made by Richer !sun but points would not come, Ding oong play followed and then the game settled down at mid- field. Final. Runcorn, two goals two tries ; Broughton Rangers, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>IfuseLET v..OLDBAM,</head>
        <p>IfuseLET v..OLDBAM,</p>
        <p>At Hnnslet on Monday evening. Although the visitors frequintiv gained Possession at the commencement, nothing tangible resulted from their movements. Eventually, how- ever. Flurtslet worked their way to the Oldham llne,and here the hall coming out of the scrimmage to Robinson. Hannah. who received the pass. raced over at the corner. Albert Goldthorpe made a splendid attempt at goal, but the ball u t passed outside the posts. Shortly afterwards whilst</p>
