      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION CHALLENGE CUP.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION CHALLENGE CUP.</p>
        <p>berni-Finals.</p>
        <p>BATLEY v. SALFdaD.</p>
        <p>Played at Oldiptin on riaturday. The attendance was estimated at between 13,00) and 14,J0). With regard to thr game itself, the firWqaortero an hour was tame, and it was eventually left to ,hat fine payer Wattle Davles to furnish the first bit of interesting play. he dodged both Griffiths and Bone, but found when ho reached the Salford .quorter flag that Lemas and Smith ware baring his progress. He quickly turned round, threaded his way back to the centre of the giound, and then mode a splendid attempt to drob a goal. it was a capitol bit or play, and thoroughly deserved the applause which followed. tit course balford had their spells of attack, but when the inServal arrived nothing had been scored.</p>
        <p>The game had scarcely been in progress ten minutes in the second half when Salford crossed the Batley line. This was through a line effort by Lomas, who received the ball from Griffi..hs in mirth. Id and made his way quickly towards the Batley line. Fitzgerald slightly checked his career, but quick as lightning Lomas darted here and there, and linalty placed the ball over the line close to the Baths( posts. The same player easily added the 40a1 points. For some time after this Batley struggled hard to get a footing inside time Salford quarter, but all to no purpose. The Sadord forwards held their oppeuents.witn the resit t that Griffiths and Lomas COMRIO3Ot,4I EldV oral hue passing movements which it took the Batley backs ill heir time to prevent taking effect. Dal Davies at length drew Fitzger•ld out of pofition by feigning to pass the ball to Price, and then darted over the Valley line himself, but Lomas failed to kick a goal. With a lead of eight points and )nly a few minut.e left for play Salford relaxed their efforts and set themselves out to ft rostrata the individual efforts of Oaklaid. Fitzgerald and Davle3. Tbie they successfully did. and entered the final stage by one goal and 2 tries (eight points) to Baley nothing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>BROUGHTJ:CRAI74I3R3 o HUN3LHIT.</head>
        <p>BROUGHTJ:CRAI74I3R3 o HUN3LHIT.</p>
        <p>At Wakefield, before ,6,000 spectators. Both teams Dlaye3 as selected, I-tableaus standing out, of the Hunslet team. Albert Goldthorpe started. and with the reply Hunsiet ,ttacked to the Boughton half, were 07 Goldthorpe made a mark A Goldthorpe tooa the p ace-kick, the ball striking the crossbar and rebounding into play The Broughton for- w,-.rdi worked clear, and Mitchell failing to field thy had a grand opportunity, but the brothers Goldthorpe recovered smartly and pliyed back. By a strong kid!: Broughton again cleared. Several scrummages followed-in cue vicinity to the Broughton lin., and after several exchanges H •nnah scored, Alber Goldtborpe failing. Broughton al b.ek...J from the restart, Wilson scoring an easy try behind the ports, for James to fail The restart saw Ilunsle,t attacking, but penalty was cleare I. A grand passing bout by James,Barrett, and Wilsoa enabled th latter to score a beautiful try. Harry failing, liven *day followed this splendid effort, heti sides ' showing sr lendld fJotball, a run by Hannah being especially fine, Interval : Broughton, six points Iloraslot, three points</p>
        <p>Broqghtm rstartel, before 18 000 people, Smith replying to centre. Immediately afterwards Wilson intercepted and raced over, but was recalled. Smith cleared by a beauti- ful run and kick, bu, Broughton returned to the attack, for James TO miss .1 penalty Grand ierwtard play enabled Huuslet to gain the centre and from a aro s-kick by Smith Hannah gained the Bronghton qu wter. From a scrummage Albert Goldthorpe secured and dropped a goal, thus reducing the thong/non lead to one point. For several minutes the Hunslet forwards played with rare vigour and the Lancashire men were forced back to their own twenty-five. Wilson and Fielding brought off a number of daring saves. Jackson was ordered off the field. Whiteley went half-htrik in its place, and Tunniugley was brought Out to three-qoarters. A long run by Wilson put Broughton on the attack, and for a time they bet a position In the Hunslet h,lf A free kiek reduosi the pressure, and a naiskick by Fielding enabled Hunelet tet clear their line, Sam James however, kicked back, and Hunslet• were once more in their own half. Towards the finish Sans James passed out t 3 Wilson, woo sent the ball to Harry, who trans. erred to Widdefon, and the last-named ran over at the corner. The kick at goal was unsucsesaful. Result Broughton Rangers, three tries (nine points) ; Hunalet,one goal and one try (five points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>NORTHERN RUG BY LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN RUG BY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>TRAM TO MHST THH CHAMPIONS.</p>
        <p>THB YORKSIII-138 SLOUCH COHPBCITION DISPUTB•</p>
        <p>- ------- A meeting of thia body was held on Monday at the Re- change Hotel, Manchester, Mr F Linter, Br K 1 ord, presiding. Much impartant business was decided the first Item teleg the Axing of the dtte for Broughton ',lenge a (champions) versus Rest of League • It was decided that, having regard to the tact that the btangers are figuring in the final tie of the Northern Hawn Cup, that the match be played on Wed- nesday. April 30, at BroughtorLkick off 6 pm. The follow- ing tem was chosen to represent the HAM OF THIS LSSOISE.—if fey or (Hull) W Davies (13,tley) J Butterworth ttencorn) W J Hagen (BrAford), and J 'Fish (Warrington) ; j Lomas (.ialford) and D Dseies (Swinton) ; J Rh pps (Salford), J shooter (Hunslet) J Fergnson ()ldham), ixS Boardman (Warrington) J alley (Halifax). S Walker (Runcom). M Sutcliffe (Huddersfield), and A lailleW araliOrd)• Reserves : D Smith (Salford), full-back ; J W Wallwork (Swinton,. centre three-quarter. and T Davies (Leigh), wing three-qusrter ; J Jolley (Kuno )rn), hard:pick ; J Hodgson (Brighoune Rangers) and J Evans (Swinton) forwards Mr J 1-ISznitil (Widnes) svas appointed to act as referee THE DISEHJES WITH TIPi YORKSHIRE SENIOR CO 4 P</p>
        <p>MCP,I important still was the consideration of a communi- cation from the Yorkshire Senior Conaptition with reformce to the cilipme between that bo iy and the Northern League. As is well known. the %atter has bean the subject of much heartburning all through the season, and naturally the.fol- lowing communication from Mr H Hutchinson, the York- shire Senior Competition se3retary, aroused considerable Interest and discussion at the meeting, viz. " That this committee of the Yorkshire Senior Competition ask the Northern League Committee to appoint a sub-com- mittee of three to meet a 1,1.^e number from the Yorkshire Senior Competition to discuss and try to come to some amicable agreement as between the two bodies for the future." After a warm dlsmstlon, the following resolution was agreed upon " That this c ,mmittee, with a view to an early settlement of the matter In dispute. meet the full committee or the Yorkshire Senior Co opetiticm. and that body be written to at once to such effect, with the suggestion that the meeting be held at the iieorge Hotel, Huddersfield. on Friday next at 5 o'clock."</p>
