      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>FATAL TRAM ACCIDENT AT</head>
        <p>FATAL TRAM ACCIDENT AT</p>
        <p>HAYDOCK. ---- Inquest at Warrington.</p>
        <p>Mr. S Brighouse, County Coroner, conducted an in- quest in the Coroner's Court at the new Police Buildings Warrington. cn Monday evening, respecting the death of Thomas Moore, a hawker, of Haydock, who died at tbe Warrington Workhouse H-ispital on Saturday after- noon from the effent of injuries sustained by being knocked down by an electric tram car in Courch-road, Haydock, on Sunday, the 6th inst.—Mr E W Swift ap- peared for the raltives of the deceased and Mr Walter Valli, manager for the St. Helens Tramway Company, Co whom the car belongs, was also present. P C. Addison, ststionad at, Elaydock, gave eyidenco or identification and said deceased was a very drunken man. _</p>
        <p>, Frederick Holley, said he was the driver in charge of an eleCtrie car on Sunday week. At the crossing in Courch-road 11.ydo.k, the car slowed up as usual, when suddenly the deceased seemed to stagger from the side of the road against the car. He was knocked down hut tho iifeguard attached to 113 car picked bind up. Watoess brought the car to a standstill and decessod was removF'd to the Haydock Cottage Hospital. Deceased seemed td be drunk when the accident occurred. P C Addison, was recalled, and stated that ho saw the deceased after the accident and he was then very drunk. Nurse Bray said the deceased was brougot to the Warrington W-rkhouse H ,spital fredn H .yd ,ca Cottage Hospital on Tuesday, the Bth init. Hi was suffering from a severe cut on the back of the head and both his eyes were discoloured. Oa Friday erysipelas set in and deceased died on Saturday afternoon.</p>
        <p>e vercie:: of "Aceiden.:4l dea7h " was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>rftLL CLIFVE BAPTHT CIIAPEL —The RSV J S Hughes (pastor) spoke on :Sunday night oo The wonderful cv,ll of a great man." Taking as his text Hebrews ix 18.17, he dealt with what he called the most wonderful will of modern times—the testamentary legacy of the late Mr Cecil Rhodes. Re acknowledged that though Mr Rhodes was undoubtedly a roan of great eifts,strong will, deter- mined character and mighty i, fluence, he had had no fascination for him. The will he had 'left •wag to him a revelation Of the men, and presented him in a more at- ' tractive light than be hid ever looked upon him Approve or disapprove as one might no no c u d deny the great- was andteven the sublimity of the ideas whicla be set consciously before himself as the purpose of his life. His will Was a maniCisto to three great nations, as much as a, testamentary document, and the v•lue of it, from a religious standpoint, even though Mr Rhodos was not professedly a religious man, was very great, for he thereby made provision in the most practi- cal and generous manner to bring three nations into such a bond of amity and union, that it would at once boa guarantee of the peacc of the world, and the peace of all mankind 97P Abe ultimate aim of the'r Christian religion. It was remarkable that idle ambition of a great man of the world should so coincide with the grand purpose of the Gospel they preached. It wis tot brig since the Czar startled- the world with his proposi- tion for universal disarmament, and now it was a monarch of another realm, who, in the most practical manner poasible, disseminated and furthered the idea of peace and broth, rhood between nations. God used men of different types, characters, and positions in order to carry out His purposes in tho world. The newiipapers were, as they should he, delightej with Rhodes's mag- hific-nt idea of the unity of the tfiree great nations, the now triple allianee, hot it moat not be lo7gotten tint the Bible idea of moire was broader, wider, and vaster. Rhodes thought only of the Anglo-Saxon race.</p>
        <p>COMMERCUL FamnaEs.—Acoordiag tn " Kemp' NTer- eantile Gazette." the Lumber a New eantrrup:cy pro_ ceedings in England and walps gsz 4:led during th,,, week:anding, April 1201, was 85. Tno, numher in tne correFpluding week of last year was 38,.h0win4.3,, an in- crease of 49, being a net decrease in 1902,0 date,ot 42.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>A SERI3ITS CI:URGE DISMISSED.</p>
        <p>At the County Borough Police Court, on Friday, before I the Deputy-Mayer and other magistrates. William ihomas Shutt, 19, Albert-street, was summoned for having been found in;l possessiou of the carcase of a cow intended for humancoasumption which,was unlit for_the food of man—He pleaded not guilty. Mr Stanley Wilson, deputy-town clerk, mese:silted and stated that on Raster Monday morning ' Chief Inspeztoi Flood visited the slaughter house at the Lower seven stars," 13ridge-steeet, and found two men named Frederick Shaw and Wiliam Allen engaged iu dressing the carcase of a cow. On examination the Inspector found , the carcase to be in a very inflammatory condition,asidit was i givisg off a very offensive smell. ftij lungs were about three times the proper size and the whole carcase was in suati dire 'seri audition that the Inspector had no hesita- tion in seizing it. The Medical Ofncer of Hedith was seut for and he condemned the whole carcase. From inquiries which he made, Inspector Flood ascertained that the de- fendant had given instructions t ) t,,e men to dress the car- case, and there were traces of blood from the shippou, from wnich it would appear that the OJW had been kilted there and,dra.ggied into the slaughter house to be dressed.—Chief Inspector Flood and Or Hornell gave evidence, the last named gentleman stating that the carcase appeared to be that of an animal which had sulereit trom pneumonia.— di Whim Allen and Fiederick °haw, the men who dressed the carcase, were called and stated that defendant ~sve them Instructions to dress the carcase, hut he told them that if they found anything wrong with it, they must communizate with inspector Food. They had not, however, got to the lungs when the Inspector Caine upon toe so ne. when they , got to the lungs Alien said to the Inspector " There's one ' for you here." —The detendan gave evidence on o 'Lb. and stated that he called at Inspector Flood's office to inform him of the matter, hut the office was closed. Before going out, he had left instructions with shaw and Aden to let 'lnspector Flood know if they found anything wrong with the carcase.—The chairman said there wee an element, of doubt in the Code, and as the Bennis was divided the case would be dismissed. At the same time the magistrates thought it was a very proper ease to have teen brougut before them. Alderman Roberts added that he would like to naaka it perfectly clear that these cases were not reported to the Health Committee until alter they had been brought before the magistrates. 'Those magistrates who adjudicated in the eases and happened to be members of the Health Committee had no previous knowledge of the cases.</p>
        <p>A WARRINGTON POLICEMAN SAVAGELY ABS•ULTED.-</p>
        <p>ShILR r_ WENTENCE:</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court on Monday, before Mr F Monks and other magistrates, Herbert Cooper,l6. Liorman-street„and William Thomas Shelmerdine, 3, Provide/toe-place, were summoned for gaming with cards in Hardy—street, on Sunday atternoon dneimerdiue was also charged with having assaulted P.O Clough.—P.O. Clough add that he and P.O. Chrimea saw a large number of men playing cards for money in Hardy-street ()brims, caught Cooper. and wits eat Sheisserdine. The other man got. away. 6helmerdine acted like a madman and kicked wdneas severely on the left leg. causing a wound about an Inch long They struggled together and the man got away. Witness followed and ca.ight him, and he again struck him. A large hostile crowd assembled and told the prisoners not to give anything away about the other men,— P.O Chrimes gave corroborative evidence.—The Chief Con- stale said he was at the Police Aation when ihe men were brought in, They denied that they had played cards. Its (the Chief Constable) found a pack of cards upon iihelme-- dine. Clough was in a very exhausted condition when he brought his trall2 in. Perspiration poured from him and he was evidently in great pain.and in a very weak state, Ho had a wound fully an in.M mad a half lo ,g on the shin ken* and it was bleeding. This gambling went on in this and other neighbourhoods every Sunday, and when the nen were caught. and fined the money was paid out of a common fond. —the Chail man sail the time for small fines tat gone by. Defendants would each be fined 10, and costs for gaming, Shelmerdine wont,' be fined 40a and costs for the assault. Tho magistrates bed the power at iotheting a fine of £2) and costs or aix mouths' imprisonment for assaults on the police. and as far as he (Mr Monks) was concerned if these continual ass,ults 0.. the police by gaming gangs did not cease he would inflict the full penalty MORE DISORDICRLEY BEstavtonn IN WARRINGTON STREETO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>-PINES INFLICTED.</head>
        <p>-PINES INFLICTED.</p>
        <p>Several further cases of disordeny conduct in the streets occupied the attention of the rnagistratss at the Warrington Police Court on Monday. tdr F Monks was in the chair. James Costello (18), 104, Longford-street , John Holloway (19). 3, Cross-street ; James Fitzpatrick (16), 78, Longfotd- street ; Waiter Atherton (16). eo. Longford-street ; an.i Thomas Topping (lb), 21, Warburton-street, were surnmontA for isorderly conduct in Cross Street on the 6th fast —P Clough said that he and P Cooper Plumb and 01,rke flew therdefeadants kicking a small ball atout the street.Tl ec were jostling each other, and were shouting mad behaving In a very disorderly mann- r They had been cautioned about similar conduct time after time. An owner of property in the street had written to the Chief (Instable, stating that his tenants lied thrmtened to leave the neighbourbood,unlest such conduct was put a stop to.—Defendants were each fined 5e and costs.—James Ilarper,labourer,4.Chorley-street,was sum mooed for disorderly conduct In Winwiak-streat,on the night of April 7th, P.C. Dyson said the defendant we playing a mouth-organ. He stopped when witness spoke to him, byt a little f irther on re-commenced, and played as far as the Central Station.—The Chief Constable observed that this was one of the most Ingenious instruments of musical tor- ture that had been Invented of late yeartn 'rho offence was not a very serious one, but in an industrial town like War- rington people wanted to sleep at night.and they complained that such conduct in the street was disorderly.—The Chair- man 1 I suggest that defendant should get a piecolo.—The Chief Constable : I suggest that he should ge to bed in good time.—Defendant said he had a mate who was going to the front, and he went to the station to see him off.—A fine of 6d was imposed.—George Morgan and Joseph Fearne- I ti ugh, privates in the South Lancashire liegimeiat. were summoned for disorderly conduct in Bold-street and Sankey - street at two o'clock on the morning of April was stated that defendants were shouting and singing at the top of their voices, They desisted \viten spoken to by a con- stable, but in a few minutes re-commenced.—A fine of Is. was itillicted.—Thomas Gilbert Quer:at:by (16), 31, New-road, sal Albert Green lb) 93, Knutsford-road, were lined 28 6cl sects for playing football with a paper ball in York-street on April 71.14—Harry Hatton (18), 29, Froghall-lane ; Prank Thorley (16, Park- ! plane ; narnuel Meyer (16), 33, Froghail-lane and Williarn 1 Wallington (12), 41, Froghall-lane were similarly fined for playing football iu Firth-place on April Bth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>MINOR OASES. ---- Warrington Borough Court.</head>
        <p>MINOR OASES. ---- Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY—Before the Deputy-Mayor (in the nhalr), Councillor Smethurst and John itoborta, and Messrs llepherd and Thornton.</p>
        <p>ILLTREATMENT OF A DONKEY.—James Murthwaite, 329, Ashton-street, was charged with illtreating a donkey by working it whilst in an unfit state on April 2nd —P.O. caper (26), stated that be was on duty at Market Gate and law the defendant in charge of a donkey attached to a cart which he used for the purpose of driving children about. The animal was exceedingly lame on the near fore leg and was also lame on the off tore leg. He ultimately saw the donkey at the stabla and defendant said it had been lame on and off for abont two yeare.—lnspector Print, R.S P.C.A. stated that the lameness hid undoubtedly been caused by tho over exercise of running about.—Defendaut said he had takers great care of the animal. It was suffering from rheumatism and hi had been advised that exercise would do it good.— The Chairman said the magistrates did not think the defend- ant deliberately and intentionally did what in his judgment was cruelty, but at the same time he undoubtedly had been cruel to the animal. He would be flood in the Costs (Bs.) TIIESDAY—Batore Aldermen_ Shaw OreDe and Hutchinson</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>and Dr Fox.</head>
        <p>and Dr Fox.</p>
        <p>A DANORROUB SHOPLI7/1`;:117:4;;; Zobtneon a well-known character was ch aged with stealing from the stall of James Hailiwell. in the Market•place oa the 22ud ult, a pair of boots valued at 123 6d. elle ple,ded guilty, The Chief Constable said the prisoner was well-known to the police and her movements had been under the observation of the detec- tive department for some time. Superintendent Monaghan made inquiries at the shop of Items Jackson, pawnbrokers on monday, ant found that she had pledged a pair of boots which had been rubbed on the ground, but had evidently not been worn. He discovered that these boots had been taken from Kr lialliwell's stall and arreated the prisoner, who sald at first that the boots were bought by her son who had just wine back froth the front. She however, tubsequently amnitted having stolen the boots. The prisoner got her living by theft, and was one of the Prost dangerous shoplifters in the town, she was a terror in the market. She had been ten times committed for theft. Prisoner was sent to gaol tor three months' hard labour.</p>
        <p>OATUnDAY.—Before Messrs T Birchall (in the chair), Geddes and Hayward. PHXVT 73V I Boy.—Tnomas Cogan, a boy twelve years of age. was charged with stealing trom the stall of James Halliwell, In the Harket Place. at 4 o'clock the previous afternoon, a pair of boots value 10s 61.—The Chief Constable said that Mr (*eorgeAdsineors, the manager for Mr saw the boots safe at about four o'clock and at six o'clock he missed them. The boots were then iu possession of the police as was also the boy. It appeared that at live o'clo,dc the prisoner went to the pawnshop of Mr Tyrer, Winwick-street, and offered the boots in pledge saying they belonged to him However, Mr Bracegirdle, the fleal9tallt, WAS not satisilet and began to press cmestion after question on the boy who became alarmed and ran out of the shop. Info:lnrstion was given to the pollee and prisoner was apprehended by Detective Bar- low tc/whom he admitted the theft. He (the Chief Constable) was afraid the boy was growing into an habitual thief, lie was before the Courtin Bovember last and was then birched. He was about the streets a great deal in very questionable compAny.—Tbe magistrates decided to send prisoner to an indestrial school for five years.</p>
        <p>SIIOP-DOOR THEFT.—thmrles Stewart, a stranger, was charged with stealing a pair of boots of the value of 53 lid from the shop of Louis Fleming, Buttermarket.street.—The Chief Constable said Mr Fterning was on his premises at about seven o'clo3k on Friday evening and SAW the prisoner looking la a suapi:dous manter through the side window of a shop. Shortly afterwards he heard ainoise, and on looking nd rou he missed a pair of boots from just inside the shop door. He SAW the prisoner walking away and f .bowed him. He caught up to the man and finding the boots in his pn,ses- sion brought hla., beck to the shop. and ultimately gave him irate the custody of Deteltive Barlow .—Prisoner was sent to gaol for a month with hard labour. WIFE APBAULT.—WiIIiam Cosgrove, 29. Oss.street, was charged with assaulting h is wife Winifred on the 9th inst. He pleaded guilty Complainant stated that prisoner klaked her in the mouth, causing a sever injury, which she had. to have attended to at the infirmary.—The husban I denied kicking her, but admitted hitting her with his fist.. A. line of 2ds and coats, or one month's hard labour, was inflicted and a separation order, with an allowance of 1.28 a week was granted.</p>
        <p>MONDAY—Before Mr F Monks (in the chair). Alderrnsu Sutton Councillor omethur6t, and Messrs Dirchall, 1149-</p>
        <p>ward aud Thornton. A PAUPER WIT O WOULD NOT WORK —Joseph Webb, AD In- mate ot the Warrington Workhouse, was summoue‘t for refusing to petite rin a tsek set by the Master on April 12th. —kir Shearer prosecuted on behalf of the Guardians. —Mr Gate (the master) said he sent the man to the stonerard and Instrueted the porter to give him a task. absolutely refused to do the work.—Prirener : Is an inmate suppose. to chip a bolier ? —rho Chairman : He is supposed t 3 do what he is told. You will be sent to gaol for 21 clnys,- I'risoner Right.</p>
        <p>; Warrington County Petty Sessions</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Messrs G Comber (in the chair), Jolll2 White and F A Frost. RAKING UP AN OLD QUARREL SlNK6l".—Williant Taylor, farmer, oankey, was charged with assaulting Jarisei Ryan, 'Sankey, on April let. He pleaded not guilty. W H J Jenkins prosecuted and defendant was represente. be Mr 11 J Willett.—Mr Jenkins said complainant Was employed by Mr Jeans, and on the IA inst he was sent t 0 the Chapel House Inn on an errand, lie left his jacket,costs and basket at the pis.ce,and returning on his way home later. he called for them and also had a glass of beer. It appearo4 that eight years ago there had been a difference of opinion between Ryan and Taylor and the result was that blows wer!. exchanged. The case =AS brought to court, but the bens., was of the opinion thattne was as bad as the other, and dill missed the ease. This was the old grie,yance woica w tb.o cause of the present quarrel Wilde ifyian was drinking lai.„ beer Caylor name in, and said, Hallos Jimmy, canst hit me as thou didst eight years ago ?' ifyau said he tnougb. he could. whereupon, without ceremony, Taylor struck WO a very violent blew, knocked a tooth out, threw him on t 0 floor and held him by the week One or Urine companions pulled Taylor uff,and each of the rneu went laarne.-4videnoe,„ was called in support of the above staternent.—Defendal," eald that when he Came into the room liyau rushed at hill' when he mentioned tile affair Of eight years ago, and all be (delendant) did *as to hold him in self-defence to preveS6 any blow. He did not knock any, teeth out. —Thorrio , Sylvester was also called.—The bench ccnsidered that tbe case was a very trifling one, but as Tayhr used the fit words referring to the old quarrel be must ply the costs s. the summons. BkoKstN C.al..o3.—Measrs William and John Stranger , bakers, were lined 6s, !deluding costs, for not having scaisi on their van —lnspector Shepherd said the balance or the scales was broken, and that was practically the same*. having no scales.</p>
