      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions. moNDAY.—Before t:ir Richard Brooke and Mr. F. T. Norman.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions. moNDAY.—Before t:ir Richard Brooke and Mr. F. T. Norman.</p>
        <p>A LICENsING PROSECUTI—O-N—DiS—MISSED.—JAMe3 Tatlo licensee of the Holyhead Harbour, High-street. a beerhou WitS summoned at the Instance of uperintendent Brin for permitting drunkenness on his premises on the 1 March —.:Mr Fletcher, Nortlawich, appeared for the po</p>
        <p>and Mr .1 W Knowles for the defelant.—Superiatereideli Nnnlon deposed that on the date in quettion he versa golf down High-street snd when passing the Holyhead Hashoc he saw a man looking through the window and at a same time a women went to the front clei As soon as the woman saw witness she went. hick'. His sn, picions were aroused and he went into Ulu house, Re the] saw three women and arn ,n, named McKenna. The ual was drunk. Defendaat's wife said to witness He has had his ,rink hero. I have given him a soda to square hi up." McKenna was drunk and could not stand up. eventually walked away with the assistance of a sailor. Ca ledlthe attention of the:licensee to the man's 'condition St defendant denied serving him with drink. Unswort clothier, Runcorn, stated that at 2.30 p m he was at the CU tom House when he saw lalgleuna who had then had quantity of drink. He saw him at the Holyhead Hare aft,rwards at the time nuperintendent Minion was tbes The man was suffering from the effects of drink but was incapable. He would not :Ike to say he was drunk.—Jao Sherratt, manager at Unsworths.stated that the man ,41 drunk but by no means incapable of traneactiu business. ife transacted business at the shop la perfectly clear headed manner.- Mr Knowles ,itl submitted that the,e was no case and that should not be allowed to proceed further.—After conaultatio the Bench decided to hear the defence on one point." whether the mall had been served with drink. The deter,' ant stated on oath that the only drink the man had had i the house that day was one pint of beer at dinner time: had been lodging at his house and was qui' sober when he left at dinner time, but was t! worse fr drink when became In at the time seen by +3uPer; tandent Bunion. He then gave him a drink of soda _ . . . . .211</p>
        <p>which was all the driuk he'r.had at that tinae.—The case dismissed. _</p>
        <p>A PAIR OF VAGABoNDS.—John Waddington and Me, Moorefiel were charged with bogging on Sunday and v.' assaulting Sergeant Gregory and 0 Wilding and Wat' and the latter priety,er was charged with smashing a tin the property cf the Che.hire Constabulary.—The eyide showed that on Sunday the two prisoners accos Sergeant Gregory and e 0 Wilding in Halton-road 1, Raked for some coppers. They wanted to be guided lodgings. Sergeant Gregory replied be could find them V,l, suitable lodgings, and teok the prisoner. Itt custody. prisoners then became most violent in their behaviour, ! nicked both the officers. Gregory they knocked down then bit his thumb, while Wilding was kioked and stru Mr Watson, of Egerton—street, had to come to their ses ance, ar.d the defendants were with difficu.ty rer.acived tO station, using the most disgusting language..l4 The prig Waddington then beams so violent in his cell that he 44 ally pulled his clothes off an tore them into shreds, was the reason why he appeared in the dock with all thrown round him.-1 efendants were sent :as prison, bogging. 7 days; assaulting Gregory, one month; assault' Wlldrag, 14 days ; smashing the can, 7 days. AESAULTING A SENGLEANT.—Wf lilacs Savage, a Strang was charged with being (Irani' and with assaulting Sol Gregory,—The evidence showed that the prisoner assau Gregory while being taken into custody. —For the ass&amp; committed for 21 days ; for drunkenness. 7 days. A COUPLE OF DEsERTIBB. —Yobert Pritchard and WO'</p>
        <p>Holland were oliarge,l with deserting from the sth ot the King's Own Liverpool Regiment, stationed at rlngton on Sunday. It was shown that the prisoners in Mersey-strect when they were seen by `lerge .nt Grei42, who asked to 1,e3 their puses•—eks they had none thee takea into custody. Prisoners were remanded to alga' escort.</p>
