      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <p>THE NORTHERN LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Sad Death of a Warrington Footballer.— Funeral of Private Barnes. The funeral of Private Sidney Barnes, C Compsny Depot South Lancashire Regiment, who (lied at the Birkenhead Borough Hospital, as the result of injuries received whilst engaged in a football match between Orford Barracks and Earlestown Thursday teams at PrentOn Park, Birkenhead, on Easter Mon- day, when he collided violently with a comrade named Peter Lloyd, of the Imperial Yeomanry South Camp, Aldershot, took place on Saturday, at Droylsden Cemetery, and its semi-military character combined with the sad circum- stances under which the death took place, attracted great public attention The coffin was covered with the Union Jack, and on the lid the deceased soldier's helmet and bayonet were deposited. The coffin was borne in a glass funeral car, and in front of it walked a number of young , men, and on each side and behind it marched a number of his comrades in uniform. Wreaths were sent by the members of the Orford Barracks Foot- ball Club, from his , comrades, from Sergeant Saunders, from the members of the Liverpool Post Office Football Team, the members of the Earles- town Football Team, Mr and Mrs Hoy (Manches- ter), Mr ana Mrs Downing (Dukinfield), Mr and Mrs Moss (Dukinfteld),Mr and Mrs Moores (Openshaw), Mrs and Miss Cope (Openshaw),his companions and friends at Openshaw, from the family, and other friends.</p>
        <p>A Warrington River MysteN. An inquest was held at the Police Station on Monday evening on the body of a man unknown which was recovered. from the Mersey at Howley on Saturday afternoon—PS Smith stated that be was on the Merse* bank' at Howley in company with P C Dennett when he saw the body of the deceased floating in the water. He procured a rope end got the body out and afterwards conveyed it to the mortuary.—Detec!ive Barlow,ceroner's officer, said he had made a careful examination of the body and failed to find any marks of violence. Deceased appeared to be about 35 years of age and was a finely built man. ThA body might have been in the water about 14 days, but was in a good state of pre- servation. Descriptions had been sent to the police in surrounding districts and photographs would shortly be circulated with a view to identification. Deceased appeared to be a ship's fireman and could be identified by his clothing.—A verdict of "Found Drowned " was returned.</p>
        <p>The Rev. E. R. Tarbuck and Intemperance. Addressing the members of the Band of Hope at a public meeting held at Altrincham last Friday night the Bev Elt Tarbuck said for the last three years he had been the Government chaplain to a large workhouse in Warrington. Again he ,aw at every point in his work COMO of the most awful results of drink that it was possible to imagine. Children were being constantly taken to the Woilthouses in the country simply starved to the very bone. In many cages it was because the fath,q• had been spending the greater portion of his wages not on his wife and family, as he ought to do, but on himself and on his companions in the public-house. Lie did not suppose in the present modern times they had the right to say that it was not right for a matr to have a glass of beer, but when he spent a greater proportion of his wages in drink than he ought to do, then it was his duty to sign the pledge for the future. It was difficult to get drunkarks to keep the pledge,and the easiest and best way to go about it was to get hold of the young so that they should never begin to drink alcoholic liquor at all.</p>
