      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>PRINCIPLE OF LIFE AND DEATH.</head>
        <p>THE PRINCIPLE OF LIFE AND DEATH.</p>
        <p>THE SECRET OF HEALTH. HOW ANY MAN AND WOMAN CAN CURE THEMSELVES.</p>
        <p>All ordinary diseases arise from a deranged or diseased condition of the four great vital organs of the body, viz. :—the stomach, liver, kidneys and blood; when these are healthy disease cannot exist. This is not a theory but an acknowledged physiological fact. When these organs are sound and in a vigorous condition man is then in all his natural glory, an ideal specimen of humanity, free from ache or pain, moving about in the full enjoyment of nature; immune from contagion or microbe, a stranger to disease ef any kind. A man or woman in perfect physical {condition, that is, with a perfect stomach and liver, kidneys and blood, would repel the attacks of disease at every point ; but let that man or woman allow his or her stomach to be- come weak, and see how quickly disease will creep in at that weak spot ; indigestion, flatulence, headache, weak heart, general debility, fatigue, and a score of other ailments will follow; let him or her allow the liver to become torpid and see how quickly nature will retail- ate; sick headache, pain between the shoulders,jaundics biliousness, weakness, loss of appetite, and many other unpleasant ailments will follow ; let the kidneys become ' inactive or congested, and see how quickly you suffer from pain in the back, feverishness, mflamation, rheu- matism, Bright's disease, urinary troubles, cystitis, dropsy, albumenaria, diabetes, etc. I repeat that the principle of life, immunity from 1 disease and perfect health lies in the:natural and perfect condition of these four wonderful organs, the stomach, liver, kidneys and bleed, and that disease and debility. which are the cause of all premature deaths, follow their imperfect and diseased condition as assuredly as "night follows day," It is merely nature's law, and all the genius of the Royal College of Physicians and Surgeons will never alter it. All r, wural laws are simple,but men and women do not like anything simple, they like a pre- scription written with a golden pencil in a language they do not understand, and as long as the mixture is black and nasty they will take it eagerly and pay cheerfully, because they love " mystery." There is no mystery about VENO'S SEAWEED TONIC, for it contains five ingredients known to restore the stomach, liver, kidneys, and blood to perfect health; it cures all diseases arising therefrom, and for all round potency it has not its equal anywhere. Mr Veno is the well known and popularllecturer,whose grand illustrated medical lectures are always received with such unbounded enthusiasm. Many regard him as a physiological wonder, and the most successful healer of the present age. His name has been made famous in America and Great Britain by Veno's Seaweed Tonic. He has prayed that diseases of the stomach, liver, kid- neys and blood are radically cured by this remarkable medicine. It was he who first introduced Seaweed as a medicine, and so great has been its success that Veno's Seaweed Tonic is now used in hospitals end by doctors themselves because of its extraordinary healing, curative and strengthening power. Price Is lid and 2s 91 per bottle. Sold by all chemista and medicine vendors everywhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>EAR LESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EAR LESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>POLICE CASE. For being drunk in Earlestown on Saturday John Holt of Collins Green was fined 2s 63 and costa by Mr Emmett on the same day. CO-OPERATIVE CONFERENCE AT EARLESTOWN. The Conference of the Bolton District Co-opera- tive Association was held on Saturday afternoon in the Baptist Schoolroom. Earlestown, when there was a large attendance of delegates from Societies included in the district. The principal business was the reading of Mr Henry Avevard's paper on "Co-operative loyalty," by Mr J G Mullins of Earlestown. A discussion ensued upon the argu- ments set forth in the paper, in which several dele- gates took part. At the close tea was provided by the Earlestown Society for the delegates. EARLESTOWN P.S A.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting completing the winter session of the above Society was held in the Co-verative Hall on Sunday together with the distribution of prizes. There was a very fair audience and Mr Smith was in the chair. A solo was rendered by Miss Stronach entitled " Fcr ever with the Lord" After a short address by the Chairman, Mr William- son on behalf of the committee distributed upward of 200 prizes. It was decided to carry on the P.S.A for another session. Votes of thanks were accorded</p>
        <p>to all the artistes who bad given their services dur- ing the session and also to Miss Williamson for presiding at the piano, and to Mr Williamson who • has acted as secretary. BRUNSWICE-ROAD MISSION, EARLESTOwN. In connection with the Sunday School attached to the above Mission, special anniversary services were held in the mission room on Saturday and Sunday last. Mr E J George should have presided at the Saturday night meeting, but he was unable to be present, and his place was taken by the Rev E C Bowcock, who gave a very inspiriting address. The scholars acquitted themselves remarkably well in their recitations of scriptural records of the mira cies of Jesus which they interspersed with singing. This was continued on Sunday afternoon, when Mr Joseph Webster presided. In the evening a capital sermon was preached by Mr JII Kinnings. The choir and children did excellently well in their singing, which was much appreciated by those pre- sent. Mr A Dring conducted and Mr S Kenyon pre- sided at the organ. The collections realised £4 Its. PRIMITIvE METHODIST CHURCH, EARLESTOWN. Under the auspices of the young ladies connected with the above chureb, a tea party and concert was held in the schoolroom on Thursday evening, the proceeds being in aid of the new chapel fund. There were about 320 persons to tea,land there had conse- quently to be two sittings,the tables being attended to by members of the congregation Atter tea a concert was given, when tee following programme was efficiently submitted : chorus, " We are the fairies," choir ;mandoline duet, Messrs Toombs and Stronach ; song, " Odd plaid shawl," Miss Woods, part song "Mistress Mary," recitatioo," Women and Work," Miss Smith ; trio, " A bird in hand," Misses Stronach, Woods and Baxter; dialogue " Is he married ? "nine characters ; chorus " The Gipsy chorus," choir; song, " The fortune teller," Miss Stronach ; trio, " The quakeresses," Misses Baxter, Toombs and Stronach, trio, " 0 Memory," Messrs Stronach and Woods,song and flag drill,"The death- less Army " (solo) Miss Baxter ; dialogue, " The rebearsai, ' nine characters ; chorus, " The fairest day," choir.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>GOOL TRMPLAILY.</head>
        <p>GOOL TRMPLAILY.</p>
        <p>The Hope of Earlestown Lodge meeting was held in the Co-eperative Hall, Wargrave-road, on Monday evening, Bro Cutler, CT, presiding. Bro Woods, L D. read the circular convening a special meeting of District Lodge to revive the report ot Grand Lodge representative ; also invitation to send a deputation to Great Heart Lodge, St. Helens, when Bro M Jones,District Secretary,would address the meeting. An invitation was received from " Hope of Sutton" Lodge which Bros Clarke and Newman were appointed to arrange for. Bros Woods, Clarke, Hatton, Williams and Wakefield were appointed to arrange a series of open air meetings. —A resolution in favour of Sunday Closing was adopted and ordered to be sent, to Cal. Pilkington, M.P.. Mr Balfour, M.P., and Sir II CanapbPll- Slannerman. The programme was arranged by Bro Williams :—Recitation, " Bundle of Sticks," Bro Williamson ; song," Work and Win," Bro Single- ton ; reading, Bro Newman; Welsh solo, Bro Davies; solo, " OttrY an armour bearer." Bro Hatton ; reading, "Story of a Handkerchief," Bro Woods —The Lodge was:closed in the usual manner by Bro Cutler, C T, BAZAAR AT SARLESTOWN. 1</p>
        <p>' A three days' bazaar was opened in the Mission Hall, Earlestown on Thursday afternoon, in ccn- nection with the Church Army mission for the parish of •••t John's, Earlestown, the object being to raise a sum for the Mission Nurse's salary, and to clear off the debt incurred in the completion of the Mission kitchen. The bazaar was christened the " Daffodil" bazaar and the decora- tions partook of colours in imitation of the popular spring flowers. Altogether there were 13 stalls which were very tastefully arranged in the room, and the actomnanying decorations were carried out by Mr Arthur Clarke. Nurge Irwin took a prom- inent part in the bazaar, and she was very ably assisted by many of thwe attending the Mission, and several of the congregation of St John's Church the result b,•ing well laden stalls There were the Alpine-street, Wonien's Bible Class, Fancy, Mothers',Mission Choir, Sewing Class,sweet, Ilower, refreshment. gentlemen's, teachers, toy,and Ice Cream stalls. In addition there were numer- ous side shows, such as a shooting range, Old Eng- lish Pillow Lace Making E'xhibition, Electric Battery, Mission Picture Gallery, and various entertainments. Hat trimming competitions, needle threading race, for gentlemen only, and a nail driving contest for ladies afforded, much amusement. The opening ceremony on Thursday was per!onned by Mrs Valentine, who expatiated briefly on the objects of the bazaar, and of the benefit to be derived from the Missions. There was a fair number present, and after the bazaar was formally opened, business proceeded briskly. It is expected a substantial amount will be realised.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>EDUCATION BILL.</head>
        <p>THE EDUCATION BILL.</p>
        <p>Twelve Reasons for Mending or Ending the Bill.</p>
        <p>I.—Because it is not so mush an Education Bill as another Voluntary Schoela Relief Bill. 2.—Because, whilst professing to make provision far Secondary Education, the Bill only gives the new educa- tional authority permissive povi era, and casts no obliga- tion or duty of any sort upon it to provide Secondary Education, admittedly the kind of education for which it is imperative that further provision should at once be made. • 3.—Because, so far from promoting Secondary Educa- tion, the Bill will black the way towards progress in it, since it will make a heavy additional rate compulsory for the maintenance of denominational schools, and the new educational authority will hesitate to impose a double burden upon the ratepayers. 4.—Because the Bill, so far from creating " One authority " will produce a multiplication of authorities with powers and duties so complex and conflicting that administrative chaos is the first and almost certain result. s.—Because the Bill allows Education to be banded over to so-callcd Education " Committees " not a single member of which need be directly elected by or respons- ible to the ratepayers, or the people. 6.—Because the Bill permits and encourages the immediate destruction in all parts of the country of the School Beards, which have done such splendid service in the Cause of Education duringthe last third of a century. 7. —Because the Bill permits the entire cost of the maintenance of the voluntary schools (except that of the up-keep of the schoolhouse) to be taken from the taxes and rates, without giving the ratepayers any effective right of control or management. B.—Because the Bill will leave the great majority of denominational schools precisely as they are now, ex- cept that the cost of maintaining them will be thrown upon the rates, which will be enormously increased to save the pockets of the voluntary school subscribers. 9.—Because the Bill, if passed, so far from getting rid of denominational strife, will lead to increased sectarian bittorness. . .</p>
        <p>10.—Bectuse under the specious guise of decentraliea- !ion, the Bill gf t 3 rid of the control from Whitehall which in backward counties has hitherto proved the one element of stimulus towards educational progres3 and efficiency, with the result that the backward counties will be more backward than ever, thus work- ing grave injustice t 3 the children who happen to live in them.</p>
        <p>11.-3ecause the Bill, in ne provision as to new schools, so arranges matters that practically all new schools will he denominational, whilst it encourages the multiplication of small schools, a policy educationally unsound. _ _</p>
        <p>12 —Because the Bill recognises and permite in schools which aro to become rate•maintained the im- position of a religious test for teachers as a condition of employment in such schools.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>What the Bill does NOT do for Secondary Education.</head>
        <p>What the Bill does NOT do for Secondary Education.</p>
        <p>It is beyond question that the greatest gap in our educational system is in the supply of secondary educa- tion. Whatever may be th,3 shortcomings of our cl^- mentary schools, at all events they exist, and on the whole the teaching in them is excellent. The really urgent thing is to create and re:rganise our secondary education. .</p>
        <p>But what does the Education Bill really do towards that end? It creates an education committee which may do certain things but which need do nothing. The royal commission on secondary education recommended in 1396 that one of the duties of any new authority should be " to secure a due provision of secondary edu- cation." There is no such obligation laid down in the present Bill. The committee need not oven spend the "whisky money" on higher education if they do not want to, and, indeed, it will not be surprising if there are places in which this money will be hailed as a means of helping to pay the increased rate which will be necessary when the voluntary schools have also to be supported out of the rates. Nor is there any state help comparable to the equiva- lent of a halfpenny rate, which is given to the localities in the Welsh Intermediate Education Act. Yet if this stimulus to local expenditure was considersd necessary in the case of Wales, it is certainly infinitely more necessary in England, where educational zeal is un- happily much less keen than in Wales. In many, if not most, parts of the country, the Bill will not merely do nothing for secondary education but will make progress even more difficult. For when the voluntary schools are maintained out of the rates, this will mean a new rate for elementary educatirn, from which there will be no escape. But not one single penny need necessarily be spent on secondary education, and in too many cases nothing will he spent. That is to say the rrost urgent educational need is only touched in the most half-hearted and wholly in- effective fashion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Warrington Trade Unionists' Cr!ticisms.</head>
        <p>Warrington Trade Unionists' Cr!ticisms.</p>
        <p>" Endowing the Church Schoo Is."</p>
        <p>A special meeting of the Warrington Trades and Laboar Council was held at the Druids' Hall on Tuesday evening for the purpose of discussing the Government's Education Bill. Mr Plumb presided. The attendance was very meagre and the submission of any resolution on the subj.ct was therefore deferred until a more representative meeting. At the outset it was decided to discuss the Bill in detail, clause by clause. .</p>
        <p>_ Mr I.lccxr.av was the first speaker. Lie said the Bill seemed to him to be very favourable indeed to the working classes in some points, but there was one special point to which he must call attention and that was what might be termed the optional clause which gave the municipal authorities or the County Councils the option of tasting over schools or the powers of a School Board where a Board existed. lie contended that the authorities should be compelled either to take them over or leave them alone,wirhout any option what- ever.</p>
        <p>Mr FLANNERY was of the opinion that the Bill was designed tor the particular purpose of endowing the church schools. It was really a religious Education Bill and he did not think it was intended to provide a good. sound, secular education at all. In that sense he con- sidered the Bill was reactionary. At the same time he was constrained to admit that he had not the slightest otjection to a good, sound, religious instruction, but he I thought that at the present time it was not religious instruction that was given in many schools, but a Int of " Churchianity " and other things which were nothing more or less than a waste of time. He thought it wou.d be much better if the School Board system were ex- tended, and a child could then get a good elementary education, and pass on to secondary and technical education. The money should be spent under the direction of a thoroughly popularly elected body, where- as be thought that under the Government Bill managers of schools might be co-opted on the educational author- ity. The idea was to get simply as much as they could out of the rates to use for the purposes of religious in- struction, and to extinguish the Board School system. He could understand a child receiving instruction from the Bible but he disagreed with it being instructed in the interpretation which the church put on the Bible. The SECRETARY pointed out that Clause 4 which re- ferred to religious instruction dealt only with higher education. There was a clause similar to it respecting elementary schools in past education acts, and it Clause 4 were eliminated from the present Bill it would not effect religious instruction in elementary schools. Mr PmeisTeN:understood that the clause applied to all schools. Continuing, be said that Mr Balfour said he did not wish this portion of the Bill to cause religious squabbles on the authorities which had to deal,with edo- catioc. They knew that nothing caused a squabble sooner than religious matters, and to his (the e3-alter') mind to place education in the hands of an authority that was not directly sent to deal with education was far more apt to cause dissension, religious squabbles and controversies than any other act which had already been passed by this Government. He considered that the Bill was catering more than anything else for the church schools of the country. A person had a right to be a member of the Church of England or any other religious body, but when the taxation of the country was goin7 to b 9 used for educational purposes it should be used to educate tne children thoroughly, regardless of religion or anything else. At the present time they found that half the children attended volun- tary sehools, and six sevenths of the coat of educating them was borne by the taxation of the country. Be contended that the parents of the children who attended voluntary schools should pay more taxes than they did now for educational purposes. As it was they wanted the thildren to receive a certain class of religious edu- cation, but they wanted those who did not agree with that religious education to nay as much as they did towards the education of their children. That was not fair in any sense of the word. The Bill would place educational matters in the hands of a committ.-e which had to deal with tramways, street improvement and lighting, and matters of that description. At the present time some committees could not look otter that in a proper manner, and yet the Bill sought to add to it the education of tbe people. The Corporation could co-opt other members but did they (his hearers) think they would co-opt a certain number of workingmen? ("No"). He was opposed to the Bill altogether. Mr BCCRLEY said he did net think the Bill was a bad on.. It was favourable in some senses to the working classes, but the optional clause was taking away a great deal of the benefit that might be derived from it. The SECRETARY, in referring to the constitution of the proposed authority, remarked that it load been said that as the Town Council included members who had to come before the electors at set periods, the electors would have a voice in the selection of the committee. If the Town Council desired it, however, there was nothing to prevent them from electing aldermen to con- 1 stitute the Council's part of the educational authority, and the electors would then have no control over the</p>
        <p>authority. There seemed nothing to prevent some of the managers of voluntary schools being co-opted on the Committee, and also continuing to be managers of the schools. He contended tOat if there was going to be one educational authority, that authority should have control over all the schools. He disagreed with the manner in which the authority would be constituted and was of the opinion that it should be popularly elected by the people. Be would also like to see a local rate for education done away with altogether, and the education paid for by national taxation. Continuing he said there seemed to be no likelihood of another Govern- meat at the present time and he did not think it was possible to get a much better Bill from the present Government. Be was therefore in a difficulty as regarded voting against the Bill. The CHAIRMAN expressed himself in favour of the School Board system, remarking that there was no com- parison between the teaching given under that system and that given in voluntary schools. If the Bill were not passed they would very soon have to form a School Board in Warrington because the voluntary school managers could not find the money to provide the! necessary accommodation. Mr PurrsroN also agreed with national taxation for educational purposes and maintained that children should be educated thoroughly, apart from religious matters. Be did not know what they wanted religious, education in day schools for. The Sunday schools were to educate children in religious matters, and if the children did not attend them it was that own look- out. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr BUCKLEY said the Bill, in his opinion, was a step- ping-stone in the right direction. Until they coald get the working classes to send their children to Sunday schools and bring them up in a better state of life than they did to-day, religious instruction would have to be given in the day schools, otherwise a child would grow up an atheist. He, however, held that the reading of a chapter of Scripture each day was all that was required. Mr PLINSION said children should receive religious education at home,and then they would not be brought up as atheists. The CHAIRMAN said the Bill was bad and they ought to resist it with all the power they possessed. Had the attendance been larger he would have moved a resolu- tion protesting against the Bill on the ground that it withdrew education from public control and aimed at the further endowment of sectarianism.</p>
        <p>The debate was then adjmrned unlit the next monthly meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>EXAMINER. FRIDAY APRIL 18, 1902</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER. FRIDAY APRIL 18, 1902</p>
        <p>Was it Inspired?</p>
        <p>Significant Resolutions by the Church Party. There is a certain amount of significance in the similarity between the resolutions passed by the Joint Committee of the Convocations of Canterbury and York in July, 1901, and the general principle and proposals of the Government's Education Bill, which is at once apparent on comparing the two. The resolutions of the Convocations aro given , below. It will be seen that the fourth resolution is the only one not incorporated in the Bill. Resolution (1) That all schoola be financed, as far as trio cost of maintenance, exclusion of repairs to the structure, in voluntary schools is concerned, out of public funds, whether Imperial of local ; and that it is no condition</p>
        <p>of participation in these funds by voluntary schools whether any form of religious instruc- tion be, or be not taught in these schools. (2) That the funds needed for capital expenditure on the school buildings as well as for necessary extensions and structural alter- ations, be provided by the body to which the school belongs, but that the managers be not liable for any other expenditure. (3) That the government of every school and especially the appointment and dismissal of the teachers, be lett in the hands of the pre- sent committee of management with the addi- tion of certain members appointed by the local authority, such additional members not t) exceed one third of the whole number. (4) That wnenever a reasonable number of parents desire that religious instruction in I accordance with their own belief should be , given to th?ir children, opportunity for such inatructiens should be secured to them by statute, in a 1 elementary schools, provided that , this can be done without expense t 3 the managers. The principle of the first resolution leas been ac- cepted by the Government and with respect to the second and third resolutions, Clauses D and E of Section Bof the Bill speak for themselves. For the purpose of comparison we append them. SECTION 8. (d) The managers of the school shall, out of funds provided by them,keep the school house in good repair, and make such alterations and im- provements in the buildings as may be reas ably required by the local education authority. (e) Toe local education authority shall have the right of appointing such persons as they think fit to be additional managers, so that tho Lumber of the persons so appointed, if more than one,docia not exceed onc-tbird of the whole number of managers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>WESLEYAN AND GENERAL ASSURANCE SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WESLEYAN AND GENERAL ASSURANCE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of this progressive Institution was had a slam% time ago in Birmingham, where it was instituted over 60 years since, viz., 1841, and whose balance sheet we publish in our advertising columns. To realise what is being done it is necessary to glance backward and note its career. which by the ceaseless energy of its many representatives has t een one of steady yet sound advance. During the first decade a premium income of less than £1,600 was all it could' show, but by 1870 this had risen to £27,713 and since then its receipts have been more than doublpd et each i successive decade. To take an average ef the last 10 years over £32,000 per annum has been added to the income, and d till further by reducing to an average of the past five years the increase has been £45,682 per annum, while! the funds have gone up at an average of £45,661 the sum added last year reaching the sum of £71,863. All this has been accomplished by a steady reduction of working expensee. A society that can do this and clhtribute so large a sum in claims as it has done : V,z £2,859,043 must find favour in the eyes of the public and no agent need have a better can- vassing document to recommend him than the annual Ireport presented. The Society is not given to trumpet- ing its virtues, preferring rather to,rest upon its merits. In our midst we have quite ail army of worksrs any of whom can impart to enquirers full information and the local staff take some pride in having assisted in thi* magnificent enterprise. There are 75 Superintendents and Agents lowed in Warrington, Earlestown, Whines and Runcorn and they have added 11,169 new ',Mielea and paid in claims £6,500 during the year just closed. For local addresses see advertisement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>WATER POLO, -- EVERTON v. sr. HELENS.</head>
        <p>WATER POLO, -- EVERTON v. sr. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Egerton started their polo season on Wednesday evening by playing St Helens league team at Margaret- , strett. At the commencement there would be 500 spectators present. Everton defended the deep end, went away stromfly, and, after acme mid-water play, Treanor, the ea-Yeoman, secured, and scored the first goal of the season with a grand shot. Soon after Nevin scored No 2. The home team kept up the pressure, but Sharrock, robbing his man, scored witn a long shot. In the second half the Saints scored again. Some loose play followed on both Ades, but the visitors kept their heads t)etter, Walker putting his t'ain ahead. Evertnn livened op after this. Hayes slipping his man and beat- ing Manning with a crcas alio*. Time was called soon after, with the score :—Evertor.,2 ;St Helens, 3. Pre. vicus to the Above. Evi,rton third played the taints third, the home team having an easy victory, winning by 8 goals to 1.</p>
        <p>....-....</p>
        <p>Presentation to Mr Michael Chambers of</p>
        <p>Bewsey Iron Works</p>
        <p>The Old Cheshire Cheese. Litchtorl, on Saturday was the scene of a very pleasant gathering of the workmen employed at the Pearson and linowl-s Bewsey puddling and scrap forges and departments when a purse of gold sub..cribed by the men was presented to Mr Chambers, their late foreman, as a slight token of their respect and esteem. An excel- lent knife and fork tea was provided by the land- lord. Mr Griffiths, at which 100 of the workmen sat down' After tea was over the business of the meriting commenced. Mr •ihomas Gregson was voted to the chair. The chairman in opening the proceedings said they were gathered together that , evening to show the respect and esteem in which I they all held Mr Chambers, He had been amongst them a great number of years, and they had always worked amicably one with the other and had al- ways found him whenever they were in any diffi- culty willing to assist them to the best, of his abilities and he had great pleasure in making this presentation, which he hoped Mr Chambers would accept. Mr Chambers suitably responded, and thanked them all for their kindness to him which he would remember as long as he lived. After the presentation and the usual toasts, the remainder of the evening was spent in a very pleasant manner. Sings were rendered by Messrs J Flynn. James Lea, J Jones, and Mr Chambers all of which were loudly encored.</p>
        <p>THE GLEN SPEY DISTILLERY, situate in the finest hiSky-producing District of Scotland, is tho property of W and A allbey. This Whisky Is made entirely from Home-grown Barley and is kept absolutely unblended in His Majesty's Bonded Warehouses ts mature,and sold by Iv and A Gilbey's agents in every town _at 34 6d per bottle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>'By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>The last League match of the season at Wilderspool on Monday was one of the most exhilarating and en- joyable of the lot. Salford brought practically a full team notwithstanding their severe struggle with Batley on Saturday, and they played fine football from start to finish. With the home team likewise in good form, the standard of play was high and even those who were chagrined at the result enjoyed the game. Warrington were generally expected to win, and they were a trifle unfortunate in finishing up just one point in the rear of the Salfordians, who aggregated eight points to War- rington's seven.</p>
        <p>With the kick-off at 6 p.m., there was a good crowd, though less than would have been the case on Saturday afternoon. Warrington turned out as selected, while S alford substituted Brown for Dan Smith at full back. Warrington went off with a rattle at the commence- ment, and Dickenson had a drop at goal which was not far from the mark. Then play became more even, and the combination on both sides was excellent, Salford being slightly the better. There was some exhilarating football and Price opened the score by racing in at the corner from an opening made by J and T Williams. The reverse roused Warrington to greater deeds, and a hot struggle followed on the Salford line. Burgess got hold and foolishly dropped at goal,instead of passing to Fish who had a good chance of getting in. The ball only just sailed outside the posts, but the effort resulted in the Reds being able to clear. Before the interval Fish got possession and made a remarkable sinuous run down touch which was only checked on the, verge of the goal-line.Salford lead at half-time by 1 try to nil.</p>
        <p>Immediately on resuming Warrington dashed down to the visitors' quarters and Morrison marked in fair position, Fish placing a neat goal. It seemed odds on the homesters winning now, especially as Salford were penned on their line. One or two men got laid out in the struggle, and eventually Barg.,ess got away and beat his man only to run into Brown on the line when he should have passed. Salford next' go the ball and a rousing punt by Lomas sent the ball to centre. Another ding.dong battle was waged here, and the Reds made progress with a fine passing bout. Davies ran across and then swerved beautifully with the result that he rounded the home posts, and Lomas added a goal. This was a damper :for Warrington but they still worked hard, and were rewarded with a sensational score. Dickenson intercepted' a pass from Griffiths at half way/ and made for the lne at a .gallop. Brown waited but was completely beaten by the W'a rrington centre who grounded under the goal, and Fish safely piloted the ball over the bar. With only a point between their respective scores each side strove hard. Salford, by dint of superior scrimmage work, controlled the ball oftener and at the call of time were still defending but a point to the good.</p>
        <p>As aforesaid the game was a fine one and the result just about a correct indication of the skill of the teams. Salford were just a trifle smarter all round, though Warrington gave an excellent display and had really the largest proportion of the play. They were hardly so dangerous as the Reds however, the wings not get- ting the work they desired. The visitors showed sur- prising dash and were entitled to their victory, though one has to acknowledge that Warrington were unlucky at times. Burgess, though playing vigorously and well, twice did the wrong thing at critical moments when a try Irnigilt have accrued. Hallam was not at his best, while the forwards met a better class eight. Dickenson was the best of the home backs, all of whom did creditable work.</p>
        <p>On the Salford side Lomas was well watched, and though playing well was not allowed to go far. In the centre Davies and Williams both gave a stylish and effective display, the former, particularly, catching the eye frequently. Brown did well in the rear, but lacks the smartness of Smith. The Northern League table up-to-date is as follows For Against.</p>
        <p>P W D G T PTS ...G- T P PT. Broughton Hangera 26-21 4 1...42 67-285_23 22-12...33 9011 ord *Runcorn 25...15 9 2...31 41-185...16 23-101...31 .3wlnl.on 26...16 11 0-29 56-226...29 21-121-28 Halifax 26.12 10 4...26 31-142 35-165...2 Bradford 6...14 11 1...3) 47-201-23 37-157...27 *Warrington Hull Oldham Leieh 26...11 15 0...31 32-158 ..24 33-152...22 *Hunslet Bat'ev Huddersfield Brighouse Rangers 26... 3 21 1...22 10— 74...51 74-324 .. 7 *Swinton, and Hunslet have had four points, and War- rington, Runcorn, Bradford and Salford, two points de- ducted for br,aches of professional rules.</p>
        <p>Warrington have, as usual, finished up with a point per match, but including the two points deducted, they have scored more points in the Northern League than they ever did in any other competition The record is not a bad one but should have been better. Oldham lost their concluding came at llunslet on Monday, while Runcorn disposed of the all-conquering Rangers very comfortably. Just lately Runcorn have not given much away in the poine line.</p>
        <p>The semi-finals resulted very satisfactorily from a Lancashire point of view, Salford and Broughton coming ut on top at the expense of Batley and Hunslet The " gallant youths ' were beaten pointless,but they would probably have won on Yorkshire soil. Warring- ton would have had a much better chance last season if the final had been in Lancashire. The final this time will be at Rochdale, and there should be a fine struggle between the Reds and the Rangers with slight odds on the latter.</p>
        <p>The South-West Lancashire League table up to-date is as follows :</p>
        <p>Points P. W. L. D. For Agst. Pts Widnes Wigan.. Runcorn 9 4 5 ..., 0 ... 43 ... 51 ... 3 Leign Bt. Helens LIO . 2 8 . 0 ... 40 ••• 83 4</p>
        <p>Widnes play Leigh to-day at Widnes in the West Lancashire Competition, and should they win they tie with Warrington in the final for the Cup. At a meeting of the Competition Committee at Warrington on Wed- nesday evening it was decided that the encounter between Warrington and Widnes should then be played at Runcorn on Tuesdey evening, the 29th inst.</p>
        <p>Cricket. On Saturday last; that promising young bowler E Gibson playing for a Lancashire Eleven against 16 of Derbyshire at New Mills succeeded in taking 6 wickets for 9 runs, a very creditable performance—lt has been previously mentioned in these columns that young Gibson's ability as a bowler would quickly be recog- nised in first class cricket. On May Ist Gibson com- mences duty with the Norfolk County Cricket Club under a twelve foontlis agreement, on very lucrative terms. his efforts during the coming season will be eagerly watched by all lovers of the game in his native town.</p>
        <p>Angling. The monthly meeting of the King Edward VII. Coronation Club cf JollY An,glers was held at the head- quarters, the Ring o' Bells. Wilderspool-road, on Mon- day evening. Mr S Foulkes presided and 24 members were present Two new members were enrolled. The Assistant Secretary reported that several applications for membership had been made, but the limit number of 50 was now reached. They also repor- ted that the President (Mr Waldron), had presented to the club a very handsome prize, to be competed for, as the Committee should deem fit. The specimen fish chosen for prizes are perch, bream, dace, roach, gudgeon, and the judges were appointed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>DEAD ALIVE AGAIN. Strange Story of a Lost Son from Widnes.</head>
        <p>THE DEAD ALIVE AGAIN. Strange Story of a Lost Son from Widnes.</p>
        <p>An extraordinary story is being told at Widnes by the family of Mr Willimn Jones. living in Oaford-streer, Widnes.</p>
        <p>Their son Williarn,was supposed to have been drowned at Birkenhead a10..3ut iX welts ago. A body was, aft°r an inquest,buried without. identificatior, J-mes's family, missing their soo, got CIOSITS of this death, and identified the clothes as thosl of their eon. In due course two insurance companies paid on life policies for the young man; On Tuesday morning the missing son put in an appearance with a bundle under bii arm, having come (rum Halifax, Yorkshii e.</p>
