      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>VOLUNTEERS FOR THE FRONT THE CONTINGENT ENTERTAINED AT DINNER.</head>
        <p>THE VOLUNTEERS FOR THE FRONT THE CONTINGENT ENTERTAINED AT DINNER.</p>
        <p>Interesting Speeches on the War.</p>
        <p>Nothing was lacking to make Monday evening's function at the Town Hall an extremely enjoyable and memorable one, and no doubt the memoers of the contingent will retain pleasant recollections of the event for 801319 time to come. As was the case on the occasions ef the departure of the other three contingents, the proceedings took the form of a dinner which was served in the Council Chamber, and at the conclusion the field glasses and life insurance policies which had been subscribed for by the townspeople, were presented Co the men. The section under the command of Lieutenant Willcocks arrived at the Town Hall from the Barracks shortly before half-past seven. It was evident that their military training had had a good effect upon the men, and one could not fail to be struck with their smart yet sturdy appear- ance, as they lined UD in the entrance hall clad in the now familiar khaki costumes. In a few minutes the Volunteers and other guests proceeded to the Council Chamber where the tables had been very tastefully set out. It was a matter for regret that the Mayor was unable to be present, owin; to an engagement in London, but an excellent chairman was found in the Deputy-Mayor (Alderman Roberts) to whose lot it has fallen to similarly entertain each conting- ent; of Warrington 'Volunteers which has served in the campaign. The Chairman was accompanied at the cross-table by the Rector (Rev Canon Willis), Alderman Fairclough, Councillor Bennett, Colonel Fitzherbert (commanding officer of the Bth and 40th Regimental Districts), Colonel Ridgway (command- ing officer of the Ist V BSouth Lancashire Regiment), Captain Appleton. and the Town Clerk (Mr J Lyon Whittle). A capital dinner was served and was keenly enjoyed by all, the catering of Mr Poivis giving the utmost satisfaction. After dinner the usual toast list wa.? gone through The .oyet.I toasts having been enthusiastically honoured, The CHAIRMAN expressed his very sincere regret that their excellent Mayor had not been able to be present with them. He was quite sure that if it had been possible for him to have been present he would have done his very best to have made he occasion a memorable one to them, and as he had entire and heartfelt sympathy with them in the undertaking which they had volunteered for he (Alderman Roberts) was sure they would regret his absence quite as much as he (the speaker) did. The Mayor had forwarded the following letter : —" I vary much regret that an engagement in London prevents my having the pleasure of presid- ing and welcoming my guests on Monday next on the occasion of the farewell dinner to our gallant contingent of Prince of Wales's Volunteers on.their departure for service in South Africa. I rejoice that I have already had the opportunity of bidding them farewell and heartily wishing them God- speed on their leaving Great Britain for Greater Britain to serve their Bing in another part of his great Empire. I know they will spend a pleasant evening, and I am glad to feel that under your kind and able presidency the send-off dinner will leave nothing to be desired, and will not be forgotten by our honoured guests, but will always bring pleas- ant recollections to their hearts even though they have left our good old town 6,090 miles behind them." (Applause.) Mr Robert Pierpoint, ' had written to the Mayor as follows :—" It is with great regret that I have to say that I shall not be able to be present at the dinner on Monday of the 4th Detachment of our volunteers (active serviee). I have to be in the House of Commons on Monday. May I ask you to offer to the detachment my very best wishes and to say how heartily I join with all Warrington in appreciating the devotion of the officers and men who during this prolonged war have been willing anti anxious to endure tee hard- ships and dangers of a campaign for the honotir of their country, and the good of the Empire. ?lease give the fourth detachment my wish of God-speed." (Applause.) The Rev E J Willcocks wrote regret- ting his inability to be present in consequence of a cold and sore throat. Mr F Monks was also unable to be with them, but sent his best wishes for tee detachment. is ruE BISHOPS AND CLERGY,AND ALL MINISTERS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>OF RELIGION."</head>
        <p>OF RELIGION."</p>
        <p>ME RECTOR AND TEIE WAR'S CONTINUANCE. ' Alderman Fairelough proposed the toast of "'[he Bishops and Clergy, and all rnieistera of religion." H said the Bish mg and clergy of all denominations were held in respect and whenever be met them he never felt any the worse for haying been in their society. (Laughter.) Tway were always very pleased to see the Rector amongst them, He (the speaker) knew that Canon Willis took a great interest in the Volueteers,and in fact, in anything that went on in Warrington, Whether people were Roman Catholics, Norm oeformists, or rnembe,s of any denomination, if they went to Canon Willis, he was always ready with his sympathy. The toast was heartily drunk. In responding the RECToR said he was sure the Bishop of the diocese thomeghly deserved all they could say of aim Re (the speaker) had known him in a way tor a long time and since the Bishep had been at the head of the diocese he bad made himself so well kuown and so popular in tbe best and highest sense, that they all felt they had a pergenal aevaintance with him. He had paid many visits to Warrington and many people had heard him with the greatest pleasure. Continuing the Rector said they all knew, he supposed, that he (Canon Willie) was a man of peace, and they were he supposed, men of war. He nought, however, that there was not I one of them who did not wish this war was at an end. It was very natural that they as young men should like to go out and see military life and action, but he was sure that deep down in' their hearts they all felt as every Englishman felt, most intensAy anxious to see this lamentable war come to an end. And yet he was not sorry that the war should have Distal so long. It was now nearly two and a half years since it began, and he was not sorry for two reasons, and only two, that it bad lasted so long. The first reason was that he thought they bad learnt to come to a truer and better estimate of the Boer character. At first he did not think tho' thought very much of the Beere, and when he was talking to Some soldiers who had returned from the war, he was speaking about the courage of the Beers. The soldiers smiled and said it was very easy to be brave when they were firing from behind rocks on men who were in the open. He supposed our men did not think very kivicli of the courage of the Boers then, but be thought that now their opinion bad changed. They had seen that the Beers could be really brave, generous and humane. He was sure they could not have wished for any better treatment to be accorded any man than that shown by General Delarey to Lord Methuen. The second reason why he was not sorry that the war had lasted so long was because he thought that nothing bat a really good beating of the Beers would have given us lasting peace. If the war had been over at once he was sure the Boers would have said what they would have had reason for saying "If we had only had an opportunity of showing our strength you would net have beaten us so easily.'' Ito war had gone on for two and a half years and he thought that when it did come to an end—and they had good reason for 1 hoping that it could not last very much longer—the real beating in the best sense would be the best moans of creating a lasting peace. The Boers would respect us and we would respect them and he believed the future would be one of lasting peace and great pros- perity, not only to the British empire, but he hoped to the world. Addressing the Volanteera the Rector wishea them God-speed and expressed the hope that every one of them would come back again. He hoped they would not forget their God and their relieion while they were away. All brave men, ha thought, felt the value of their religion. He asked them to do him the kindness of accepting a little waver card when they received their field glasses and life policies from the hands of the Deputy-Mayor: (Applause.) "THE NAVY, ARMY, AND RESERVE FORCES." COLONEL FITZ HERBER PS COMPLIMENT TO THE COMPANY.</p>
        <p>THE PROPOSED SOLDIERS' CLUB.</p>
        <p>The next toast was that of " The navy, army, and reserve forces " which was proposed by Councillor Bennett. In the course of his remarks the speaker sr.id they were all extremely proud of the part which the army had played in the great struggle in South Africa. whiah they h iped was drawing to a close ; As regarded the branch of the Army which particularly concerned Warrington, they would always remember with melancholy pride how Co'onel O'Leary died fight- ing at the head of the regiment in the battle which did se much to open the way to Ladysmith and how every member of the South Lancashire Regiment did his duty throughout the arduous struggles of the war. (Applause) With respect to the Militia, he sometimes wondered if they had qui' A been given their due. Every single man in the Militia Regiment volunteered for Sarvice,and they all did their duty very bravely under the leadership of a member of a famous old Warrington family, Colonel Ireland Blackburne. (Applause.) AS to the Volunteers. like every other Warringtonian, be had only feelings of the profoundest pride for the way in which they had justified the best traditions and brightest hopes. Colonel Ridgway had said he believtid that Warringtoa was the third place in the country to offer to send Volunteers to the front in the early stages of the war when it was not seriously contemplated that their services would be</p>
        <p>required. Warrington had altogether sent over 2130 Volunteers out,including Yeomanry and possibly ambu- lance men, and he would like to know how that number , compared with the number sent from other tewns. He rather thought Warrington would even stand higher than in the record he had already referred to. they hoped sincerely that by the time the fourth con- tingent arrived at the Cape, peace would have been declared, but he believed that if the war went on for another two and a half years, Warringt3n men,would if necessary, still do their duty and come to the front as they had already done. He had absolute confidence that Warrington men would rise to the occasion and justify their own self respect and their pride as citizens of the good old town. The toast was drunk with enthusiasm. . .</p>
        <p>Colonel FITZHERBERT, in responding, said 1 it was just a year since'he bad the pleasure of being invited for the first time to enjoy the hospitality offered by the Mayor of the borough. Alderman Roberts was then in the chair, and he (the speaker) then had the pleasure of telling them bow excellently well the men of the section in barracks had conducted themselves, and how thoroughly satisfied he was with the soldier-like qualities they showed. He had only to add that the present sectiou had given him eq sal satisfaction. (Hear hear.) They well knew that he nad endeavoured to extend to them all those small privileges which a good soldier was entitled to, and ho had the pleasure of saying that not one of them had abused those privileges. (Hear, hear.) L had been a great satisfac- tion to him and he was exceedingly sorry to lose them. He felt sure they would prove crusty and faithful sol- diers, obedient to their of-liens and men in whom their honour could safely repose. On the last occasion he was there he broached a subject which was very dear to his heart. He took a very great interest in soldier 3 and whenever he could do anything to make their lot more comfortable he was always very willing to do it. Ile happened to mention that ha was very earnestly desirous of starting in the towa whet tie called a sort of soldiers' club. He made a sort of general promise that when the men returued frona South Africa they would find the club actually in existence. He was happy to say that from every line regiment con- nected with the district, from every militia regiment, and from Alderman Roberts anti Colonel Ridgway, he had met with the heartiest support. They had a com- mittee meeting a short time ago and the club Was now fairly under weigh. They inspected a house—North- field House, Beweey-road, which they nougat would be suitable for the purpose. They were met very gener- ously by the landlord. The necessary repairs were now being made, and he hoped that iu five or six weeks time the club would be opened. (Applause.) The club was dully open to; every soldier. It was for the Imperial forces of the Crown, for the linesmen, militia meal, and Volunteers, and as an experiment the club would be open to volunteo-3 in plain clothes. There were no set rules for the club. The only rule was " good n behaviour. "(Hear, hoar.) No reli- gious discussion would be allowed and the only thing they did not sell was alcoholic drink. (Hear, hear). They had gone into the finances and had been assured that their income was quite sufficient to meet the expenses. They required a little still for furnishing, and he had not the least doubt they would get it. He was confident that those kind friends who . had provided for those whom " Tommy had left be- hind him would not fail to aegis; them in attempting to do something for " Tommy " himself. (Applause). The club would provide bedrooms at very low rate of pay- ment, there would be a dining room, a game room, non- commissioned officers' room, reading-room, and a silent room. The club would not 53 merely for the immediate benefit of soldiers but also as a memorial of this long and protracted war. In the silent roma there would be panels on which would be painted the names of all from Col. O'Leary down to the humblest drummer boy, who had died from wounds or dieease in the war. fle did not think they could hive a nobl sr memorial thin this for those who had died for the country. (Applause). The memorial would not only record the names of the professional soldiers who bad given their lives for their country, but it would also csll to memory those who were not cane i upon but who voluntarily Cams forward to fill the blanks in the raulcs wheu the country had need of men who were ready not only to go out and suffer ilsootrffort but te die for King and•country. (Applause) Colonel RIDGWAY &amp;ISO responded. He Sitid he felt sure the men of the contingent would acquit themselves Governineat beef seemed to have done them good —(laughter)—and he hoped it would enable them to maintain the good name of old Warrington in South Africa. Tne Volunteer force had had many difficulties to centend with, but bit by bit they had maneged to fight their way to their present position, and he must say that the war, although most deplorable, had done the Volunteer movement a groat deal of good. It had shown the c-mntry that there were men in the Volunteers who were ready to take their stand and place in the defsnce of the country and to see that their country was not insulted by any foreign foe. Rsferring to the forms- tioa of the eoldiere' club, he spoke appr 'milady of Colonel Fitzlierbert's effcrt to better the soldiers' posi- tion and said the club would prove a great bole. Con- tinuing, he said this Was the titled occxsioe upon which. he had been at a dinner given his men by tee g,tof people of Warr neton. They thank,A the Mayor and the people of Warrington for the excellerat mariner in which they had always entertained them and tor coeir God-speed when they lit or the frout. (Applause.) He trusted they wou.d not have ()cession to send out ' another detachtn,nt and that peace might be shortly aeclared. He wished the men who were now going out a prosperous voyage and a safe return. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>"WARRINGTON'S PATRIOTIC SONS." SPEECEI BY VIE DEPUTY-MAYOR.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN thee proposed the toast of the evening —" Warrington's patriotic eons." Ile skid a good many opinions had been expressed aboiet the war, and they hal been expressed in a way wbiclo had shown, at least as to its initial cause, the very stroog difference of ; °pillion which existed at one time in too country, out he thought he should not be open to be charged with under-rating the position wnere he said that as far as , they who had reached midelo age were concerned there never was a time in the tuatara' of this groat empire , when the importance of a conflict had reaceed such a stage as this war had done. He we not old enougn to remember the position of things when the Indian Mutiey,after the terrible war in the Crimea, broke out, but anyone who had read the histcry of the country at that -time could only corns to this conclusion : that if they had lost India at that time it would have been a very serious blow to the prestige of the Empire, and no one could possibly tell how their position among the peoples of the world would nave been affected ; but even that issue, great as it was, was not t qual to the trumendous issue that the polsiMlity of losing the pre- sent war would have involved them in. eelear, hear.) Be was not going to say anything which might be con- strued into an expression of political opinion upm this question, but he taought that all men who had given any time to the consideration of the subject would come to the conclusion that we could not have afforded under any circumstances to have been ultimately defeated in the conflict. (Bear, hear.) We must not forget—and he spoke as a member of a n Jlitical party which was help- lessly divided upon the suoject—that the Boers them- selves first invaded British territory and threw down the gauntlet, and whatever righteous and proper differ- ences there might have been between US and them before that act, it alone was sufficient to justify Us in using all the resources at our command to repel that invasion and retain inviolate the King's dornieions in that part of the werld. (Applause.) Tremendous as these issues had been, equally tremendous had been the calls upon the patriotism of the whole Stripire, and the magnificent way in which tho,le calls had been responded to proved that the old spirit of bull-log determination which was characteristic of our fore- fathers even at the time of Waterloo had dcsaended upon their sons in equal measure. (Applause.) But if there WIS one thing we :night be proud of more than another it was not simply this determination not to be beaten, but that our men, from the head of the army to the humblest man in the ranks,had bhaveii as men pos- sessed of human feelings and as geetlemen. (Applause). Be did not think that anything had roused such a Mat- ing of universal indignation as the abominable and ! villainous slanders that hadlbsen poured upvn our troops in rsgard to their conduct. They knew how utterly unjust those slanders were, hut there was at sae time a disposition on the part of some of our neighbours on the continent not simply to believe, but to exaggerate them in a coarse and abominable fashion. Fortunately for us thorn were attached to our army lizmourable men belonging to the various nations of the continent who one after another had given their own unasked for testimony of the manner in which our troops bad be— haved, and he (the speaker) would say that there was I no parallel MO in the world's history where an army of such magnitude bad been sujected to such trials and temptations and had come out of the tremendous ordeal with such splendid credit as our men had done. (Ap- plause.) Elis friend, Councillor Bennett, had said a good deal as to the manner in which the various parts of the Empire had responded to the eels made upon them, and he bad to some extent expressed a hope and fore- shadowed a belief that the time would come when a sort of federation of the army forces of a f thmeaEttrnerpir(t)if would be rendered absolutely necessary a politics if we were to maintain the position we hold and improve upon it as time went on- tie theught this was last the only branch of the setv:ee of the Empire in which federation was needed. There WAS a great and wniderful development among the people themselves, but be would bo trespassing outside the object of the gathering if he venter, d fulther in test direction. Still he would say that any- thing, which would help to cemebt the immense forces of th.a great Empire,this Empire which in .delmore ti any other empire html ever done for the civilization and the ultimate peace of the world, demlnded the serious consideration of all thoughtful men. (Applause.) Their town had done its sh are in regard to its feeling of teneoetptlll: 6rt: o r h ibi i3ityin t ce o second,neczion with the war, and this was or the third, but the fourth time Warrin etrall had sent her quota of men to join in this great and important work. He was glad ts find in the present section one man at least who was going out for the snood time. (Applause). He did not know whether he was of Welsh parentage, but he was glad to find that he bore a Welsh name. (Hear, hear.) He was</p>
        <p>known by the socially homelike definition of Dicky Thomas. (Laughter and applause.) Re (the speaker)• was glad to congratulate Lieutenant Willcocks ma having in the company under his command a men who was going out a second time t do what he considered. his duty in this great matter. All the sections who had previously gone out had been officered by Warrington men with the exception of Lieut. Carey, who, although, not a Warringtonian, had all the characteristics of a Warrington man in his desire to do credit to the town. (Hear, hear.) In command of their present section they had their good friend wile was bred and born among them—Lieut. Will- cooks. (Applause.) He had no doubt that Mr Willcocks and the men who were going out with him would do the same credit to the town as those who had gone before. Their behaviour in barrac're had been testified to by Colonel Fitzherbert and was all that could be desired. The men now in South Africa were doing their work as those who went out with Captain Appleton did theirs,and he was quite sure the men of the present section would feel that they had the honour of the town to maintain and they: would not allow that honour to be tarnished. In Lieutenant; Willcocks they would have a considerate officer, who would do all he possibly could co make their lives as comfortable as circumstances permitted, and. who by his own example would encourage them to do credit to the battalion, to the town, and to the countrVe (Applause.) He wished the men a very pleasant and comfortable voyage, an escape from the dangers of the war, and a safe return. He hoped they would ceme back if possible to retake their old places as honoured citizens among those who wished to see the town pros— perous in the time still before them (Applause.) The toast was accorded musical honours and cheers were given for the Volunteers. Lieutenant WILLCOCKS thanked the Mayor and the people of Warrington for the entertainment and for the gift of field glasses and life p He was sure the field glasses would always be retained by them and highly valued not only for their usefulness and intrinsic. worth but because of the occasion upon which thex• were presented. Be was glad Col. Fitzierbert had spoken so well of the men he (the speaker) had the honour to command. Toe men bad performed their duty most keenly and they well knew that they held in their hands the honour of the battalion from which for a short time they were to be separated, sna that famous battalion to which they were about to become affiliated, the 4.oth. He was confident that the men under no cir- cumstances would bring discredit to the town or the battalion. (Auplause). PRESENTATIONS TO THE VOLUNTEERS.</p>
        <p>_ The DEPUTY-MAYOR then preiented the field glasses: and life policies to Lieutenant Willcocks and the men of the contingent, and the Rector gave a prayer card to, each one. Toe Deputy-Mayor mentioned that as Private. Thomas retained the field glasses which were presented to him when he went out before a pendant for:his watch, chain had tken purchased and ha had pleasure in pre- senting it. (Applause)..</p>
        <p>THE DEPUrY MAYOR AND THE PRICE NEGOTIATIONS.</p>
        <p>Captain APPLET)II proposed tne health of the Dvuty Mayor. He said the name of Alderman Roberts was a household name in the company he took out. He was present at all tneir functions. He was the labt man he (Captain Appleton) saw when he left and the first when he came back. In addition he never failed to take the most kindly interest in them while they were away. (Applause.) The toast was drunk with enthusiasm. The comp any sang "For he's a jolly good fellow," and gave three cheers for him and one for Mrs Roberts.</p>
        <p>The DEPUTY-MAYOn, in reply, referred to the probabil- ity of peace being shortly declared, and confessed the hope that the Boers would have the wisdom given to them to see that the struggle was in this case at any rate with the strong, as he (Aldermaa Roberts) bAieved with the right, and would not ask for terms which it would be impossible for the Government to grant. Independ- ence was out of the question, and he hoped that on our side we would .be generous, and that having frund a worthy foe we would recoguiz f that it was manly and Christian and right to treat a defeated enemy with con- sideration and with respect. (Hear, hear.) We were a wealthy and powerful nation, and could afford to make it easy for these people, if their mind and disposition was to sue for peace, to accept terms by making them as reasonable and fair as possible. (Rear, hear ) He again expressed regret that the Mayor was not present, for he knew the deep imerest he took in the battalion and in everything eise which concerned Warrington in this important matter. (Applause.) It was upon Mr a Ballard and Bugler Inca that the task of entertaining the company in the intervals be- tween the epeeches devolved, and it can truthfully be said that their efforts in that direction were attended with complete success. We have never heard Mr Ballard to better advantage, and his humorous contributions almost convulsed Ms audience. The recitation of "How Bill Adams won the battle of Waterloo," was of particu- -1 Ikr inter( st to the volunteers, and the songs" Seranad- ing under difficulties," " The elopement " and " Gfine under the old umbrella ' were given in Mr Hallard's best style. Mr Percy Randall anly played the accom- paniments to the songs. Bugler lace, who went out with the first contingent, gave two solos on the concertina wiell considerable ability, his rendition of " The lost chord'" being very praiseworthy. A most eijoyable gathering was brought to a close with the hearty singing of "Auld Lang Sync "and "God save ,the King."</p>
        <p>MR. SAM WOODS AND NEWTON DIVISION. Mr Sam Woods. the Labour candidate for the Newton Parliamentary Division. addressed a meet- ing of miners at the liaydock Colliery Schools on Thursday night. Dr Dowling presided. Mr Woods who was warmly greeted, said that wlreu he was invited to fight the Newton Division he was told on all hands that if he dropped the element of Liberalism which had been attached to his previous cauditlatures, and if he came out purely as a Labour candidate, he would have the support of the Conservative and Liberal working men in the divi6ion. He believed in measures, not party, and in measures which affected the interests ot the working men of the country,and when he was inithe House of Commons he voted against anything which adversely affected the intereqs of the working men. Mr Woods then proceeded with an address cn what he called the foundation question of social and political matters —viz, the state of the land laws.—A resolution of confidence in Mr Woods and a pledge to support his candidature was heartily passed.</p>
